\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 I Won't Enforce  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Laws\b0\par\par\uc2 By Steve Descano \par \uc2 1,289 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 June 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 22\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Almost two and a half years ago, I took my oath of office as prosecutor and swore to protect my community from those who broke the law. The real threat, I now realize, may stem from those who write the law.\par \par \uc2 If the Supreme Court overturns Roe v. Wade, the rights of thousands of Virginia women will be thrown into question. While the commonwealth does not have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban on the books, our governor has said that he is ''staunchly, unabashedly'' against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fully committed to ''going on the offense'' against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in our legislature. Should Roe fall, he could well strip women of their \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- and go after thousands more who flock to the state whenever neighboring jurisdictions clamp down on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. What's more, in Virginia today women who are suspected of terminating a pregnancy without the assistance of a certified medical professional can face felony charges if they miscarry.\par \par \uc2 So when the court's draft decision overturning Roe v. Wade was leaked earlier this month, I committed to never prosecute a woman for making her own health care decisions. That means that no matter what the law in Virginia says, I will not prosecute a woman for having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or for being suspected of inducing one.\par \par \uc2 I didn't make this decision lightly. But I knew my constituents were tired of the empty rhetoric from Democrats in Washington, desperate for action and understandably fearful for their future. One woman in her 60s told me she was irate that her daughters and granddaughters would have to fight for a right she has had for most of her adult life. A woman who recently got pregnant after a miscarriage was overcome with anxiety about how her choices may be limited if there are complications with her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Such accounts are, unfortunately, heartbreakingly numerous. But we local prosecutors are uniquely positioned to safeguard the rights of the women we represent, by virtue of the discretion the legal system affords us. Every day, we decide where to deploy our limited resources, making judgment calls about which cases are consistent with the values and priorities of the communities we represent, which plea deals to pursue and which crimes merit our attention. When it comes to charging individuals, we are the arbiters of the law.\par \par \uc2 For hundreds of thousands of American women, access to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon may depend not only on which state they live in but also on how hard-line their local prosecutor is. That's why I hope prosecutors across the country will join me in choosing to lead on behalf of the women we represent.\par \par \uc2 I'm taking this step, in part, because of public safety concerns: Before Roe, hundreds of women lost their lives annually because of unsafe attempts to terminate pregnancies. Marginalized populations were most at risk. One 1960s study of low-income women in New York City found that 98 percent of respondents who had terminated a pregnancy indicated that no physician was involved in the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to the proliferation of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , women today have more, safer options than they did in the 1960s. Still, there's no doubt that many women, especially those who are the most vulnerable, are likely to end up in situations that are medically or legally unsafe. That is the reality that Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and those who joined his dangerous draft opinion are tacitly endorsing.\par \par \uc2 I also have privacy concerns about anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws. Our Constitution protects individuals from government intrusion into their personal lives. If Roe falls, many states will have to rely on an Orwellian regime to enforce blanket bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Imagine, for instance, the trauma a woman would have to endure after a miscarriage if a prosecutor with a political ax to grind decided to pursue a criminal case against her. The grief of losing a pregnancy could be followed by a law enforcement interrogation about drug use. The police may root through her trash bins under the cover of darkness for empty bottles of liquor or beer. Her most private thoughts may be pored over by police officers wielding search warrants that grant them access to her text messages and emails with intimates.\par \par \uc2 Law enforcement may coerce the medical professionals who rendered treatment on one of her darkest days into revealing information she provided in confidence about her medical history. And her past partners may be hauled into police precincts to discuss her sex life. After this intrusive investigation, a prosecutor could stand in front of a grand jury and present deeply personal findings before encouraging those impaneled to criminalize a woman still racked with grief.\par \par \uc2 This scenario may sound alarmist, but it is already happening in conservative states that have severely constrained \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. In Texas, a prosecutor in Starr County indicted a woman for murder after she lost a pregnancy. The hospital staff reported her to law enforcement, claiming to have had reason to believe that she had induced an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In Oklahoma, a woman was sentenced to four years in prison for manslaughter after losing her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 These stories are not anomalies. According to the National Advocates for Pregnant Women, from 1973 to 2020, more than 1,700 women were arrested or prosecuted for charges related to the loss of a pregnancy in the United States. With the toppling of Roe, local prosecutors would be the last line of defense standing between American women and an expansion of this enforcement dragnet.\par \par \uc2 By making clear to law enforcement that we won't prosecute women for making health care decisions, we can disincentivize these intrusions into the personal lives of the people we serve.\par \par \uc2 I recognize that this may come at a personal cost. Since making this pledge, a death threat was phoned into my office, forcing me to worry about the effect my actions may have on my family -- at a time when I am terrified that my daughter is growing up in a country that limits her choices.\par \par \uc2 But I know that this exercise in prosecutorial discretion is one of the few ways to reform the justice system. I've seen it happen: Shortly after I took office, I elected to stop charging adults with simple possession of marijuana. I could not justify wasting my team's limited resources on saddling disproportionately Black and brown marijuana users with a criminal record when we had serious crimes to prosecute. While many stakeholders in the system wagged their fingers after I made this announcement, Virginia's legislature legalized simple possession of marijuana statewide less than two years later.\par \par \uc2 That story should offer some hope to those fighting for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Currently, 13 states have trigger laws that will severely restrict or ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  the day Roe falls. Since the Supreme Court's draft opinion leaked to the press, Republican officials in at least eight states have also called for special legislative sessions to introduce new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. But by committing to not prosecuting women for having abortions, we might succeed in preventing the irreversible expansion of a deeply intrusive enforcement dragnet -- and, as we did with marijuana legalization, maybe even motivate legislators to take the necessary action to safeguard Americans' rights.\par \par \uc2 Steve Descano is the commonwealth's attorney for Fairfax County, Va.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY KATHLEEN FLYNN/REUTERS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220602ei620004y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
