\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Men Benefit From  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 , Too\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andr\u233\'00\'E9a Becker \par \uc2 1,335 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 23\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Matt Lavallee was in college when he learned his girlfriend was pregnant. ''The news scared me,'' he said, acknowledging that an unintended pregnancy was an even more daunting prospect for his girlfriend. ''But there was no question that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the best option for us.''\par \par \uc2 Now in his 40s, he shares this \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  story whenever he can, including on social media. If his girlfriend had been unable to obtain that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he believes, both of their lives would have been drastically affected. At the time, neither of them had stable jobs or housing, and finding the $400 for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a struggle.\par \par \uc2 ''It's easy for men to see this as someone else's problem, but it's not,'' said Mr. Lavallee. ''Without the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , all of our lives would be much worse. I am living proof.''\par \par \uc2 We don't often hear \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stories from cisgender men like Mr. Lavallee, even though they are responsible for the overwhelming majority of the world's unplanned pregnancies, and so often benefit when an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  occurs. The much more familiar pattern when efforts are made to curtail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is for a slew of mostly women to share their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stories.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  science has likewise disproportionately focused on the people who have abortions. Other researchers and I have devoted our careers to examining not only who gets abortions but also what the experience is like, what barriers must be overcome and how having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or being denied one alters a person's life trajectory. This research has found that access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is associated with improved physical and mental health and is correlated to higher educational and financial attainment in the long run for both women and their children.\par \par \uc2 Yet amid all this \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  research, some critical questions remain: What's the effect on men when they co-conceive and then the pregnancy ends in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? And who even are these men?\par \par \uc2 The data on who gets an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is extensive. We know that about one in four U.S. women will have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that about 60 percent are in their 20s, about 75 percent are low income, about 62 percent are religious and around more than half are already parents.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, there's scant data on how cis men benefit from abortions, let alone demographic data that characterizes this population. This is partly because of methodology concerns: A man might not necessarily know he helped cause an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Moreover, amid continual attacks on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, the urgency among researchers has logically been to demonstrate the benefits of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access for those who can become pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Despite this gap in the literature, the data we do have on male \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  beneficiaries indicates that the benefits extend well beyond the person having the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . For instance, one study found that among men involved in a pregnancy before the age of 20, those whose partner had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were more likely to have graduated from college compared with those whose partner gave birth.\par \par \uc2 Given the strong connection between education and income level, it's plausible that male \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  beneficiaries experience increased income benefits over their lifetime as well, though there isn't yet data to support this. So far, no such large quantitative study has been conducted on the benefits of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for men above the age of 20.\par \par \uc2 One of the few social scientists who has conducted research on adult men involved in abortions is Jennifer Reich, a professor of sociology at the \uc2 University of Colorado\uc2 , Denver. ''Everybody benefits when individuals can control their own reproduction, but the benefit can be invisible for cis men, since they don't absorb the risks of pregnancy and it's not written on their bodies,'' said Dr. Reich.\par \par \uc2 In interviews she conducted for a 2008 study, men said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  made it possible for them to continue their education or employment goals and to ''evaluate whether this was the relationship they most wanted to have a lifelong connection to.'' Dr. Reich also found that the 20 men in her sample had been involved in 30 abortions, which could suggest that a smaller number of men are involved in a larger proportion of abortions, further supporting the need to understand men's role.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Brian T. Nguyen, an assistant professor of obstetrics and gynecology at the \uc2 University of Southern California\uc2 , has established the EMERGE lab to begin to fill the gaps in what's known about men's role in \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. His team has estimated that approximately one in five men has been involved in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is likely an underestimate, as some men might misreport because of stigma or because they are unaware of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''Men can and should be involved in sexual and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, and we'll do this work until it becomes very clear that this is everyone's issue,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 In a forthcoming paper that draws on a survey data from over 200 men who have been involved in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Dr. Nguyen and his colleagues found that approximately half of the men surveyed said they desired the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  so they could focus on the children they already had, which mirrors motivations reported by women who choose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that I've found in my research as well.\par \par \uc2 Still, such studies account for a tiny fraction of the existing research on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The lack of focus on men's role in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and how it affects them reflects a broader issue in the way society considers \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''We spend so little time thinking about how men's \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  matters and what men's experiences of reproduction are,'' said Rene Almeling, a professor of sociology at Yale.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Almeling has found that society's vision of a man's role in reproduction has come to be limited to ''having sex and providing sperm.''\par \par \uc2 This dearth in knowledge reinforces ideas about \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an issue for women alone, which results in their shouldering all reproductive responsibility. In the case of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  specifically, the lack of knowledge and cultural attention obfuscates the benefit that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access has for the partners of pregnant people as well.\par \par \uc2 According to Dr. Nguyen, the lack of focus on men's involvement in \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care may lead men to tune out the battle for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights or even become opposed to them because they feel unheard or unwelcome in the conversation.\par \par \uc2 It's easy to imagine the benefit of shifting some of the focus onto men's role in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, there's also an inherent tension around the question of what role men should have in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decisions. While the data we do have suggests that all parties benefit from having access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the pregnant partner solely carries the burden of the physical dangers of pregnancy and childbirth, which in the United States is 14 times as deadly as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is particularly dangerous for Black women. ''It is the person whose body can be or is pregnant who should be making any and all decisions,'' said Dr. Almeling.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, shedding more light on the invisible benefits of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for men could be a powerful opportunity to combat stigma and bring more people into the fight for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . After all, women have been telling their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stories for years, and Roe is still expected to fall.\par \par \uc2 Andr\u233\'00\'E9a Becker (@andreavbecker) is a medical sociologist, researcher and writer at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 , Bixby Center for Global \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY Holly Stapleton FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220527ei5r00037\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
