\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Tightest Ban On Abortions Becomes Law In Oklahoma\b0\par\par\uc2 By Luke Vander Ploeg and Kate Zernike \par \uc2 893 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The law prohibits \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the point of fertilization, and relies on civilian enforcement to sidestep Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Kevin Stitt\uc2  of Oklahoma signed a bill on Wednesday that bans nearly all abortions starting at fertilization. The new law, which takes effect immediately, is the most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in the country.\par \par \uc2 The law makes exceptions in cases where an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary to save the life of the mother, or in cases of rape or incest if they have been reported to law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 ''From the moment life begins at conception is when we have a responsibility as human beings to do everything we can to protect that baby's life and the life of the mother,'' Mr. Stitt said after signing the bill into law. ''If other states want to pass different laws, that is their right, but in Oklahoma we will always stand up for life.''\par \par \uc2 Passed by the Oklahoma Legislature last Thursday, the law relies on civilian enforcement to get around the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that was enshrined by the landmark Roe v. Wade decision.\par \par \uc2 State officials cannot bring charges. The law instead requires private citizens to sue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers or anyone who ''aids and abets'' an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- which can include a friend who drives a woman to the clinic. Successful lawsuits in civil court yield at least $10,000 in damages.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers had anticipated the governor's signature but expressed fresh outrage.\par \par \uc2 ''Oklahoma's politicians, from the governor on down, are determined to strip rights from anyone who could become pregnant,'' said Emily Wales, the president and chief executive of Planned Parenthood Great Plains. ''Today, for the first time in nearly 50 years, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal -- at every stage of pregnancy -- in an American state.''\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma has been at the head of a pack of Republican-led states passing laws banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in anticipation that next month the Supreme Court is likely to overturn Roe. A draft opinion that was leaked this month in a case involving a Mississippi law that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy suggested that a majority of justices were prepared to sign on.\par \par \uc2 Already this year the governor has signed a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that includes criminal penalties, and a six-week ban that relies on civilian enforcement.\par \par \uc2 The civil suit enforcement mechanism allows the law to sidestep court challenges that have blocked \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the past. The Oklahoma bans are modeled after a Texas law that allows individuals to sue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who offer the procedure after cardiac fetal activity is detected, about six weeks into pregnancy. The Supreme Court declined to block the enforcement of that law.\par \par \uc2 Wendi Stearman, the sponsor of the bill in the Oklahoma House, posted a picture of a newborn baby on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to celebrate the governor's signing of the law. ''The success of this bill is a direct result of the people of Oklahoma letting their representatives know that the citizens of Oklahoma value LIFE,'' she wrote. ''Keep it up, Oklahoma!''\par \par \uc2 The bill passed overwhelmingly in the Oklahoma House with a vote of 73 to 16, after its proponents, who believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is murder, spoke in its favor.\par \par \uc2 ''There can be nothing higher or more critical than the defense of innocent, unborn life,'' State Representative Jim Olsen, a Republican, said on the House floor at the time.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers have warned that the bans in Oklahoma will stress the entire region; after the ban in Texas, many women seeking abortions flooded across the border between the states.\par \par \uc2 ''We are seeing the beginning of a domino effect that will spread across the entire South and Midwest if Roe falls,'' said \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , president and chief executive of the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''Right now, patients in Oklahoma are being thrown into a state of chaos and fear. That chaos will only intensify as surrounding states cut off access as well.''\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood noted that the day after the six-week ban took effect in Oklahoma, the Tulsa Women's Reproductive Clinic was forced to cancel 35 appointments and send home 10 patients because the pregnancies were too advanced for the clinic to address without breaking the law's gestational limits.\par \par \uc2 Andrea Gallegos, the executive administrator at the Tulsa Women's Clinic, said that it had canceled its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  appointments for this week in anticipation of the governor's signature of the new bill. The clinic is working with the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  to file a lawsuit to overturn the ban, but she does not expect the clinic to last long under the current ban.\par \par \uc2 ''Abortions are what we do,'' Ms. Gallegos said. ''We don't provide any other type of services.''\par \par \uc2 In a statement after the bill was passed, Trust Women, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, vowed that its clinics in Oklahoma City and Wichita, Kan., would remain open to help pregnant women find services in places ''wherever abortions remain legal.'' The group called the bill a ''gratuitous and cruel flaunting of power by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislators.''\par \par \uc2 Gov. Kevin Stitt of Oklahoma in April after signing a bill that makes it a felony to perform an \uc2  after six weeks of pregnancy. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Sue Ogrocki/Associated Press FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220526ei5q0004o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
