\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Administration Urged to Grant Federal Workers Leave for Out-of-State Abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Benner \par \uc2 777 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The request for federal employees to be granted time off to travel out of state for the procedure comes as large companies are starting to make accommodations in the area.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Justice Department employees pressed the Biden administration on Wednesday to grant federal employees time off if they or their family members need to travel out of state to obtain abortions.\par \par \uc2 The request puts the administration in a potentially tricky position: either denying a benefit even as several large companies have made accommodations in the area, or granting one that pits it against state governments that restrict access to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Members of a group dedicated to gender equity, the Department of Justice Gender Equality Network, sent a letter asking the administration to ''swiftly consider'' the move two weeks after a leaked draft opinion showed that the Supreme Court was poised to overturn Roe v. Wade. Should the court do so in June or early July, about half the states are likely to strictly curb \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or ban it outright.\par \par \uc2 Granting leave to employees who must travel to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is akin to the time off that federal employees received to obtain coronavirus vaccinations, the group argued.\par \par \uc2 ''The administration mandated agencies to grant federal employees administrative leave so that they or their family members can obtain Covid-19 vaccinations for their 'health, safety and physical and mental well-being,''' the group said in the letter. ''Likewise, the administration should be able to provide federal workers with a reasonable amount of administrative leave to access \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care for their health, safety and physical and mental well-being.''\par \par \uc2 The letter was sent to the White House's budget office and Gender Policy Council as well as the Office of Personnel Management. A Justice Department spokeswoman declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Given that the federal government is the nation's largest employer, granting leave ''would send a powerful message to America -- and in particular, American employers,'' said Stacey Young, the president of the Department of Justice Gender Equality Network.\par \par \uc2 As of now, about 150,000 federal employees in Texas and Mississippi have little access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and an additional 227,000 federal employees in 11 other states could immediately lose access to the procedure if the Supreme Court overturns Roe, the group said.\par \par \uc2 Already, several large companies, including Amazon, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  and \uc2 Yelp\uc2 , have said they will cover some expenses for employees who must travel to receive certain medical procedures that are prohibited by state law, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The adoption of such policies in the private sector could put pressure on the Biden administration to respond in kind, offering a way for employees to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without having to use their limited sick days and vacation time to do so.\par \par \uc2 ''Employers can choose to provide accommodation, and the federal government is allowed to act like any other employer to a degree,'' said Stephen I. Vladeck, a professor at the University of Texas School of Law.\par \par \uc2 Granting leave could open the federal government up to lawsuits that claim the benefit violates the Hyde Amendment. Under the provision, federal funds cannot be used to pay for abortions, except in special circumstances, like a birth that puts the mother in mortal danger.\par \par \uc2 Providing administrative leave ''would not run afoul of the Hyde Amendment, which restricts the use of federal funding in most cases for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure itself, but does not impose restrictions on ancillary accommodations,'' the gender equality group argued in its letter.\par \par \uc2 Given that no federal money would be used to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, it would be harder to make a claim that granting leave violates the amendment, said Melissa Murray, a professor at New York University School of Law.\par \par \uc2 Granting such a benefit could also pit government employees, and possibly the federal government itself, against states that have moved to limit access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If Roe is weakened or overturned, some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates say they expect legislation seeking to further limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access to gain momentum. In Missouri, for example, a lawmaker proposed legislation that would allow private citizens to sue anyone helping people cross state lines for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Legal experts say it is conceivable that federal employees could be sued for violating such a law.\par \par \uc2 ''There could be a separate fight over whether the federal government, by simply providing leave, creates a defense for those employees,'' Mr. Vladeck said.\par \par \uc2 An \uc2  rights rally in Washington. About 150,000 federal workers in Texas and Mississippi now have little access to \uc2 . (PHOTOGRAPH BY KENNY HOLSTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220519ei5j0003r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picsca