\par\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 Pregnant? They Won't Help.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Carly Thomsen, Carrie N. Baker and Zach Levitt \par \uc2 1,472 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 With the Supreme Court likely to overturn Roe v. Wade this year, the American anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement is preparing for a near future in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is more restricted, in more parts of the country, than at any time in the past 50 years.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have spent decades fighting Roe while waiting for this moment, in part by establishing a national network of about 2,600 so-called crisis pregnancy centers, through which they try to deter women from getting abortions, often with deceptive practices -- and, in some cases, using taxpayer dollars.\par \par \uc2 As the number of C.P.C.s has increased in the United States, the number of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  facilities has dropped, from more than 2,700 in 1978 to about 785 today. As a result, the centers now outnumber \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  facilities three to one.\par \par \uc2 If the Supreme Court overturns Roe, at least 26 states are likely to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reducing the number of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  facilities nationwide to fewer than 600. In that scenario, we estimate that C.P.C.s would outnumber \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  facilities more than four to one and more than half of American women of reproductive age would live closer to a crisis pregnancy center than to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  facility.\par \par \uc2 In 1967 a Christian carpenter in Hawaii named Robert Pearson opened his home as one of the first C.P.C.s in the country, as part of the backlash to expanding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access around the United States. By 1973, the year Roe v. Wade was decided, he reported that more than 120 women had visited his center. He later published a 93-page manual on how to start such a center. It was circulated widely among anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists across the country.\par \par \uc2 The manual recommends that activists locate centers near \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  facilities, choose neutral-sounding names and show women a slide show that includes misinformation about the health risks of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The manual includes example scripts to help employees and volunteers deceive women about whether the C.P.C. performs abortions.\par \par \uc2 Many of the strategies that Mr. Pearson recommended are still used.\par \par \uc2 Once inside one of the centers, women can be greeted by volunteers or staff members wearing lab coats or hospital scrubs who, despite their appearance, are usually not medical professionals. They might try to frighten women by falsely claiming that abortions lead to breast cancer, mental health issues or infertility. They are also likely to suggest that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is much more dangerous than it is -- in reality, women are almost 15 times as likely to die because of childbirth as from an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Today, C.P.C. volunteers increasingly use ultrasound machines in order to further appear as if they provide medical care. Women who've visited C.P.C.s have reported that volunteers underestimated how far along their pregnancies were. Other women say volunteers overstated natural miscarriage rates -- so they might delay seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and miss the window in which they are legally allowed to have one.\par \par \uc2 A 2014 study concluded that 80 percent of the centers included at least one false or misleading piece of medical information on their websites. Also, it appears that some could share women's private health information with national anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  networks, something that could be especially concerning in states that criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  post-Roe.\par \par \uc2 Not all C.P.C.s engage in every tactic mentioned above, especially as the centers have come under greater scrutiny in recent years. And some provide material resources to women, like diapers and baby clothes. These resources often are limited -- not enough to care for a baby in the long term -- and come with strings attached, like required attendance at parenting or Bible classes. A large share of women who visit C.P.C.s are not pregnant or are undecided about having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but are parents who in many cases have been failed by a society that does little to help poor mothers. The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement takes advantage of their economic vulnerability.\par \par \uc2 Many C.P.C.s in the United States are affiliated with one of four Christian organizations: Care Net, Heartbeat International, Birthright International and the National Institute of Family and Life Advocates.\par \par \uc2 For Camilla Allison, who was raised in an ultraconservative Protestant community in New Mexico, C.P.C.s were viewed as agents of the church. After she became pregnant at 22, she visited a Care Net center in Albuquerque to get an ultrasound. There a staff member took her to a back room and lectured her.\par \par \uc2 ''It was almost like a Sunday school lesson, all of the fundamentalist dogma I grew up with. It brought up a lot of old shame in me,'' Ms. Allison said. ''It was all wrapped up in 'Jesus died for your sins. You've already sinned, and if you kill your child, you'll have lifelong trauma.'''\par \par \uc2 Cherisse Scott was given misinformation when she visited a Chicago C.P.C. in 2002 after seeing what appeared to be an advertisement for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Yellow Pages. She said that she was sent to another site for an ultrasound, where she was told that an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would perforate her uterus, leaving her infertile. In reality, major complications occur in less than one in 400 legal abortions.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Scott didn't learn that she had been manipulated until years later. Today she runs SisterReach, a group that helps young people and women of color find accurate health care and sexual health education.\par \par \uc2 SisterReach is based in Tennessee, where we estimate that more than 99 percent of women will live closer to a C.P.C. than to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  facility if Roe v. Wade is overturned. (Tennessee has already passed a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks of pregnancy; it would immediately go into effect post-Roe.) Ms. Scott said her work has taken on new urgency. As a result of not having access to birth control, emergency contraception or \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, ''young people will not only become pregnant but might often have to make some decisions out of desperation,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 For many women, these decisions will be shaped by the presence of C.P.C.s across the United States. The share of Black women and Latinas who live closer to a C.P.C. than to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  facility would nearly triple if Roe is overturned, according to our analysis.\par \par \uc2 Black women are already 2.5 times as likely to die during childbirth as white women. If more Black women end up at C.P.C.s, they may experience more delays in obtaining professional prenatal care and encounter untrained volunteers who give them nondiagnostic ultrasounds that miss serious medical issues.\par \par \uc2 In at least a dozen states, centers are funded in part by taxpayer money. This fiscal year, according to an Associated Press tally, a dozen states paid a total of at least $89 million to the centers, up from around $17 million a decade ago in about eight states. An analysis by Equity Forward found that some of this funding is diverted from the federal Temporary Assistance for Needy Families program, which helps low-income families pay for rent and food.\par \par \uc2 Unlike most programs that compete for government contracts, C.P.C.s are subject to almost no oversight, according to Amal Bass, a lawyer at the Women's Law Project. Investigations have alleged that crisis pregnancy centers repeatedly misused and possibly skimmed public money and that some did not provide sufficient health services to warrant funding.\par \par \uc2 Despite the centers receiving taxpayer dollars, lawmakers have struggled to regulate them. Because the centers do not charge for their services, in recent years the Better Business Bureau and state attorneys general have mostly declined to go after C.P.C.s that misled women. And most of the centers are not licensed medical facilities, so they are usually exempt from state and federal rules governing patient privacy and medical ethics.\par \par \uc2 Groups around the country are already working to limit the harms that C.P.C.s can have on their communities. In Connecticut, lawmakers recently banned deceptive advertising by crisis pregnancy centers. As with previous attempts to regulate the centers, the law is being challenged in court.\par \par \uc2 Individuals who are concerned about C.P.C.s can take action, too, by asking their representatives or church leaders to stop funding the centers or by calling their school board to make sure that a C.P.C. is not teaching the sex education in their middle or high school. And of course, change can come by simply raising awareness in one's community, including by talking to family and friends.\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPHS BY ANNIE MULLIGAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220515ei5f0007s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
