\par\par \uc2 Style Desk; SECTST\par \b \uc2 Weighing Cryptocurrency's Role in  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Access\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anna P. Kambhampaty, Alisha Haridasani Gupta and Valeriya Safronova \par \uc2 1,878 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In the loud and confusing world of cryptocurrencies, fund-raising efforts are forming around all sorts of causes. Groups known as decentralized autonomous organizations, or DAOs, have sent aid to Ukrainians whose lives have been upended by war, worked to offset carbon emissions, bought land and collected NFTs.\par \par \uc2 Now, after the leak of a Supreme Court draft opinion that would overturn Roe v. Wade, entrepreneurs and activists are floating ideas for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -focused DAO. They see it as a way to provide money to women in more than two dozen states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services may soon be severely restricted or banned -- a kind of ''Underground Railroad for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' as \uc2 Reshma Saujani\uc2 , the founder of \uc2 Girls Who Code\uc2  and a host of the ''De-Broing Crypto'' podcast, put it in an interview.\par \par \uc2 The hubris of crypto idealists often draws the ire of critics on the internet, and with good reason. Boosters have promoted blockchain technology as a solution to just about every global problem, while at the same time cryptocurrency has created plenty of new ones -- regulatory, environmental and otherwise -- and helped some of the wealthiest people grow even richer.\par \par \uc2 And though many crypto enthusiasts dangle the lure of anonymity -- an especially salient issue if Roe is overturned -- because of the precision with which the blockchain traces transactions, paying for abortions using crypto could potentially have the opposite effect: exposing both the women getting abortions and the people paying for them. Not to mention that the crypto market just went through a major crash and regularly experiences wild price fluctuations.\par \par \uc2 Still, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists and nonprofit leaders see a fund-raising opportunity. (Several anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups already accept cryptocurrency donations.)\par \par \uc2 Here's how they think an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights DAO would work: Members would pool funds that would be used to pay for out-of-state travel, in-clinic procedures and the pills that account for 54 percent of abortions in the United States. Grass-roots \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds have been doing similar work for decades, and multinational corporations such as \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  have agreed to help employees cover travel costs. (In many states, there could soon be legal consequences for financially facilitating abortions.) DAOs have raised huge sums of digital currencies in short periods of time; \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups are in a rush to do just that.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Giovannina Anthony, an OB-GYN, feels the urgency acutely. She works at a small practice in Jackson, Wyo. -- the only office in the state that provides abortions. If Roe is overturned, a trigger law will effectively ban all abortions in the state. Should that happen, she said, providers and women seeking abortions will confront new hurdles and costs.\par \par \uc2 ''They're going to have to try and find child care. They're going to risk losing their jobs. They're going to have to pay for super-expensive gas,'' Dr. Anthony said. ''We're in uncharted territory.''\par \par \uc2 Given the challenges of crypto, what may be most exciting about its role in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate -- on both sides -- is the problem fund-raisers have always faced: how to raise money quickly.\par \par \uc2 Crypto for a Cause\par \par \uc2 Last year, thousands of people raised more than $40 million in Ether, a top cryptocurrency, through a DAO in just a week with the hope of buying a first printing of the United States Constitution. Then the DAO lost the auction to \uc2 Ken Griffin\uc2 , the billionaire founder of the hedge fund \uc2 Citadel\uc2 , and the group began to dissolve.\par \par \uc2 Jonah Erlich, a core contributor, still thinks ConstitutionDAO was a valuable experiment. ''The speed at which we were able to move, as well as how many people were able to contribute, was a big success,'' he said. (Refunding contributors has been much harder; the group had to cover the high transaction fees associated with the Ethereum blockchain, so it's working with less money than it raised. ''That is a problem that's still being worked on,'' Mr. Erlich said.)\par \par \uc2 Separately, UkraineDAO, organized in part by Nadya Tolokonnikova of Pussy Riot, raised more than $7 million in a few days by selling an NFT of a Ukrainian flag. In March, President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelensky\uc2  signed into law a bill legalizing cryptocurrency exchanges in the country. As of that month, according to Alex Bornyakov, Ukraine's deputy minister of digital transformation, the country had received nearly $100 million in crypto donations.\par \par \uc2 But these are isolated examples in what remains for many an impenetrable sphere of finance. Only 16 percent of Americans have experience with cryptocurrencies -- through investing, trading or paying for things -- according to a recent Pew survey, and more than half of them are men. Affluent men. Those demographics can mean that the fanciful projects some crypto entrepreneurs have proposed don't always reflect public interests, and can seem driven by impulse.\par \par \uc2 ''Anytime there's a problem, people say that crypto or web3 is going to be the solution without putting much thought into it,'' said Molly White, a software developer who has been archiving crypto crashes and missteps on a website called Web3 Is Going Just Great.\par \par \uc2 Ms. White pointed to the crypto project Nemus, which aims to raise funds for the \uc2 Amazon\uc2  rainforest. ''People are talking about protecting portions of the \uc2 Amazon\uc2  rainforest, but they're using blockchains that are very damaging to the environment,'' she said, referring to the energy-intensive process of putting cryptocurrencies into circulation. ''It's like they don't see the damage they're doing with the solutions that they're proposing.''\par \par \uc2 There have been attempts to make crypto donations more accessible to charitable organizations. Change, a company that sells an interface for web donations, recently created a nonprofit arm that manages crypto wallets for charitable organizations. The point is to avoid ''putting the daunting security, technical and accounting burden on them,'' said Sonia Nigam, the chief executive of Change. Donations are paid out to those organizations -- including \uc2 Girls Who Code\uc2 , \uc2 Make-a-Wish Foundation\uc2  and One Tree Planted -- in the form of grants.\par \par \uc2 Most national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nonprofits and health care providers still do not accept cryptocurrency. Elliott Kozuch, a senior communications strategist at the nonprofit \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2 , wrote in an email that ''crypto has not come up in conversation at NARAL, including in relation to the SCOTUS leak.''\par \par \uc2 But there could be a shift coming. Elissa Maercklein, the chief executive of Crypto Chicks, an NFT collective, said that the organization made a donation of 5 Ether (worth more than $15,000 at the time, and around $10,000 as of Friday) to the \uc2 International Planned Parenthood Federation\uc2  in February; it was the first crypto donation the organization accepted.\par \par \uc2 ''When I contacted them, they were a bit surprised, but excited to learn more,'' Ms. Maercklein said. She has worked with I.P.P.F. to ensure the organization will accept crypto donations moving forward through a platform called the Giving Block. The platform also enables several anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups, including \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 , National Right to Life and Students for Life of America, to accept donations in cryptocurrencies.\par \par \uc2 ''We offer nonprofits the ability to protect donor privacy by allowing anonymous donations,'' Alex Wilson, a founder of the Giving Block, wrote in an email. ''This has become popular for human rights organizations where donors might not otherwise give for fear of retaliation or targeting based on a cause they support.''\par \par \uc2 The Privacy Question\par \par \uc2 Blockchain technology, which underpins every cryptocurrency exchange, produces a public ledger where all transactions are recorded. If that's the case, is crypto really more private than other payments?\par \par \uc2 It can be. Monero and Zcash, for instance, are known as ''privacy coins.'' Unlike Bitcoin or Ethereum, Monero's public ledger doesn't show addresses or amounts for each transaction, making it harder to identify how funds are moving.\par \par \uc2 Justin Ehrenhofer, the vice president for operations at Cake Wallet, which allows people to store Monero, said that the currency could be used to discreetly buy \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills in cases in which that may become illegal. (Right now, he said, common uses include black market purchases and ransomware payments.) But he noted that using Monero requires more technical expertise than Bitcoin or Ethereum.\par \par \uc2 In part because of the Silk Road, a digital black market where thousands of vendors sold drugs and other illegal goods from 2011 to 2013, cryptocurrency is often thought of as a way to make private payments. But Sarah Meiklejohn, a professor of cryptography and security at \uc2 University College London\uc2 , said that most digital currency payments were fairly traceable.\par \par \uc2 ''The transactions that you make are less inherently tied to your name,'' Professor Meiklejohn said, because people typically use pseudonyms, but they are visible to everyone on a digital ledger. ''Literally anyone in the world can go look at the transactions that are taking place.''\par \par \uc2 Pseudonyms, she said, ''provide a really thin level of anonymity.'' \uc2 Chainalysis\uc2 , which has become the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 's blockchain surveillance partner of choice, can identify patterns in transactions made through certain wallets, though a company representative said in an email that \uc2 Chainalysis\uc2  aims to protect individual privacy. Likewise, \uc2 Coinbase\uc2 , one of the more mainstream cryptocurrency exchanges, states on its website that it maintains records of transactions and may report some to law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 ''With privacy, it's like, once it's out, it's out,'' Professor Meiklejohn said.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Rebecca Gomperts, a physician and the director of \uc2 Women on Waves\uc2 , a nonprofit that provides resources for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers, found this to be the case when she tried setting up her own crypto wallet. ''It had exactly the same diligence requests as a normal bank account, where you have to provide IDs and other information,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 She could see how anonymous transactions might appeal to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, whose work could soon turn them into legal targets. But, she said, ''I haven't found a cryptocurrency where you can do that.''\par \par \uc2 Legal scholars are not convinced that cryptocurrencies would shield patients in most cases. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans ''will cover everything, whether you pay with cash or crypto,'' said Rachel Rebouch\u233\'00\'E9, the interim dean at the Temple University Beasley School of Law and an author of a forthcoming paper called ''The New \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Battleground.''\par \par \uc2 ''If \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal in your state -- it doesn't matter whether you get a surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , whether you self-manage your \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- if it's illegal, it's illegal,'' said Kimberly Mutcherson, a dean and professor of law at Rutgers Law School who has focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (In the first three months of this year, 22 states introduced more than 100 restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills approved by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 , according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  research group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.)\par \par \uc2 Still, organizations like \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  are keeping an open mind about how they might raise and distribute funds.\par \par \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2 , the organization's president and chief executive, said \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  was ''looking into a number of things'' in the realm of cryptocurrencies but would not divulge details.\par \par \uc2 ''The bottom line is all of the options are on the table,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY Mark Pernice FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220515ei5f0005e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
