\par\par \uc2 JAMELLE BOUIE\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 The Weak Pro-Democracy Case Against Roe\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamelle Bouie \par \uc2 1,650 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A significant part of the case against Roe v. Wade is that the Supreme Court was wrong to intervene in 1973 to recognize a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  while the democratic process was still playing out. Better, instead, to have left the issue to the states -- to voters and elected officials -- who could then tailor their laws to their respective communities.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  takes note of this in his draft opinion overruling Roe. ''In some states,'' he writes, ''voters may believe that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right should be even more extensive than the right that Roe and Casey recognized.'' Voters in other states, he continues, ''may wish to impose tight restrictions based on their belief that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  destroys an 'unborn human being.''' He concludes that ''Our nation's historical understanding of ordered liberty does not prevent the people's elected representatives from deciding how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be regulated.''\par \par \uc2 The end of Roe, in this telling, is a victory for democracy against judgeocracy.\par \par \uc2 That might be true, if Americans lived with fair and representative institutions. But they don't. And even if they did, there's more to democracy than just voting or the process of making a law.\par \par \uc2 Which is to say that the pro-democracy argument against Roe v. Wade and the constitutional right to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  falters on a few key realities. The first relates to democratic government, or the lack thereof, in the states. The second relates to the expansion of state power inherent in any effective law against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And the third concerns the intimate relationship between bodily autonomy and political equality.\par \par \uc2 On the first point, let's begin with a little Madison. Among the most famous essays in American political thought is Federalist No. 10. In it, James Madison makes his case for the ''extended republic'' against naysayers who argue that the United States is too big to be a functional country with a representative government.\par \par \uc2 His argument, in brief, is that the smaller the republic, the more acute the ''violence of faction'' (defined here as a group united by ''some common impulse of passion or of interest'' and ''adverse to the permanent and aggregate interests of the community'') to its citizens.\par \par \uc2 ''The smaller the society,'' Madison writes,\par \par \uc2 the fewer probably will be the distinct parties and interests composing it; the fewer the distinct parties and interests, the more frequently will a majority be found of the same party; and the smaller the number of individuals composing a majority, and the smaller the compass within which they are placed, the more easily will they concert and execute their plans of oppression.\par \par \uc2 He concludes that if you\par \par \uc2 extend the sphere, and you take in a greater variety of parties and interests; you make it less probable that a majority of the whole will have a common motive to invade the rights of other citizens; or if such a common motive exists, it will be more difficult for all who feel it to discover their own strength, and act in unison with each other.\par \par \uc2 Madison's point is that a federal union will be less vulnerable to the ''mischiefs of faction'' than the states it comprises, that ''the influence of factious leaders may kindle a flame within their particular states, but will be unable to spread a general conflagration through the other states.''\par \par \uc2 Now, Madison's theory isn't airtight (mostly because it doesn't anticipate the emergence of national political parties), but it isn't wrong, either. It is easier for narrow factions to win power at the state level than for them to win control of the federal government.\par \par \uc2 And this, in essence, is what has happened with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Last year, in a review of public opinion data, the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  found 14 states where a majority of adults agreed that ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be illegal in all or most cases.'' State legislatures in each may well outlaw the practice if the Supreme Court overrules Roe v. Wade. But so will legislatures in states where a majority of adults support legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in all or most cases. Fifty-six percent of Florida adults, according to Pew, support the status quo under Roe. Despite this, Florida lawmakers have already passed a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. A similar situation exists in Oklahoma, where 51 percent of adults support the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in most cases but where the Republican governor just signed a far stricter ban into law. Then there are states -- like Arizona, Michigan and Wisconsin -- where pre-Roe bans may take immediate effect if Roe is overturned.\par \par \uc2 But, Alito might say, if voters do not want their states to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they can elect representatives who will then take steps to protect it.\par \par \uc2 That's not so simple. Thanks to Alito's own votes and opinions (and those of his conservative colleagues) in Shelby County v. Holder, Rucho v. Common Cause and Brnovich v. \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2 , state legislatures have nearly free rein to restrict voting, gerrymander in a hyperpartisan fashion and otherwise insulate themselves from democratic accountability.\par \par \uc2 A pro-Roe electoral majority might exist in Wisconsin, but the state Republican Party has gerrymandered itself into durable control of the legislature; it needs only a minority of voters to win a majority of seats. The same is true in states like Ohio and North Carolina where -- according to a New York Times analysis of public opinion data -- most adults support Roe.\par \par \uc2 In other words, there are a number of states -- home to tens of millions of Americans -- where voters may not actually have the power to elect lawmakers to protect the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights they say they want. If states and state legislatures are supposed to be the places where democracy happens -- and that itself is debatable -- then these facts are a real challenge for the pro-democracy case against Roe.\par \par \uc2 Next is the matter of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans themselves.\par \par \uc2 Although anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists insist that they intend to penalize only providers and clinics, lawmakers in Republican-led states have already introduced bills that would criminalize patients as well. But even if that weren't the case, there's simply no way to enforce an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban without the state intruding deeply into women's lives.\par \par \uc2 Think about what it would take to establish that someone had an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The state and its agents would need access to everything from search results, call histories, text messages and medical records to bank statements, social media posts and location data. It would need to turn its attention to anyone who may have helped, friends and family included. (To this point, the Texas bounty law extends legal liability in exactly this manner.)\par \par \uc2 The state would need to treat the womb -- any womb -- as a potential crime scene, with anything other than a healthy birth as evidence of a possible crime. A miscarriage or stillbirth would have to invite the same scrutiny as an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . There is no other place a total ban can go. Indeed, this kind of scrutiny is already part of daily life for many women, especially those who are poor, nonwhite or both. The criminalization of pregnancy is not new, but it is poised to get much worse.\par \par \uc2 There's a word for this, and it's certainly not ''democracy.''\par \par \uc2 Which brings us to the final problem with the idea that a world without Roe is somehow more democratic. Democracy rests, on paper at least, on the idea of political equality -- that all citizens have equal standing and equal say when it comes to representation and political decision-making.\par \par \uc2 But equal standing is undermined and eroded when the state can effectively seize your person for its own ends -- that is, when it can force you to give birth. And the erosion of political equality has social consequences; it leads to disregard and disrespect, to treating the people in question as a subordinate class.\par \par \uc2 We know this. Prisoners represent the most extreme end. They are citizens, but they are not political equals. We see it in our history as well; before Jim Crow, there was the decades-long effort to erode the already tenuous political standing of Black Americans in the aftermath of Reconstruction.\par \par \uc2 To put the right to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up for debate is to put the bodily autonomy of women up for debate. There's no other way to spin it. It's just the nature of the thing. And to put the bodily autonomy of women up for debate is to degrade their citizenship, their social standing and their political equality.\par \par \uc2 Assuming Roe is overturned, there may be more legislators casting votes over the right to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but that's not the same as more democracy. Just the opposite: States that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will undermine the values of democracy and curtail the liberty of their citizens. Subjected to surveillance and criminal scrutiny, people who give birth in those states -- and those who support their right to privacy and bodily autonomy -- will live with a degraded form of citizenship.\par \par \uc2 Democracy is substantive as well as procedural; it is a set of values as well as a set of processes. Our system can and should be much more representative than it is. But even if it were, a democracy that allows this strict control of reproduction -- that curtails the rights of its citizens in this manner -- isn't worthy of the name.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY SHURAN HUANG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220515ei5f0004t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
