\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Effects of Roe On Midterms Worry G.O.P.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Maya King, Alexander Burns, Dan Simmons and Ryan Patrick Hooper \par \uc2 1,940 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The party was making headway with suburban women on crime, schools and inflation. Now the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate is front and center.\par \par \uc2 ATLANTA -- For months, Republicans have been poised to make inroads in the diverse and economically comfortable suburbs of cities like Atlanta. The moderate communities here swung toward Democrats in recent years, led by women appalled by Donald J. Trump. But lately, rampant inflation and rising crime have taken a political toll on President Biden and his party.\par \par \uc2 Sandra Sloan, 82, is the kind of voter Republicans are counting on to help them reclaim this contested section of a newly purple state. Yet Ms. Sloan, a retired high school teacher who lives in Atlanta's upscale Buckhead neighborhood, is uneasy about the party for one main reason.\par \par \uc2 ''I am a Republican, but I still believe that it's a woman's right to choose,'' Ms. Sloan said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Sloan said she had followed the news recently about a leaked Supreme Court draft opinion striking down Roe v. Wade, as well as the passage of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation in states like Texas and Oklahoma. She said she was not sure how she would ultimately vote in the fall, but \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights would be a factor.\par \par \uc2 ''We still don't know, after the draft, when it's finished what it will say,'' Ms. Sloan said. ''But leaving it to just men -- I'm sorry, no.''\par \par \uc2 It is voters like Ms. Sloan, in communities like Buckhead, who may represent the greatest challenge for Republicans in a renewed national debate over the rights of women to legally terminate a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Should the Supreme Court strike down Roe in the sweeping manner of Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 .'s draft opinion, it would unleash a ferocious state-by-state battle over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations -- and introduce a powerful new issue into the calculus of voters who might otherwise be inclined to treat the midterm election as an up-or-down vote on Mr. Biden's performance in the presidency. Moderate women who have tilted back toward the Republicans might now have second thoughts; young people who feel let down by Mr. Biden could well find motivation to vote Democratic out of a feeling of fear and indignation about the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 The urgency of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue could be particularly intense in Georgia, where state lawmakers in 2019 passed a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the sixth week of pregnancy, knowing at the time that existing Supreme Court precedent would forbid the law from going into effect. If that precedent is overturned, then Georgia voters could find themselves living under one of the most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the country.\par \par \uc2 National Democrats have indicated they intend to campaign on the issue ahead of the midterms in November. On Wednesday, Senate Democrats voted to provide a broad guarantee of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights nationwide, though they knew the bill lacked enough support to overcome Republican opposition.\par \par \uc2 Many Republicans, however, are hesitant to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  outright. On the campaign trail, Republican candidates have been encouraged by party leaders to focus on the economy, crime and the border, according to a memo from the National Republican Senatorial Committee obtained by Axios.\par \par \uc2 State Senator Jen Jordan, a Democrat running for attorney general of Georgia, said she expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights issue to eclipse other concerns as a top consideration for voters.\par \par \uc2 Previously, Ms. Jordan said she had been campaigning on issues related to the cost of living, vowing to crack down on price gouging. The leaked Supreme Court opinion ''completely changed the conversation,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 ''I think fundamental rights is a little bit above the day-to-day economic issues that have been batted around,'' Ms. Jordan said.\par \par \uc2 In closely divided states and congressional districts around the country, many moderate voters suddenly find themselves choosing between a Democratic Party that has disappointed them since taking power in 2021, and a Republican Party newly emboldened to enact a right-wing social agenda that makes many voters deeply uneasy.\par \par \uc2 That could create a major challenge for Republicans in their efforts to win back the centrist and center-right communities that shunned them during the Trump years and turned America's suburbs -- from areas near Atlanta and Philadelphia to Minneapolis and Salt Lake City -- into at least a temporary political desert for the party. That exodus was particularly pronounced among centrist and even Republican-leaning white women, a constituency that tends to favor \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights with modest limitations.\par \par \uc2 Christine Matthews, a pollster who has studied the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue and worked in the past for Republicans, said she expected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to become a top concern of the 2022 elections. But she said it was too soon to gauge how voters would prioritize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as an issue relative to other close-to-home considerations, like the cost and availability of consumer goods.\par \par \uc2 ''We've never been in a situation like this,'' Ms. Matthews said, adding, ''We are in a situation where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are now being threatened in a way they haven't been in nearly 50 years.''\par \par \uc2 Voters, she added, were likely to see six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans like Georgia's as ''well outside the mainstream.''\par \par \uc2 National Republicans have attempted to mute the political impact of Roe by urging their candidates to focus on unpopular elements of the Democratic Party's position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , shifting the focus from the hard-line views of the right and making Democrats defend their opposition to most limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In Washington, Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the minority leader, acknowledged it was possible that Republicans might seek to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the federal level but stopped well short of pledging to do so.\par \par \uc2 Some Republicans have been far less guarded about their intentions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulation. Gov. Brian Kemp of Georgia, a conservative Republican who signed the six-week ban, is facing a primary challenge from a former senator, \uc2 David Perdue\uc2 , who is demanding that Mr. Kemp call a special session of the state legislature to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  altogether.\par \par \uc2 Other swing states have passed strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, including a 15-week ban in Arizona, and Republican lawmakers in Wisconsin have introduced a measure to ban the procedure after six weeks. The most extreme restrictions have been proposed in deeply conservative states like Louisiana, where legislators debated a bill that would have classified \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a form of homicide, and would have made it possible to bring criminal charges against women who end their pregnancies. Lawmakers scrapped the bill on Thursday before it reached a vote.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, where the offices of an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group were set on fire on Sunday, Republicans are defending a Senate seat and seeking to defeat Gov. \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2 , a Democrat. A crackdown on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could alienate some of the moderate voters who would otherwise be reliable Republican votes. The state already has a dormant law, enacted in 1849, that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in nearly all cases. The current Republican front-runner for governor, \uc2 Rebecca Kleefisch\uc2 , has said she totally opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plenty of voters feel more conflicted. Nancy Turtenwald, 64, of West Allis, Wis., an inner-ring suburb of Milwaukee, said she had voted Republican her entire life but also supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Ms. Turtenwald said she would prefer that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  not be the main issue in the country's political discourse, citing access to health care, the cost of gas and housing, and the availability of baby formula as more important issues.\par \par \uc2 If Roe is overturned and Republicans attempt to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Ms. Turtenwald said, she will consider crossing over to vote for Democrats. ''I think a lot of women would,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  poll published last week found that about three in five Americans believed that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases, though many of those people said they preferred some restrictions on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, Republicans are seeking to defeat Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , a Democratic rising star, and pick up several congressional seats. Similar to Wisconsin, Michigan never repealed an archaic law that makes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a felony, meaning that the procedure could be instantly criminalized in the event of a court decision that closely resembled Justice Alito's draft.\par \par \uc2 The state's Democratic attorney general, Dana Nessel, has announced that she would not enforce that law; like Ms. Whitmer, she is facing a competitive fight for re-election.\par \par \uc2 Rose Deveson is a 60-year-old homemaker from Birmingham, Mich., a town in politically split Oakland County about 25 minutes from downtown Detroit. Ms. Deveson said she had been a reliable Republican voter for 20 years until disgust with Mr. Trump drove her to vote for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Deveson said she would still consider voting for Republican candidates on a case-by-case basis, mainly because she found the party appealing on fiscal issues. But she said she could not embrace candidates who led an attack on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''I cannot believe what they are trying to do,'' Ms. Deveson said. ''The fiscals will work themselves out, but this is about our rights and what kind of country we want to live in.''\par \par \uc2 Simona Vancea, 38, of Sterling Heights, Mich., described herself as a ''pro-choice'' voter who cast her ballot for Mr. Trump in 2020 because she believed he would run the country better than Mr. Biden.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Vancea said she believed that ''every woman should be able to do whatever she feels comfortable with'' when it comes to pregnancy and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But a decision striking down Roe would not change her overall inclination to support the Republican Party, she said.\par \par \uc2 ''I would vote pro-choice on that specific issue, but I still sway more to the Republican side,'' Ms. Vancea said.\par \par \uc2 In many states, however, the internal politics of the Republican Party are already pushing its candidates even further rightward on the issue, testing the tolerance of voters who like the party's agenda on other issues but oppose the strictest limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Jessica Wood, a stay-at-home mother in Suwanee, Ga., grew politically active last year when she organized against pandemic-driven restrictions in schools. Ms. Wood, who identifies as libertarian, said she was frustrated with Democratic leaders in Washington but was not sure if she would vote Republican or cast her ballot for a third party this year.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Wood said she was ''100 percent'' supportive of women having access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though she would never feel comfortable having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  herself. She said the possibility of a six-week ban going into effect gave her pause.\par \par \uc2 ''There's just so many gray areas,'' Ms. Wood said. ''I feel like people are just going to do it anyway. So it might as well be safe and legal.''\par \par \uc2 Maya King reported from Atlanta, Alexander Burns from Washington, Dan Simmons from Milwaukee and Ryan Patrick Hooper from Detroit.\par \par \uc2 Maya King reported from Atlanta, Alexander Burns from Washington, Dan Simmons from Milwaukee and Ryan Patrick Hooper from Detroit.\par \par \uc2 -rights protesters in front of the Supreme Court after a draft opinion indicated the court might overturn Roe v. Wade. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KENNY HOLSTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Sandra Sloan, 82, said she is a Republican who supports \uc2  rights. Far right, Rose Deveson, 60, who lives in Birmingham, Mich., said she had been a Republican voter for 20 years but could not embrace candidates who led an attack on \uc2  rights. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY NICOLE CRAINE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; CYDNI ELLEDGE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A16) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220514ei5e0004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
