\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 New York Is Gearing Up To Be a Post-Roe Refuge\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ashley Wong and Lola Fadulu \par \uc2 1,854 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When New York legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1970, nearly two-thirds of New York City's abortions were for women from elsewhere. If Roe is overturned, a new surge is expected.\par \par \uc2 In the post-Roe America that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates say they have long seen coming, pregnant women in many states will scrape together money, book hotels, arrange child care and board buses, trains and planes bound for places that provide safe abortions. New York will be one of them.\par \par \uc2 For New York, that would represent a return to the years before Roe v. Wade established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The state legalized the procedure in 1970, three years before the Supreme Court ruling, and soon became a sanctuary for out-of-state patients seeking to terminate their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 The New York law allowed abortions within 24 weeks of pregnancy, and at any time if the woman's life was at risk. In the two years after it passed, nearly two-thirds of an estimated 402,000 abortions performed in New York City were for women who had come from elsewhere, health officials estimated.\par \par \uc2 One of those women was Teresa Meade, now 73, whose friends drove her to New York from Massachusetts in 1971 after they raised several hundred dollars for the trip.\par \par \uc2 ''New York was just known as being the closest and most accessible, and also New York was known for having clinics where it was very efficient and sympathetic,'' Ms. Meade said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Now, with the Supreme Court seemingly poised to overturn Roe, advocates, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and lawmakers are working to expand New York's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights laws, and gear up for a potential surge of out-of-state patients.\par \par \uc2 Local leaders of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds, which assist people who face financial and logistical barriers, are ramping up fund-raising efforts. Providers are studying the law to understand whether they are protected if they treat patients from states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal. Community groups are spreading the word about medication abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''The movement has been sounding the alarm for years,'' said Chelsea Williams-Diggs, a board member of the New York \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access Fund, a volunteer-driven \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund.\par \par \uc2 Already, a growing percentage of New York \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients appear to be coming from out of state. Nearly 9 percent of abortions performed in New York in 2019 were for out-of-state residents, according to the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control\uc2 , compared with about 4 percent in 2009.\par \par \uc2 Groups that oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  said they were also preparing for an influx of out-of-state patients if Roe is overturned.\par \par \uc2 ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is never a solution,'' said the Rev. Brian A. Graebe of the Basilica of St. Patrick's Old Cathedral in Manhattan. He said his congregation works closely with the Sisters of Life, a group of Catholic nuns who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to bolster services such as housing and job training for women who ultimately choose to continue their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 A legislative push to solidify rights\par \par \uc2 In 2019, sensing that Roe might not always be around, New York officials passed the \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2  Act, which codified the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and allowed the procedure after 24 weeks of pregnancy in cases where the fetus wasn't viable or the health of the mother was at risk.\par \par \uc2 Thirteen states have trigger laws that would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if Roe is overturned, and officials in several are seeking to punish patients who seek care elsewhere and doctors who provide it.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers have introduced a number of bills aimed at supporting providers, including measures that would shield them from certain legal liability and prohibit law enforcement from cooperating with out-of-state investigations on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases.\par \par \uc2 There is also an amendment, sponsored by State Senator \uc2 Liz Krueger\uc2 , that would enshrine the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the State Constitution, one of many measures on the table that would strengthen and expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Many Republicans in the State Legislature oppose such actions, calling them a waste of government resources. ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are protected in New York, regardless of the Supreme Court's ultimate decision. New York Democrats are fully aware of that,'' said Assemblyman Will Barclay, the minority leader, adding that the plan to spend millions was ''unfortunate and unnecessary.''\par \par \uc2 But in heavily Democratic New York, legislative attempts to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are unlikely to gain traction. On Tuesday, Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  announced a $35 million fund for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics that she said were already treating more out-of-state patients. Unlike the legislative options under consideration, Ms. Hochul's action went into effect immediately.\par \par \uc2 Most of the money, $25 million, is being funneled to providers through the state health department, while the remaining $10 million is going to grants for security, which Ms. Hochul framed as critical given violent attacks on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers or clinics.\par \par \uc2 ''No one should feel unsafe accessing health care, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, and no one should feel unsafe providing that health care as well,'' she said, adding that she hoped the commitment would ''set an example'' for the nation.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents, who have long been outnumbered in New York, are also organizing around the issue. The Catholic Bishops of New York State on Thursday called on elected officials to shift public policy away from supporting abortions and toward educating women about their options, in hopes of steering them toward making different choices.\par \par \uc2 ''Let us work toward making New York a state where even if \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not illegal, it will one day be unthinkable,'' the bishops said in a statement.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds prepare for 'a dramatic increase'\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds, which help \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients arrange and pay for their procedures, say they are already seeing growing demand this year.\par \par \uc2 As of early May, the New York \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access Fund had pledged about $350,000 this year to people seeking abortions, more than twice the $150,000 it had promised by this point last year.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Diggs and Karla Salguero, two of the fund's board members, said it is too soon to tell whether last week's leak of the draft Supreme Court decision overturning Roe has directly translated into a surge of out-of-state interest.\par \par \uc2 But they have seen a steady uptick in calls in recent months from outside New York, especially from the South.\par \par \uc2 Last September, Texas passed a law effectively banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state, kicking off the most recent wave of restrictions across the country. In the eight months since, the fund has helped 28 callers from Texas, compared with just five callers in the previous eight-month period.\par \par \uc2 Patients who contact the Brigid Alliance, a group based in New York that assists with logistical costs, usually travel about 1,000 miles to get care, said Odile Schalit, the group's executive director, and their needs typically amount to about $1,000.\par \par \uc2 When the Brigid Alliance began in 2018, the organization helped about 20 patients a month, Ms. Schalit said. Now, it supports as many as 125 monthly.\par \par \uc2 ''We are expecting that the number of people who need to travel to New York or states like New York is going to grow,'' Ms. Schalit said. ''We're expecting a dramatic increase, no question.''\par \par \uc2 'I tremble to think about what could have happened'\par \par \uc2 For Representative Gwen Moore, a Wisconsin Democrat representing Milwaukee, securing a safe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York in 1972 would never have been possible without the help of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund based in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 ''I had no money,'' Ms. Moore said. ''I tremble to think about what could have happened.''\par \par \uc2 She was about 20 years old, she said, and studying at \uc2 Marquette University\uc2 . She already had one child, whom her family was helping her raise.\par \par \uc2 A second child would have forced her to drop out of school and would have stretched her family's resources to the limit. But she couldn't afford to travel to New York, let alone pay for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''What you hear people say,'' Ms. Moore said, ''Black and brown women are going to bear the brunt of not making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  accessible -- I was that person.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Moore happened to have befriended several women who were white and middle class, and had a phone number for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund in Madison.\par \par \uc2 Within a few days, she was on a plane to New York, her first time traveling there. The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund had arranged for someone to pick her up at the airport and take her to a clinic.\par \par \uc2 Hours later, she was headed home. All of the costs had been covered.\par \par \uc2 ''I have had no regrets, not one day of regrets, for having terminated that pregnancy,'' she said. ''It was for me, my little girl and my future family, my two sons that I had. I did it for them.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Meade was 22 in 1971 when she was raped by four men at gunpoint in Connecticut as she waited for a bus to visit friends in Boston.\par \par \uc2 She said the assault began after the men pulled her into a car. Then they took her to a motel, where it continued. ''It went on for a long time,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 One of the men got her pregnant. She said it was well-known at the time that New York was the place to go for a safe and legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so her friends helped her scrape together money and drove her.\par \par \uc2 ''When you come through a traumatic event, the last thing in the world you need is to have to drive to another state,'' Ms. Meade said.\par \par \uc2 Merle Hoffman, 76, who founded one of the first \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in the country, Choices Women's Medical Center in Queens, recalled the busy years before Roe was decided.\par \par \uc2 Representatives from Manhattan clinics would meet women seeking abortions at airports or bus stations, wearing lapel pins to identify themselves. At Ms. Hoffman's clinic, the first patient, in 1971, was from New Jersey, where abortions were illegal.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hoffman said that in the past six or seven years, as more states have moved to restrict the procedure, the clinic has revived its program to help out-of-town patients.\par \par \uc2 If the Supreme Court strikes down Roe, she expects demand to surge.\par \par \uc2 She said her clinic, which sees up to 40,000 patients per year, about half of whom need abortions, will continue to work closely with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds to pay for the procedures, travel and lodging.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't care how many bans they put in place,'' she said. ''We're standing here.''\par \par \uc2 Sharon Otterman, Grace Ashford and Karen Zraick contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Sharon Otterman, Grace Ashford and Karen Zraick contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Merle Hoffman, center left, in the 1980s. She opened one of the first U.S. \uc2  clinics. (PHOTOGRAPH VIA MERLE HOFFMAN); Representative Gwen Moore, left, traveled to New York in 1972 for an \uc2 . Teresa Meade, right, received an \uc2  in New York after being raped at gunpoint in Connecticut in 1971. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY ANNA MONEYMAKER/GETTY IMAGES; SARA NAOMI LEWKOWICZ FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220514ei5e00045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
