\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 A New Calculus for Democratic Female Governors\b0\par\par\uc2 By Leah Askarinam \par \uc2 1,904 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  hangs in the balance, Democratic women who occupy or are vying for governors' mansions are on the front lines of the messaging fight.\par \par \uc2 Every female governor's seat is up for election this year. All nine of them.\par \par \uc2 The three Republicans are likely to sail to re-election. It's a different story on the Democratic side, where most of the women rode in on the 2018 wave, flipping Republican seats.\par \par \uc2 That year, \uc2 Laura Kelly\uc2  of Kansas campaigned on education, and \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  of Michigan pledged to ''fix the damn roads.'' \uc2 Janet Mills\uc2  of Maine, \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2  of New Mexico and \uc2 Kate Brown\uc2  of Oregon benefited, in an especially favorable climate, from running in states that lean toward Democrats.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, however, everything has changed for Democrats -- and one big issue has become a five-alarm fire for the party.\par \par \uc2 As the Supreme Court stands poised to overturn Roe v. Wade and throw regulations on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the states, governors are set to be on the front lines of the political clashes that would follow.\par \par \uc2 The end of Roe would also put Democratic female governors in a position both powerful and precarious: unique messengers on an urgent issue for the party, who hold more real ability to effect change than their counterparts in a gridlocked Congress -- and who must balance a range of other priorities for voters in a challenging election year.\par \par \uc2 Democrats and their allies believe that focusing on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will resonate from red states like Kansas to blue states like Oregon, even if candidates tailor their messaging to their states.\par \par \uc2 ''We're moving into a completely new world,'' Cecile Richards, the former president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and the daughter of former Gov. Ann Richards of Texas, told me recently.\par \par \uc2 While polling has tended to show \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  relatively low on the list of voters' priorities, supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights argue that this conventional wisdom should be tossed out the window. Those polling questions, they say, were asked when the idea of losing the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was only theoretical.\par \par \uc2 ''The fundamental issue that gets lost in reporting isn't how voters feel about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  personally,'' Richards said. ''The question is, who do they want in charge of making decisions about pregnancy?''\par \par \uc2 More effective messengers\par \par \uc2 On both sides of the aisle, strategists often prefer women to carry out messaging on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Kelly Dittmar, a professor at the Center for American Women and Politics at \uc2 Rutgers University\uc2 , said that when she interviewed women in Congress, she found that both Republicans and Democrats saw themselves as the best messengers, leveraging their identities as women and mothers.\par \par \uc2 Republicans in particular sometimes find that it is more effective to have women affirm that they oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Women have delivered both parties big victories in recent years: Female Democratic candidates helped take back the House for their party in 2018, and Republican women recovered many of those losses in 2020.\par \par \uc2 ''In some ways, it's because women are really good candidates that they're in the most competitive races, particularly the incumbents,'' Dittmar said of the 2022 governor contests. ''They're there because they won races that people didn't think they could win, like Kansas and even Michigan.''\par \par \uc2 Male Republican candidates, especially those in battleground states, face greater risks when talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Holly Richardson, a Republican former state representative in Utah who described herself as ''pro-life'' and supports access to contraception and sex education, said she had been ''a little horrified'' by what Republicans in other states have said about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''We need to decrease the perceptive need for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and we do that by supporting women,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 The nation's Republican female governors -- \uc2 Kay Ivey\uc2  of Alabama, \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  of South Dakota and \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  of Iowa -- oversee solidly red states, and have long campaigned against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That might not shift much, even if Roe is overturned.\par \par \uc2 ''Where the messaging might change more is on the Democratic side,'' Dittmar said. ''Because they're saying, 'Now we have to hold the line.'''\par \par \uc2 From Michigan to Oklahoma\par \par \uc2 Among Democratic female governors, there's virtually no debate about whether women should have access to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a guest essay for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , Governor Whitmer highlighted a lawsuit she filed last month asking the Michigan Supreme Court to examine whether the state's Constitution included the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. She wrote that the suit could ''offer a course of action'' for other politicians to follow.\par \par \uc2 Other Democrats, perhaps recognizing that the party has few legislative or judicial options nationally, have stuck to broader pledges to try to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , the presumptive Democratic nominee for governor of Georgia, recently promised attendees at an Emily's List gala that ''we will fight every day from now to Election Day and beyond, because this is a fight for who we are.''\par \par \uc2 In red states, Democratic candidates for governor are walking a finer line.\par \par \uc2 In Kansas, \uc2 Laura Kelly\uc2  has reiterated her support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, but she has so far focused more on education and taxes, issues that helped her win in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Joy Hofmeister, a Democrat running for governor of Oklahoma who left the Republican Party last year, described herself as ''pro-life,'' but said she believed women should make choices about their \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  with their doctor.\par \par \uc2 She avoided taking a position on Roe v. Wade specifically, saying that the Supreme Court would not ''be calling to ask my opinion.''\par \par \uc2 Hofmeister, who serves as the superintendent of public instruction in Oklahoma, criticized Gov. \uc2 Kevin Stitt\uc2 , a Republican, for signing into law some of the most restrictive legislation on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the country, a measure prohibiting the procedure after about six weeks of pregnancy and requiring enforcement from civilians rather than government officials.\par \par \uc2 ''Governor Stitt is leading us down a path where miscarriage bounty hunters could swipe a woman's private health information for a $10,000 reward, or \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is criminalized with up to 10 years in prison for physicians,'' Hofmeister said. ''This is extremism.''\par \par \uc2 The midterm math\par \par \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  of New York is the only Democratic female governor all but guaranteed to remain in office next year. Gov. \uc2 Kate Brown\uc2  of Oregon will not run again because of term limits, and the rest are likely to face respectable challengers.\par \par \uc2 The most vulnerable is undoubtedly Kelly of Kansas, who represents the most Republican-leaning state of the group.\par \par \uc2 Based on the 2020 presidential results, Whitmer should be the next most vulnerable female governor, after President Biden won the state by less than three percentage points. But Republicans have struggled to find a candidate to take on Whitmer -- and her $10 million war chest.\par \par \uc2 In Maine, Mills faces a tougher fight against a Republican former governor, Paul LePage. And in New Mexico, \uc2 Lujan Grisham\uc2  should be safe unless there's a huge Republican wave.\par \par \uc2 In several other states, women in both parties are challenging male governors. The outcomes of all these races will determine whether, in the year that a landmark ruling on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights is set to be overturned, the ranks of female governors may shrink -- or even make it to the double digits.\par \par \uc2 What to read\par \par \uc2 Federal prosecutors are said to have begun a grand jury investigation into whether classified White House documents that ended up at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's Florida home were mishandled.\par \par \uc2 The House committee investigating the Capitol riot issued subpoenas to five Republican members of Congress, including Representative Kevin McCarthy, the minority leader.\par \par \uc2 In anticipation of Roe v. Wade being overturned, California is gearing up to become the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider.\par \par \uc2 Shapiro campaign: Beware of Doug\par \par \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , the Democratic attorney general of Pennsylvania, is employing a familiar but risky tactic in that state's governor's race: He's paying for a TV ad that appears intended to help one of his opponents in the Republican primary.\par \par \uc2 The opponent, a QAnon-linked retired military officer and state senator, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , is leading the nine-person field by about 10 percentage points, according to the RealClearPolitics average of polls in the race. Mastriano's rise has alarmed many Republicans in and outside the state.\par \par \uc2 Some Shapiro advisers had been toying with the ad maneuver for months, although the leadership of the campaign did not discuss the idea until it was clear that Mastriano was ahead. Shapiro is running unopposed in the Democratic primary.\par \par \uc2 Public polls have shown Shapiro faring better in a head-to-head matchup against Mastriano than against the other two leading candidates in the Republican primary, former Representative Lou Barletta and \uc2 Bill McSwain\uc2 , a former U.S. attorney.\par \par \uc2 The ad notes that Mastriano wants to ''outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' and is ''one of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's strongest supporters.'' It continues: ''He wants to end vote by mail, and he led the fight to audit the 2020 election. If Mastriano wins, it's a win for what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  stands for.''\par \par \uc2 It ends by asking: ''Is that what we want in Pennsylvania?''\par \par \uc2 The ad went live in six media markets across the state on May 5, three days after the news emerged that the Supreme Court appeared ready to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 The 30-second spot is ambiguous enough that Republicans have wondered aloud whether the ploy was meant to help or hurt Mastriano, whose shoestring campaign is short on cash.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm going to have to send him a thank-you card,'' Mastriano has said. His campaign did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Will Bunch, a columnist for The Philadelphia Inquirer, has accused the Shapiro campaign of playing a ''dangerous game,'' arguing that Mastriano's support for conspiracy theories makes him a uniquely toxic candidate.\par \par \uc2 Shapiro's team is making no apologies. ''Both public and private polling indicates that \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  is poised to become the Republican nominee on May 17,'' said Will Simons, a campaign spokesman. ''Our campaign is prepared to start the general election now and make sure Pennsylvanians know his real record.''\par \par \uc2 Meddling in an opposing primary can backfire, said Mike Murphy, a longtime Republican strategist who opposes Trump, calling Shapiro's move ''irresponsible.''\par \par \uc2 Sam Katz, a three-time former Republican candidate for mayor of Philadelphia, ran ads attacking one of the candidates in that race's 1999 Democratic primary, hoping it would bolster his preferred opponent, John Street.\par \par \uc2 Street would go on to become the second Black mayor in the city's history, though he won the general election against Katz that year by fewer than 8,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 In an interview, Katz defended the ads bolstering Street as his least bad option, since the two other top Democrats in that contest were likely to beat him more easily.\par \par \uc2 The Mastriano ad is ''very shrewd'' and ''essential,'' said Katz, a Shapiro ally. During campaigns, he said, ''you won't get a defining moment but maybe once -- and now is that moment.''\par \par \uc2 Kirsten Noyes contributed research.\par \par \uc2 -- Leah & Blake\par \par \uc2 Is there anything you think we're missing? Anything you want to see more of? We'd love to hear from you. Email us at onpolitics@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Kirsten Noyes contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Gov. Laura Kelly of Kansas is campaigning in a state that Donald Trump won handily in 2020. (PHOTOGRAPH BY EVERT NELSON/THE TOPEKA CAPITAL-JOURNAL, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220513ei5d0004l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\png