\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 With Tense Debate, Louisiana Scraps Plan to Label  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Homicide\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rick Rojas and Tariro Mzezewa \par \uc2 1,162 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The legislation had divided \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents, many of whom objected to the idea of prosecuting women who end a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana lawmakers torpedoed legislation on Thursday that would have classified \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as homicide, overwhelmingly rejecting a proposal with far-reaching implications that included making it possible for prosecutors to bring criminal charges against women who end a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The measure was one of the most aggressive new restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  that conservative state lawmakers across the country had considered with the Supreme Court appearing poised to overturn the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But ultimately, lawmakers upheld what has long been a boundary for many in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement who have resisted seeking criminal punishment for women who obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The bill disintegrated before reaching a final vote on Thursday after an amendment essentially dismantling it was widely approved by the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana is a deeply conservative state where elected officials -- including the Democratic governor -- have backed even the most stringent restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. Yet this proposal failed with some of the most influential anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups in the state coming out in opposition, criticizing the legislation as a misguided effort saddled with a range of dangerous consequences, including potentially limiting treatment for women who have a miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 ''Pro-life bills fly off this floor with little to no opposition and they have for decades,'' Alan Seabaugh, the Republican representative behind the amendment, said during debate over the legislation that grew tense and personal. He and another critic accused the sponsor of the legislation, Danny McCormick, of failing to understand the content of the bill and the repercussions it could have.\par \par \uc2 ''This bill will not prevent a single \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from occurring -- not one,'' Mr. Seabaugh said, adding that if the bill as written were to pass, ''it will be enjoined the next day because Roe v. Wade is still the law of the land.''\par \par \uc2 ''It's not the law!'' supporters of the bill sitting in the gallery called out.\par \par \uc2 ''Liar!'' some of them shouted.\par \par \uc2 After the amendment passed, Mr. McCormick, a Republican, asked for the bill to be taken off the House calendar, preventing a final vote.\par \par \uc2 The bill advanced in the Republican-controlled House after it was passed by a committee last week with a 7-2 vote. It gained momentum after a leaked draft opinion from the Supreme Court showed that a majority of justices were in favor of overturning Roe v. Wade, the landmark 1973 ruling establishing the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Louisiana is one of roughly a dozen states with a so-called trigger law in place that would swiftly make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlawful if Roe v. Wade were reversed. But supporters of the new proposal argued that the trigger law did not go far enough and pushed for the bill defining personhood as beginning from the moment of fertilization.\par \par \uc2 ''We want equal protection for all humans, and we believe that life begins at conception,'' said Jeff Durbin, a pastor who traveled with members of his congregation from Arizona to cheer on the proposal on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The legislation prompted an immediate backlash from activists in support of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. Critics condemned it as ''reckless'' and predicted that it would have devastating consequences reaching beyond \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , arguing that it would criminalize in vitro fertilization and some forms of birth control.\par \par \uc2 Mimi Spiehler, 39, drove to the Capitol from New Orleans with two friends to speak out against the proposed bill.\par \par \uc2 ''I know how hard it is to be a mother and I wanted my kids,'' she said. ''We shouldn't be forcing women to have them.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Spiehler said she was 10 weeks pregnant once when she learned that the fetus had not survived. Her body did not recognize the pregnancy loss or expel the pregnancy tissue, however, so she needed medical intervention. ''Had this happened after this bill passed,'' she said, ''I wouldn't have had access to that necessary health care.''\par \par \uc2 Opposition over the proposal had swelled beyond the usual lines in the debate over reproduction rights, with some of the most prominent anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaders in Louisiana raising major concerns. One group, Louisiana Right to Life, said in a statement that ''our longstanding policy is that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -vulnerable women should not be treated as criminals.''\par \par \uc2 Detractors from across the political spectrum also noted that the bill would have called for judges and law enforcement officials in Louisiana to simply ignore Roe v. Wade or any other federal ruling that contradicted the legislation.\par \par \uc2 ''Your bill clearly violates the separations of powers doctrine,'' Barry Ivey, a Republican representative, said to Mr. McCormick during the floor debate, asking if he recognized that. Mr. McCormick did not directly answer him.\par \par \uc2 ''It's OK that you don't understand,'' Mr. Ivey replied. ''You wouldn't have brought this if you did.''\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 John Bel Edwards\uc2  signaled his opposition on Wednesday, calling the bill ''patently unconstitutional.''\par \par \uc2 ''To suggest that a woman would be jailed for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is simply absurd,'' Mr. Edwards said in a separate statement on Wednesday. ''This legislation is radical, and it goes far beyond simply being pro-life.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Edwards is the only Democratic governor in the Deep South. But he has a history of ardent support for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation, such as a state law that bars abortions, including in cases of rape and incest, at any time after fetal cardiac activity can be detected -- a move that angered many in his own party.\par \par \uc2 Still, prosecutions against those who obtain abortions has remained a line that many anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists were unwilling to cross. The strength of that line was underscored when Donald J. Trump, while campaigning for president, was assailed for saying that women who seek abortions should be subject to ''some form of punishment.'' He later walked back his statement. ''The woman is a victim in this case, as is the life in her womb,'' Mr. Trump said.\par \par \uc2 But supporters of the legislation in Louisiana were defiant even as their bill attracted opposition. They contended that an urgent effort to halt access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Louisiana was necessary regardless of the Supreme Court decision. Their logic: States had made other moves that contradicted federal law. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they said, should be no different.\par \par \uc2 As he introduced the legislation on Thursday, Mr. McCormick urged his colleagues to follow their moral compass. He argued that it did not interfere with access to contraception or in vitro fertilization, and that he believed that the proposal would protect women contemplating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''We are faced with a moral and ethical decision to make,'' Mr. McCormick said on the House floor. ''Politics should never decide who lives and who dies -- not in America.''\par \par \uc2 The bill spurred immediate backlash from \uc2 -rights advocates, but also from prominent anti-\uc2  leaders in the state. (PHOTOGRAPH BY REBECCA BLACKWELL/ASSOCIATED PRESS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220513ei5d0004a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
