\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Even the Demonstrations Over Roe v. Wade Are Dividing the Country\b0\par\par\uc2 By Zolan Kanno-Youngs \par \uc2 1,486 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Many of the protesters have expressed concern that the scrutiny over the demonstrations has distracted from the real issue: restricting a woman's right to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- For the protesters chanting loudly outside Justice \uc2 Brett M. Kavanaugh\uc2 's home, incivility was the point.\par \par \uc2 They said they wanted to impinge on his privacy with picket signs and chants of ''We will not go back!'' to condemn the Supreme Court justice's apparent support for ending the constitutional right to privacy that has guaranteed access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Roe v. Wade was decided nearly 50 years ago.\par \par \uc2 ''We can be noncivil,'' insisted Lacie Wooten-Holway, a 39-year-old teaching assistant who has been protesting regularly outside the home of her neighbor, Justice Kavanaugh, since October. She called it ''absolutely insane'' that the court might dictate what women do ''with the only literal home we'll have for the rest of our lives, which is our bodies.''\par \par \uc2 But the protests outside the homes of several justices, which erupted after the leak of a draft opinion indicating the court's conservative majority is ready to overturn Roe, have sparked another searing debate about appropriate forms of protest at a moment of enormous upheaval in a deeply polarized country.\par \par \uc2 Though they have been largely peaceful, the protests at the homes of Justice Kavanaugh and Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 . have drawn criticism from Republicans, who angrily accused Democrats of improperly pressuring the court. Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  said the court's conservatives were being ''bullied.'' Senator Tom Cotton, Republican of Arkansas, called for the protesters to be prosecuted criminally.\par \par \uc2 Those critiques have drawn a fierce rebuke from supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, who point to years of protests by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents in front of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and the homes of doctors. And they accuse Republicans who defended the Jan. 6 attackers at the Capitol of hypocrisy for being suddenly gripped by concern about passionate protesters.\par \par \uc2 Many of the protesters have expressed concern that the scrutiny over the protests has distracted from the real issue -- restricting a woman's right to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- that has prompted the demonstrations. The administration has expressed similar concerns.\par \par \uc2 But the debate underscores the divisions in a country that cannot even agree on how or when to protest its disagreements. And it foreshadows a potentially more confrontational period this summer if the court issues a final opinion that overturns the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The White House has tried to balance both sides of the debate.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the protests outside justices' homes last week, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , the White House press secretary, said she did not have ''an official \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  position on where people protest,'' adding that President Biden wanted ''people's privacy to be respected.''\par \par \uc2 After an outcry from critics of the protests at justices' homes, Ms. Psaki said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that while the president believed in the right to protest, ''that should never include violence, threats or vandalism.''\par \par \uc2 ''Judges perform an incredibly important function in our society, and they must be able to do their jobs without concern for their personal safety,'' she wrote.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, as tensions simmered, the Justice Department directed \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  to help ''ensure justices' safety.''\par \par \uc2 Many Democrats have shrugged off criticism that the protests are inappropriate, noting that protesters often demonstrate outside their homes as well. But Senator \uc2 Richard J. Durbin\uc2 , an Illinois Democrat who is the chairman of the Judiciary Committee, called protesting outside the homes ''reprehensible.'' And the Senate passed a bill this week to provide security for the immediate relatives of the nine justices if the Supreme Court marshal deems it necessary.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Wooten-Holway said she tried to abide by a set of rules: The protest must remain peaceful and remain on public property outside Justice Kavanaugh's home, where she said attendees bearing ponchos and signs crowded into the tree-lined street of the suburban neighborhood of Chevy Chase, Md.\par \par \uc2 In Justice Alito's neighborhood in Alexandria, Va., demonstrators flanked by police cars walked through the streets hoisting signs, including one that asked, ''Does this feel intrusive?''\par \par \uc2 But critics say the protesters should not be there at all. Some Republicans have pointed to a 1950 federal statute that says anyone ''with the intent of influencing any judge'' who ''pickets or parades in or near a building housing a court of the United States, or in or near a building or residence occupied or used by such judge,'' would be breaking the law. The Justice Department declined to comment when asked about potential prosecutions.\par \par \uc2 ''You must vigorously investigate and prosecute the crimes committed in recent days,'' Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 , Republican of Missouri, wrote in a letter to the Justice Department. ''The rule of law demands no less.''\par \par \uc2 The protests have not been limited to Washington. Over the weekend, Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 , Republican of Maine, called the police on demonstrators who used chalk on the sidewalk outside her Bangor home to write a message asking her to support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights legislation. Two churches in Colorado were vandalized last week with spray-painted messages of ''my body, my choice.''\par \par \uc2 Rebecca Overmyer-Vel\u225\'00\'E1zquez, a Whittier College professor focusing on global social movements, said history has shown that protests -- even ones that make people uncomfortable -- are at times necessary to create change. She pointed to the civil rights movement, when college students like \uc2 John Lewis\uc2 , who went on to become a congressman from Georgia, were arrested dozens of times for sitting at whites-only lunch counters and in other protests against Jim Crow-era laws in the South.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm not convinced that the line is whether it's legal or illegal,'' Ms. Overmyer-Vel\u225\'00\'E1zquez said. ''I think the question is: Is this decision really going to impact our lives very, very seriously? And it is, no doubt.''\par \par \uc2 She said the question was not whether protests were legal, but whether they were ''moral.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden has faced this kind of question before.\par \par \uc2 After demonstrations and riots erupted in the summer of 2020 following the murder of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  by a police officer, the Biden campaign repeatedly condemned violence and looting. And last year, advocates targeted two Democratic senators holding up Mr. Biden's domestic agenda -- taking kayaks to protest near a yacht belonging to Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin III\uc2  of West Virginia and following Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  of Arizona into a university restroom.\par \par \uc2 When Mr. Biden was pressed on whether those tactics were appropriate, he equivocated.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't think they're appropriate tactics, but it happens to everybody,'' Mr. Biden said at the time. ''It's a part of the process.''\par \par \uc2 Some demonstrators have pointed out the irony that some of the same Republicans who criticized the investigation into the Capitol attack have now turned attention to the law enforcement response to protests outside the homes of Supreme Court justices.\par \par \uc2 If those protesting outside the homes of justices should be prosecuted, ''then why is not every single person from Jan. 6 in jail?'' Ms. Wooten-Holway asked.\par \par \uc2 Some of those who disagree with her stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  also support the direct confrontation. Brandi Swindell, who flew to Washington from Idaho to protest outside the Capitol on Wednesday to support the repeal of Roe, said ''going to the justices' house in a peaceful way can be acceptable.''\par \par \uc2 But Michelle Peterson, a Maryland resident who went to the Capitol to support women across the country who may face restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , expressed unease about taking the protest to the home of a justice.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't know how I quite feel about it,'' she said. ''Their families are there.''\par \par \uc2 In recent days, Ms. Wooten-Holway, who said she had had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and survived sexual assault, decided to take a break from the demonstrations after anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigners gathered outside her home last weekend and her family received threatening messages.\par \par \uc2 She has since decided to hire private security. She drew a distinction between her protesting across the street from Justice Kavanaugh's home and those who gathered outside her home this weekend.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm protesting the fact Kavanaugh is trying to strip rights and they're protesting me exercising the First Amendment,'' she said. ''And I don't have a wall of security.''\par \par \uc2 Zach Montague contributed reporting. Kitty Bennett contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Zach Montague contributed reporting. Kitty Bennett contributed research.\par \par \uc2 -rights advocates in Alexandria, Va., marching to Justice Samuel A. Alito Jr.'s house on Monday. Republicans have lashed out at the protests, saying they are improperly pressuring the court.; Criticism of the protests has drawn a fierce rebuke from those who emphasize years of demonstrations outside \uc2  clinics and homes of doctors.; A candlelight vigil outside Justice Alito's home on Monday. The protests have not been limited to Washington. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY KENNY HOLSTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220513ei5d0003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
