\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Harris Emerges as the Voice of  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights in the White House\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael D. Shear and Alisha Haridasani Gupta \par \uc2 1,339 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The threat to Roe v. Wade presents Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  with an opportunity to recover from early political stumbles.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- With three words last week, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  inserted herself forcefully into the roiling debate over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights -- and may have finally seized on an issue that is popular among key Democratic voters, plays to her strengths and is central to the future of her party.\par \par \uc2 ''How dare they?'' she demanded.\par \par \uc2 Her question -- delivered more as a statement of outrage -- came in a speech to Emily's List, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group, just hours after the leak of a draft Supreme Court opinion indicating that at least five of the court's conservative justices were prepared to overturn Roe v. Wade. That would eliminate the constitutional right to privacy that has guaranteed access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for women for more than half a century.\par \par \uc2 ''How dare they tell a woman what she can and cannot do with her own body?'' Ms. Harris said at the gala in Washington, D.C. ''How dare they try to stop her from determining her own future? How dare they try to deny women their rights and their freedoms?''\par \par \uc2 Since then, Ms. Harris, the first female vice president and the former top prosecutor in California, has been among the most outspoken \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights voices in the Biden administration. In a commencement speech over the weekend, she decried living in an ''unsettled world'' where Americans are forced to defend ''the rights of women to make decisions about their own body.'' On Wednesday, she presided over the Senate as Republicans blocked a Democratic attempt to write Roe's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections into law.\par \par \uc2 And on Thursday, Ms. Harris continued to speak out against the draft opinion during a small, on-the-record discussion with reporters who cover gender and women's issues.\par \par \uc2 ''All Americans should realize that this is a direct assault on the freedom of women. And it is an attack that can affect all Americans,'' she told the reporters. ''There are some extremist Republican leaders who clearly want to punish and criminalize women. And you have to look no further than some of the laws that have already been passed to know that it's true.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris urged people to ''understand their power'' in ending the careers of politicians who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''I would urge folks to vote for pro-choice candidates at a local, state and federal level,'' she said. ''There is, at this moment, a time for education, a time for communication and mobilization.''\par \par \uc2 The threat to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights presents Ms. Harris an opportunity to recover from early political stumbles during her first year in office, including becoming entangled in two of the most difficult debates: immigration and voting rights. President Biden deputized Ms. Harris to take the lead on those subjects, both of which are mired in controversy and delay.\par \par \uc2 Now, Ms. Harris has a chance to become the voice of the administration on a subject which is complicated for her boss.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden, a lifelong Catholic, was opposed to Roe in the early days of his career and has only later come to embrace \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. But he remains an unlikely champion of the issue. He issued a forceful statement after the draft opinion was revealed by Politico last week. But up to that point he had never said the word ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' aloud as president.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, Ms. Harris has taken several opportunities to be outspoken about the prospect that the court would overturn Roe. Aides to the vice president say she intends to lean into the topic even more aggressively over the next several weeks, as the court gets closer to issuing a final ruling on the case, expected in late June.\par \par \uc2 Some women's rights organizations said the potentially historic moment could either make or break her legacy as vice president.\par \par \uc2 ''This is the type of moment that creates leaders, so in my mind I think the question every leader should be asking themselves is where did they want to be? Where were they when this happened?'' said Fatima Goss Graves, president of the \uc2 National Women's Law Center\uc2 . ''I would hope that everyone in the White House is raising their hand to be engaged.''\par \par \uc2 For others, the fact that Ms. Harris has stepped into a leadership void is an uncomfortable reminder that she doesn't have much authority to set in motion any policy proposals. As vice president, she, too, has avoided saying the word \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and has mostly toed the line of the White House and other Democratic leaders, putting the onus of action on voters, noted Renee Bracey Sherman, founder and executive director of the reproductive-justice group We Testify.\par \par \uc2 ''I wonder how much space she is actually being given to be the best \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -access champion she could be,'' Ms. Bracey Sherman added. ''Black women are asking you to do something. To then have a Black woman be the face of the lack of leadership -- that feels really frustrating.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris has a long history of focusing on issues that are of particular importance to women. She served as a district attorney in California, and later as attorney general of the state. In her brief career in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , Ms. Harris introduced legislation that focused on improving maternal health. Last fall, Ms. Harris welcomed a group of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers for a discussion at the White House.\par \par \uc2 On May 3, Ms. Harris was already scheduled to speak at the Emily's List dinner. But 24 hours earlier, Politico published the draft opinion. Aides said the tone of the speech completely changed as Ms. Harris sat with her speechwriters to rewrite her remarks.\par \par \uc2 During the course of those conversations, Ms. Harris repeatedly expressed outrage and surprise at the idea that a majority of the justices would end Roe, asking her aides: ''How could they?'' and ''How dare they?'' Her speechwriters urged her to include that phrase in her remarks to the group that night, according to aides.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the vice president's role at the Senate was purely ceremonial; nobody expected a close vote that would require her to break a tie.\par \par \uc2 But she appeared on the Senate dais in a symbolic show of support by the White House. After the vote ended, she took one question from a reporter as a cue to deliver a brief statement about the importance of the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 ''This vote clearly suggests that the Senate is not where the majority of Americans are on this issue,'' she said. ''A priority for all that care about this issue -- the priority -- should be to elect pro-choice leaders.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris ignored questions about a narrower, alternative proposal put forward by Senators \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  of Maine and Lisa Murkowski of Alaska that also seeks to codify Roe v. Wade, before departing from the Capitol in her motorcade.\par \par \uc2 Mark Buell, one of Ms. Harris's earliest fund-raisers since her first race for district attorney in San Francisco, said up until now the Biden administration had not taken full advantage of Ms. Harris's legal experience when defining her role. He said putting her at the forefront could mark a turning point for the vice president and present an opportunity for her to galvanize Mr. Biden's supporters.\par \par \uc2 ''This is a positive area she has a deep understanding of,'' Mr. Buell said. ''And the White House should take advantage of her understanding.''\par \par \uc2 Annie Karni and Zolan Kanno-Youngs contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Annie Karni and Zolan Kanno-Youngs contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 ''All Americans should realize that this is a direct assault on the freedom of women,'' Vice President Kamala Harris said about the Supreme Court's leaked draft opinion. Some see this issue as a chance for Ms. Harris to recover from early political stumbles. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SHURAN HUANG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220513ei5d0003q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
