\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 The Looming End to  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights Provides Liberal Democrats a Spark\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jonathan Weisman \par \uc2 1,547 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The progressive wing of the Democratic Party appeared to be flagging until a draft Supreme Court decision overturning Roe v. Wade leaked -- and shook the political world.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic primary in North Carolina's first congressional district had been a low-key affair, despite a new Republican-drawn map that will make the longtime stronghold for Black Democrats a key battleground in the fall.\par \par \uc2 Then the Supreme Court's draft decision that would overturn the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was leaked, thrusting a searing issue to the forefront of the contest. Now, voters in North Carolina's northeast will choose sides on Tuesday in a proxy war between Erica Smith, a progressive champion of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights with a wrenching personal story, and Donald Davis, a more conservative state senator with the backing of the establishment who has a record of votes against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''There's a political imperative for Democrats to have pro-choice nominees this cycle,'' said Ms. Smith, a pastor and former state senator who was once given a choice between ending a pregnancy or risking her own life to deliver a dangerously premature baby. She chose to give birth, only to lose the child tragically five years later, but said she would never take that choice away from a woman in her circumstances.\par \par \uc2 Around the country -- from South Texas to Chicago, Pittsburgh to New York -- the looming loss of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights has re-energized the Democratic Party's left flank, which had absorbed a series of legislative and political blows and appeared to be divided and flagging. It has also dramatized the generational and ideological divide in the Democratic Party, between a nearly extinct older wing that opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and younger progressives who support them.\par \par \uc2 President Biden and Democrats in Congress have told voters that the demise of Roe means that they must elect more ''pro-choice'' candidates, even as the party quietly backs some Democrats who are not.\par \par \uc2 The growing intensity behind the issue has put some conservative-leaning Democrats on the defensive. Representative \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  of Texas, the only House Democrat to vote against legislation to ensure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights nationwide, insisted in an ad before his May 24 runoff with Jessica Cisneros, a progressive candidate, that he ''opposes a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Candidates on the left say the potential demise of Roe shows that it's time for Democrats to fight back.\par \par \uc2 ''We need advocates. We need people who are going to work to change hearts and minds,'' said Maxwell Alejandro Frost, who, at 25 years old, is battling an established state senator 20 years his senior, Randolph Bracy, for the Orlando House seat that Representative Val Demings is leaving to run for the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Kina Collins, who is challenging longtime Representative Danny Davis of Chicago from the left, said, ''We came in saying generational change is needed,'' adding, ''We need fighters.''\par \par \uc2 But the youthful candidates of the left will have a challenge exciting voters who feel as demoralized by the Democrats' failure to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as they are angry at Republicans who engineered the gutting of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Summer Lee, a candidate for an open House seat in the Pittsburgh area, pressed the point that in states like Pennsylvania the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights will depend on governors, and ''the only way we're going to win the governor's seat in November is if, in crucial Democratic counties like this one, we put forth inspiring and reflective candidates that can expand our electorate up and down the ballot to turn out voters.''\par \par \uc2 There is little doubt that the draft Supreme Court decision that would end the 50-year-old constitutional right to control a pregnancy has presented Democrats with a political opportunity in an otherwise bleak political landscape. Republicans insist that after an initial burst of concern the midterms will revert to a referendum on the Democrats' handling of pocketbook issues like inflation and crime.\par \par \uc2 But the final high court ruling is expected in June or July, another jolt to the body politic, and regardless of how far it goes, it is likely to prompt a cascade of actions at the state level to roll back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Women would be confronted with the immediate loss of access that would ripple across the nation, said Celinda Lake, a Democratic pollster who has been studying what she calls a ''game-changing'' political event.\par \par \uc2 ''It's not going to die down,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 And while Republican consultants in Washington are telling their candidates to lay low on the issue, some of the candidates have different ideas. Three contenders for attorney general in Michigan suggested at a forum that the right to contraception established by the Supreme Court in 1965 should be decided on a state-by-state basis, assertions that Dana Nessel, Michigan's Democratic attorney general, latched onto in her re-election bid.\par \par \uc2 Yadira Caraveo, a pediatrician and Democratic state lawmaker in Colorado running for an open House seat, is already being attacked by a would-be Republican challenger, Lori Saine, who is proclaiming herself as ''strongly pro-life'' and seeking to ''confront and expose these radical pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Democrats.''\par \par \uc2 ''They've already shown they can't keep away from these issues,'' Ms. Caraveo said, adding, ''I want to focus on the issues that matter to people, like access to medical care and costs that are rising for families every day.''\par \par \uc2 For liberal candidates in primary contests, the timing of the leak is fortuitous. Their calls for a more confrontational Democratic Party are meshing with the inescapable news of the looming end to Roe v. Wade and the Democratic establishment's futile efforts to stop it.\par \par \uc2 That is especially true for women of childbearing age. This week, five Democratic candidates squared off at a debate ahead of Tuesday's primary for the House seat in Pittsburgh. Ms. Lee, the candidate aligned with the House Progressive Caucus, was the only woman on the stage. After one of her male rivals worried aloud about a post-Roe world for his daughters, she made it personal. She was the only one in the race directly impacted.\par \par \uc2 ''Your daughters, your sisters, your wives can speak for themselves,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Cisneros, the liberal insurgent in South Texas challenging the last Democratic \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights opponent in the House, Mr. Cuellar, appeared to have a steep uphill battle in March after she came in second in the initial balloting, with Mr. Cuellar's seasoned machine ready to bring out its voters for what is expected to be a low-turnout runoff on May 24.\par \par \uc2 Progressive priorities such as defunding the police and providing Medicare for all have come under deep suspicion, with even Mr. Biden casting doubts on them.\par \par \uc2 Now, Ms. Cisneros has retooled her closing argument around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Smith's story is gut-wrenching. She had two sons, aged 10 and 12, and another on the way when her doctors informed her of severe complications with her pregnancy. She could abort, or try to hold on until the fetus was closer to viability -- and risk her life.\par \par \uc2 She held on, and Rhema Elias was born at 24 weeks, a pound and a half ounce. He spent six months in the neonatal intensive care unit, and went home with lingering complications that required special feeding care and a tracheostomy. He died at age five and a half.\par \par \uc2 Now campaigning, she tells voters she would make the choice again but could not imagine a world where a woman facing the same situation would have no choice.\par \par \uc2 ''While I made that decision, I made that decision for myself,'' she said, adding, ''No police officer or court official can make a decision about life and death for a woman.''\par \par \uc2 Many voters are angry and scared at the prospect of a wave of new laws making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  illegal in a post-Roe America. The question is whether those voters will come out for Democratic candidates espousing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights or stay home, furious at Republicans but disenchanted with the ineffectual Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 Waleed Shahid, a strategist and spokesman for Justice Democrats, an insurgent liberal organization that supports progressive primary challengers, said his own parents did not bother to vote in the Virginia governor's race last year, declaring that Democratic control had changed nothing.\par \par \uc2 ''We're stuck,'' he said, ''A sense of powerlessness leads to apathy, and apathy is the Republicans' stamping grounds.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lake is more hopeful.\par \par \uc2 ''Democrats have to articulate that there is something we can do about it: Get people on record, frame out the decision in November and elect more Democrats,'' she said. ''I think that's going to energize voters.''\par \par \uc2 Jessica Cisneros, a liberal insurgent in South Texas, is challenging the last Democratic \uc2  rights opponent in the House. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ILANA PANICH-LINSMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); ''There's a political imperative for Democrats to have pro-choice nominees this cycle,'' said Erica Smith, a former state senator. (PHOTOGRAPH BY GERRY BROOME/ASSOCIATED PRESS); ''We came in saying generational change is needed,'' said Kina Collins, who is challenging Representative Danny Davis. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JEFF SCHEAR/GETTY IMAGES FOR IL GND COALITION) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220513ei5d0003k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
