\par\par \uc2 CALIFORNIA TODAY\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 California Foresees Influx Of Patients if Roe Is Barred\b0\par\par\uc2 By Soumya Karlamangla \par \uc2 1,375 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The state is preparing for an influx of patients.\par \par \uc2 In anticipation of Roe v. Wade being overturned and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  quickly becoming illegal in at least a dozen states, California is gearing up to become the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider.\par \par \uc2 About one out of six abortions in the U.S. are already performed in California, a figure that's expected to rise as access constricts elsewhere. Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  on Wednesday proposed a spending plan that includes $40 million in grants to offset the cost of abortions for uninsured women from both inside and outside the state.\par \par \uc2 ''We're going to fight like hell, making sure that all women -- not just those in California -- know that this state continues to recognize and protect their fundamental rights,'' the governor said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-eight states are expected to ban or tightly restrict access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the landmark 1973 ruling is overturned, as a leaked draft opinion by the Supreme Court indicates. Democrats in the Senate tried and failed on Wednesday to push forward legislation that would have safeguarded \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights nationwide.\par \par \uc2 So California, as the nation's most populous state and one with the fewest barriers to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is likely to see a major influx of patients if Roe falls, experts say.\par \par \uc2 Already, the number of out-of-state patients seeking abortions in California has jumped in recent months.\par \par \uc2 After Texas' rollout of the nation's strictest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, the number of women from other states showing up at Planned Parenthood clinics in Orange and San Bernardino Counties increased to 20 per month from four per month, the chapter's medical director, Dr. Janet Jacobson, said.\par \par \uc2 Last week, a 26-year-old mother from El Paso arrived at the Planned Parenthood health center in Orange for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . She had left her children with her sister, gotten in her car after work and driven through the night to reach the clinic.\par \par \uc2 ''That is a 750-mile drive -- for a five-minute procedure,'' Jacobson told me. ''Those stories are no longer unusual for us.''\par \par \uc2 Jacobson's Planned Parenthood chapter has started a program that helps defray the costs of lodging and travel for patients seeking abortions. There's a proposal in the State Legislature that would set up a similar program at the state level, part of a package of 13 bills that would make abortions easier to get in California.\par \par \uc2 If Roe were to be overturned, the number of out-of-state women whose nearest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider would be in California would jump to 1.4 million from 46,000, according to a report from The Guttmacher Institute, a research group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 That figure doesn't include women who may have clinics closer to their homes, but choose to come to California because there are fewer barriers to care here or because the clinics near them are overwhelmed. In other words, it doesn't account for the women from Texas already choosing to come to California.\par \par \uc2 ''We know that people, especially as wide swaths of the country become hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, will choose to fly rather than drive,'' Brandon Richards, spokesman for Planned Parenthood of California, said.\par \par \uc2 But there will remain people who cannot afford to travel to California or other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -friendly states.\par \par \uc2 Before the pandemic, Dr. Moira Rashad regularly traveled from the Bay Area to Phoenix to perform abortions there. She pointed out that in Arizona and elsewhere, women who seek abortions are more likely to be poor, so it's difficult for them to take time off work or afford travel to California.\par \par \uc2 ''These laws always more heavily impact marginalized communities,'' Rashad told me. ''It's something we've kind of been anticipating would happen since this case got to the Supreme Court, and it's still so devastating.''\par \par \uc2 For more:\par \par \uc2 See where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be banned without Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Do Americans support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights?\par \par \uc2 We know that Texan patients are coming to California for abortions. But when I was a reporter at The Los Angeles Times, I wrote about California doctors who go to Texas to do abortions.\par \par \uc2 If you read one story, make it this\par \par \uc2 Mayor Eric Garcetti's path to ambassadorship constricts.\par \par \uc2 The rest of the news\par \par \uc2 Second Amendment: A U.S. appeals court ruled on Wednesday that California's ban on sales of semiautomatic weapons to adults under 21 is unconstitutional, The Associated Press reports.\par \par \uc2 Reparations for Native Americans: A new fund will help Native American communities preserve tribal history and further California's effort to atone for its history of violence and wrongdoing against Native Americans, CalMatters reports.\par \par \uc2 Orange County wildfire: The Coastal fire led to mandatory evacuations as it burned about 200 acres and at least 20 homes in a gated community in Laguna Niguel on Wednesday night, The Los Angeles Times reports.\par \par \uc2 Immigration help: The L.A. City Council has approved a program providing universal free legal representation to immigrants facing deportation, LAist reports.\par \par \uc2 L.A.'s new watering rules: Los Angeles is reducing outdoor watering to two days per week from three, LAist reports.\par \par \uc2 L.A.U.S.D. vaccine mandate on hold: The \uc2 Los Angeles Unified School District\uc2  postponed its Covid-19 vaccination mandate from this fall to next year, The Associated Press reports.\par \par \uc2 Hearst Castle: After two years of being closed to the public, Hearst Castle began welcoming back guests on Wednesday, KTLA reports.\par \par \uc2 Health care for the undocumented: Contra Costa County is expanding health coverage to those living in the country illegally, The East Bay Times reports.\par \par \uc2 Baristas unionize: Employees at two \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores in Santa Cruz voted to unionize, a first in the state, The San Francisco Chronicle reports.\par \par \uc2 What we're eating\par \par \uc2 Lemony pasta with asparagus and white beans.\par \par \uc2 Where we're traveling\par \par \uc2 Today's tip comes from Cassandra Franklin, who recommends ''a small treasure of a museum -- Pasadena's Norton Simon'':\par \par \uc2 ''On your way into the building, don't rush past the impressive Rodin bronzes (including The Thinker around the corner from the entry path). Their careful placement invites lingering exploration of their full 360 degree magnificence.\par \par \uc2 Once inside, strolling through the galleries on first floor is like taking a Western art history class. From early Medieval paintings to gems from both the Flemish and Italian Renaissance to stellar later works from the Impressionists and early 20th century. The lower level galleries contain similarly outstanding works from Asia.\par \par \uc2 When the outdoors calls again, take in the serene greenery, pond, still more sculptures in the back garden, and, perhaps, a tasty snack or beverage. You won't regret the 'off the beaten path' trek to enjoy this visual feast.''\par \par \uc2 Tell us about your favorite places to visit in California. Email your suggestions to CAtoday@nytimes.com. We'll be sharing more in upcoming editions of the newsletter.\par \par \uc2 And before you go, some good news\par \par \uc2 In 2019, Rebecca Longworth and Joan Howard were having dinner with some friends at their home in Berkeley.\par \par \uc2 During the meal, their guests told them something surreal: They'd just been to the Oakland Museum, where they'd spotted a large image of Longworth and Howard's bright blue building.\par \par \uc2 The couple were stunned to hear about their friends' discovery. They took a trip to the museum themselves and learned their house used to be a lesbian bar that had been open for decades.\par \par \uc2 For the queer couple, who run a theater company out of their home, the revelation felt like ''an unexpected gift,'' Howard told Berkeleyside.\par \par \uc2 Longworth added: ''It feels like we're part of its history now, and maybe we're supposed to be part of its history.''\par \par \uc2 Thanks for reading. I'll be back tomorrow. -- Soumya\par \par \uc2 P.S. Here's today's Mini Crossword , and a clue: Motorized two-wheel transport (5 letters).\par \par \uc2 Shivani Gonzalez, Danielle Cruz, Briana Scalia and Mariel Wamsley contributed to California Today. You can reach the team at CAtoday@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Sign up here to get this newsletter in your inbox .\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood workers in California showed support for women's health rights protesters during a march and rally. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CAROLINE BREHMAN/EPA, VIA SHUTTERSTOCK) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220513ei5d0003f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
