\par\par \uc2 CRITIC'S NOTEBOOK\par \uc2 The Arts/Cultural Desk; SECTC\par \b \uc2 In Defense Of Liberals, Yet Again\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jennifer Szalai \par \uc2 1,470 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Francis Fukuyama\uc2 's new book joins several from recent years in defining liberalism and arguing for its continued relevance.\par \par \uc2 Last week, I had the surreal experience of reading my way through a stack of high-toned books about liberalism when news trickled out that a conservative majority on the Supreme Court was preparing to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Legal scholars were swift to denounce what they characterized as an illiberal intervention by the judiciary. \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , who served as the acting solicitor general in the Obama administration, warned that the leaked opinion ''will set women back in profound ways,'' and he pleaded with Congress to ''act ASAP.'' Five years before, Katyal had written an Op-Ed for The Times exhorting his fellow liberals to support Donald Trump's nomination of \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  to the Supreme Court -- the same \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  who is among the conservative justices set to act on what Katyal called the ''hard-line position'' to overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 ''I have no doubt that if confirmed, Judge Gorsuch would help to restore confidence in the rule of law,'' Katyal wrote in 2017, in a sentence that reads like a distillation of the liberal temperament if there ever was one. Notice the reverence for institutions, the faith in compromise, the tone of serene assurance (''no doubt''). The leaked opinion serves as a stark rebuke that whatever consensus certain liberals wanted to presume -- whether couched in terms of popular opinion or ''settled law'' -- was in fact more vulnerable than they believed.\par \par \uc2 I'm using the word ''liberalism'' here the way that most of the actual literature uses it -- not as a right-wing epithet for a ''radical leftist agenda,'' but as a political approach that emphasizes individualism, egalitarianism and pluralism. The question of whether these values are better protected when government intervenes or when it gets out of the way is one of liberalism's (many) tensions.\par \par \uc2 The definition of liberalism itself is capacious, and therefore slippery. A few months after Trump's inauguration, Nikil Saval observed in a sharp essay that the word ''liberal'' had become ''almost entirely one of abuse,'' not just for the right but for critics on the left, who deemed liberals too centrist, too smug, too complacent -- so convinced by their own bien-pensant reasonableness that they could no longer think substantively about what they stood for. Many Democrats preferred the term ''progressive.'' At a moment of anti-establishment rage, liberalism was associated with a status quo that nobody seemed to want. ''Here is an ideology that has many accused sympathizers,'' Saval wrote in 2017, ''but no champions, no defenders.''\par \par \uc2 Or so it seemed. A number of writers soon took it upon themselves to defend liberalism's honor, often recounting their own stunned incredulity that the kind of politics they had taken for granted was now under siege. Adam Gopnik's ''A Thousand Small Sanities'' (2019) opens with him trying to reassure his teenage daughter on the night of the November 2016 election, stumbling and seeking words he couldn't find in that ''startling moment.'' In ''What Was Liberalism?'' (2019), the journalist James Traub describes Trump's election and the rise of illiberalism as ''the greatest shock of my political life.''\par \par \uc2 Francis Fukuyama\uc2  is more circumspect in his new book, ''Liberalism and Its Discontents'' -- which makes sense, considering that unlike pugnacious liberal writers like Traub and George Packer (whose ''Last Best Hope'' was published a year ago), Fukuyama concludes that liberal values can only survive through an ''acceptance of limits'' and a ''sense of moderation.'' If there's something wan and deflating about this, that seems to be part of Fukuyama's point. Liberalism, he contends, has become a victim of its own success, pushed to neoliberal extremes in the realm of economics and identitarian extremes in the realm of politics. Unlike the lofty language adopted by some of liberalism's other defenders (Gopnik calls the liberal approach a ''moral adventure''), Fukuyama is partial to matter-of-fact formulations, like ''liberalism is rooted in law'' and ''the basic doctrine is correct.''\par \par \uc2 Yes, a number of these writers admit, the historical practice of liberalism has often failed miserably to live up to its ideals. Imperialism and chattel slavery were not only passively tolerated by liberal regimes but actively pursued by them. As one of liberalism's canonical philosophers, John Stuart Mill also invoked the necessity of ''paternal despotism'' in Britain's colonies because ''a people of savages should be taught obedience.'' Fukuyama describes how the ''basic liberal principle'' of ''personal autonomy'' was originally restricted to white men (and, at first, property holders to boot). Gopnik gives the examples of Belgium's genocide in the Congo and the Cold War exploits of the United States in Latin America, where ''we were exporting not liberal democracy but brutal military rule.''\par \par \uc2 Liberalism, then, has been spectacularly hypocritical -- though Fukuyama, for one, is unimpressed with the charge, arguing that this leftist critique ''fails to show how the doctrine is wrong in its essence.'' The historian Caroline Elkins might beg to disagree. In ''Legacy of Violence,'' her recent book about the British Empire, she argues that ''ideological elasticity'' was in fact what made liberal imperialism so resilient. She shows how Britain's vast apparatus of laws was used to legitimize the violence of its ''civilizing mission.'' What Fukuyama repeatedly refers to as liberalism's ''essence'' has also, Elkins suggests, amounted to a paradox: emancipation and oppression, all rolled in one.\par \par \uc2 But such tensions are less interesting to liberalism's conservative critics, who think that it's rotten all the way down. As Matthew Rose puts it in ''A World After Liberalism,'' the radical right has long deemed it ''evil in principle because it destroys the foundations of social order.'' The 20th-century extremist thinkers he discusses in his book -- among them a ''fascist savant'' and a ''right-wing Marxist'' -- derided Christianity, too, for an egalitarianism and compassion that they just couldn't abide. Still, their critiques have found echoes in contemporary arguments by right-wing Christians like Sohrab Ahmari and Patrick Deneen, who blame liberalism for making people comfort-seeking and spiritually lazy.\par \par \uc2 Liberal decadence doesn't amount just to temptation but to tyranny -- or so you might believe when reading liberalism's most vociferous detractors on the right, whose sweeping denunciations can make it sound as if there's a liberal regime coercing women into pursuing careers and forcing them to get abortions. It's notable how little liberalism's book-length defenders have to say about sexual and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while conservative critics have long been fixated on them. Gopnik did warn that if the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement truly meant business, it would have to create some sort of invasive ''pregnancy police force.'' He didn't foresee that Texas would soon figure out a way to do something even more extreme by putting that power in the hands of civilians -- a vigilante-enforced ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on the cheap.\par \par \uc2 There's an old essay by the feminist cultural critic Ellen Willis in which she said that ''sophisticated liberals'' seemed so ''emotionally intimidated'' by the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement that they didn't quite know how to talk about it: ''Nearly everyone I know supports legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in principle, but hardly anyone takes the issue seriously.'' Willis wrote this in 1980, calling the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement ''the most dangerous political force in the country,'' one that posed a threat not only to sexual freedom and privacy but also to physical autonomy and ''civil liberties in general.''\par \par \uc2 Willis pointed to liberalism's weaknesses while also identifying the room it had opened up for liberation. She had gotten her start as a rock critic, a woman in a male-dominated field, ever aware of the possibilities and limitations afforded by the mainstream culture. The late philosopher Charles Mills was similarly attuned to such discrepancies. In books like ''The Racial Contract'' and ''Black Rights/White Wrongs,'' he offered scathing critiques of a ''racialized liberalism'' that kept trying to pretend it was colorblind; Mills argued that liberalism's exclusions were historically so vast that they weren't mere anomalies but clearly fundamental to it.\par \par \uc2 Still, as he told The Nation in early 2021, ''liberalism is attractive on both principled and strategic grounds.'' Mills envisioned a liberalism that was tougher and more radical, yet imbued with some necessary humility -- a sense of how contingent it was. It was precisely the experience of subordination and exclusion that made him alert to what many liberals didn't want to see. He ended an essay for Artforum in 2018 with a warning: ''As the anti-Enlightenment bears down on us, threatening a new Dark Age, just remember: We told you so (and long ago, too).''\par \par \uc2 (C7) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220512ei5c0004m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
