\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 In Alito's Draft Opinion, Some Assertions Lacked Context or Counterarguments\b0\par\par\uc2 By Linda Qiu \par \uc2 1,219 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In the leaked draft, Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  made assertions about fetal development, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures and international laws that were disputed or omitted context.\par \par \uc2 The leaked draft opinion suggesting the Supreme Court is poised to overturn Roe v. Wade cited claims frequently made by opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The opinion, written by Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 ., at times presents those assertions as indisputable facts while omitting context and counterarguments.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court confirmed last week that the draft was authentic but cautioned that it was not final. According to Politico, which first published the draft, no other versions have been circulated inside the court.\par \par \uc2 In the nearly 100-page decision, Justice Alito made or quoted assertions about fetal development, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures and international laws that have been disputed or are open to interpretation.\par \par \uc2 Here is a fact check.\par \par \uc2 ''To support this act, the legislature made a series of factual findings. It began by noting that, at the time of enactment, only six countries besides the United States 'permit[ted] nontherapeutic or elective \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on demand after the 20th week of gestation.'''\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito referred to what he called the ''factual findings'' of the Mississippi Legislature that passed the 2018 law challenging Roe. The law makes most abortions illegal after 15 weeks of pregnancy, about two months earlier than Roe.\par \par \uc2 Those six countries, Justice Alito wrote, were Canada, China, the Netherlands, North Korea, Singapore and Vietnam. He added that Iceland and Guinea-Bissau were similarly permissive.\par \par \uc2 This is accurate, based on the letter of international laws. But comparing gestational limits alone does not present a full picture of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in different countries. As The Upshot reported, several countries that specify gestational limits also provide broad exceptions. In Britain, for example, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  must be approved by two doctors, but those requests are generally granted. In Denmark and Germany, exceptions for a gestational limit of 12 weeks are made for mental and physical health as well as for life circumstances.\par \par \uc2 Similarly, in New Zealand, a 2020 law allowed for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to 20 weeks, and beyond if a medical practitioner deemed it appropriate and considered the woman's physical and mental health. Opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights criticized the law as overly permissive and vague.\par \par \uc2 A pair of amici briefs illustrates how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws can be cast to make different comparisons.\par \par \uc2 In a brief submitted on behalf of Mississippi, a European Christian group claimed the majority of European countries allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the gestational limit ''only under exceptional circumstances'' like rape or incest, fetal abnormalities or danger to the mother. (Mississippi's law does not include exceptions for rape or incest.)\par \par \uc2 By contrast, another brief was filed by European law scholars on behalf of the Jackson Women's Health Organization, which as the only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in Mississippi is at the center of the case. It asserted that 37 European countries allowed for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least until 22 weeks either upon request, on broad socioeconomic grounds or based on the health of the mother that does not entail a risk to her life.\par \par \uc2 ''The legislature then found that at five or six weeks' gestational age an 'unborn human being's heart begins beating.'''\par \par \uc2 Whether the sound described is a ''heartbeat'' is a matter of dispute, as \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  has previously reported. At six weeks, cells in the embryo begin to form a hollow tube that will develop into a heart, and they emit electrical pulses that a machine translates into a sound.\par \par \uc2 To many medical experts, this is not the same as a heart beating -- defined as when heart valves open and close to pump blood -- because the tube in a six-week-old embryo does not yet have valves. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents contend that a heart tube is still a heart, and many doctors and medical practitioners use the word ''heartbeat'' to describe the sound.\par \par \uc2 ''It found that most abortions after 15 weeks employ 'dilation and evacuation procedures, which involve the use of surgical instruments to crush and tear the unborn child,' and it concluded that the 'intentional commitment of such acts for nontherapeutic or elective reasons is a barbaric practice, dangerous for the maternal patient and demeaning to the medical profession.'''\par \par \uc2 Dilation and evacuation is the most common procedure used in second-trimester abortions, as Justice Alito and the Mississippi Legislature correctly note. But contrary to their warnings about its danger to maternal health, the procedure is generally considered the safest for most women in that stage of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The procedure involves dilating the cervix, then removing the fetus in parts. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents and several Republican states that have passed laws banning the practice and have called it ''dismemberment \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' Supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights say such language is inflammatory and medically inaccurate.\par \par \uc2 According to a 2018 report by the \uc2 National Academies of Sciences, Engineering and Medicine\uc2 , dilation and extraction is ''effective with minimal rates of complications, ranging from 0.05 to 4 percent.'' The \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2  has said the procedure ''results in fewer medical complications than other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures, and often is necessary to preserve a woman's health or her future fertility.''\par \par \uc2 ''They note that attitudes about the pregnancy of unmarried woman have changed drastically; that federal and state laws ban discrimination on the basis of pregnancy; that leave for pregnancy and childbirth are now guaranteed by law in many cases; that the costs of medical care associated with pregnancy are covered by insurance or government assistance.''\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito summarized and provided citations to several claims he characterized as policy arguments made by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents.\par \par \uc2 For the claim about guaranteed leave, he pointed to a federal law requiring businesses with 50 employees or more to provide up to 12 weeks of unpaid family and medical leave, as well as data from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  showing that nearly 90 percent of workers had access to unpaid leave. The same data shows, though, that less than a quarter of workers had access to paid family leave in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito also cited Affordable Care Act provisions that required insurers to cover maternity and newborn care as an essential health benefit. But recent studies have found that out-of-pocket costs for maternity care still average more than $3,000 for women with employer-sponsored insurance, and 95 percent of births covered by insurance require some out-of-pocket costs. There is no cost sharing for pregnancy-related care for Medicaid recipients. But Mississippi is one of 12 states that have not yet expanded Medicaid eligibility, leaving tens of thousands of women without the coverage he detailed.\par \par \uc2 International comparisons, such as the ones Justice Alito made on gestational limits for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , were also omitted in this discussion about protections and benefits for pregnant women. The United States is the only country without a paid family leave program among the 38 members of the \uc2 Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development\uc2 , which provided an average of 50 weeks of paid leave in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Justice Samuel A. Alito Jr. wrote the draft of the court's majority opinion that suggested Roe v. Wade is poised to be overturned. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Erin Schaff/The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220512ei5c00041\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
