\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Bill to Preserve  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Right Fails in Senate\b0\par\par\uc2 By Annie Karni \par \uc2 1,611 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Republicans used a filibuster to thwart the bill, but Democrats hoped the high-profile failure of their legislation would help them at the polls in November.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Democrats tried and failed on Wednesday to push forward legislation to guarantee \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights nationwide, as Republicans and one Democrat in the Senate blocked an effort to enshrine the landmark Roe v. Wade precedent in federal law.\par \par \uc2 With 51 senators opposed and 49 in support, Democrats fell short of the 60 votes they would have needed to take up sweeping legislation to ensure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and explicitly bar a wide array of restrictions.\par \par \uc2 The action came after a leaked Supreme Court draft opinion thrust the issue into the political spotlight, suggesting that the court may be on the brink of overturning the nearly 50-year-old ruling that legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and leaving states to decide whether women would have the right to terminate their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Republicans, who unanimously opposed the measure, were joined by one Democrat, Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin III\uc2  of West Virginia. Mr. Manchin, who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, said the legislation was overly broad, noting that it would go substantially further than simply codifying Roe and warning that it would ''expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 The resulting vote showed that a majority does not now exist in the Senate to support maintaining legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide. Democrats who supported the bill framed it as a call to action, ahead of midterm elections, for voters who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to elect likeminded candidates who will preserve them.\par \par \uc2 ''This vote clearly suggests that the Senate is not where the majority of Americans are on this issue,'' said Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who appeared on the Senate dais to open the vote in a symbolic show of support by the White House. ''A priority for all that care about this issue -- the priority -- should be to elect pro-choice leaders.''\par \par \uc2 The outcome of the vote was never in doubt, given the 50-to-50 split in the Senate and the deep partisan divide over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. But Democrats pressed ahead anyway, hoping that the vote would reinforce their political message and further their efforts to portray Republicans as extremists.\par \par \uc2 Still, Democrats' failure to advance the bill capped a calculated and yearslong Republican effort, across all levels of government, to chip away at \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, by electing lawmakers who oppose them, installing judges at the state and federal levels who are hostile to them and pressing forward with legislation in states around the nation to strictly limit them and test the boundaries of Roe.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, by contrast, appeared to have little in the way of a plan for what would come next now that their legislative path to preserve \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights is effectively closed off, except to frame the stakes for voters who they hoped would be moved to punish Republicans.\par \par \uc2 ''Elect more pro-choice Democrats if you want to protect a woman's freedom and right to choose,'' Senator \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , Democrat of New York and the majority leader, said after the vote. ''Elect more MAGA Republicans if you want to see a nationwide ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if you want to see doctors and women arrested, if you want to see no exceptions for rape or incest.''\par \par \uc2 Republicans, wary of a backlash by voters who might be alienated by their opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, sought to portray Democrats as the extremists, noting the expansive nature of their bill and saying it went far beyond what most Americans wanted.\par \par \uc2 ''Today, Democrats have decided to line up behind an extreme and radical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy,'' said Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , Republican of Kentucky and the minority leader. The legislation, he said, ''goes way, way beyond codifying the status quo; it would roll back many existing laws.''\par \par \uc2 He said the legislation would allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on demand at any stage of pregnancy, calling it ''as extreme as extreme gets.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Schumer and other Democrats were making a bet that they had public opinion on their side. Recent polls show that most Americans oppose overturning Roe v. Wade. A \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  survey in March found that 61 percent of Americans believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases; 37 percent say the opposite.\par \par \uc2 But the issue carries political risks for both parties. Surveys also reflect broad support for at least some curbs on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- including limits on late-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , prohibitions on certain procedures, and even waiting periods and other barriers -- and the issue has traditionally been a bigger motivator on the right than on the left.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters have expressed frustration that Democrats have not devoted more resources to flipping state legislatures controlled by Republicans, which have become successful engines of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation. Many states have passed legislation that chips away at the protections of Roe. States like Texas have passed laws that serve as de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 The legislation Democrats tried to advance on Wednesday, called the Women's Health Protection Act, would protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access nationwide, going beyond simply codifying Roe. It would explicitly prohibit a long list of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, including some that have been enacted by states since Roe was decided in 1973 and that have severely limited access to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Democrats conceded that their bill was more expansive than Roe, saying that they had drafted the measure in line with their view that some of the statewide restrictions that have been put in place over the past decade are inconsistent with that precedent.\par \par \uc2 That position cost them support from Mr. Manchin and two Republicans who back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights but said they wanted merely to preserve the status quo -- not roll back existing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Senators \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 , Republican of Maine, and Lisa Murkowski, Republican of Alaska, had proposed a narrower bill that they said would codify Roe by outlawing any limit that would put an ''undue burden'' on a woman's right to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It borrowed language from the 1992 decision in Planned Parenthood vs. Casey, which affirmed what it called the essential holding in Roe: that states may not prohibit abortions before fetal viability.\par \par \uc2 Democrats dismissed the proposal as toothless, noting that it lacked clear guidance about what states can and cannot do, and that it would not explicitly rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans before a fetus is viable or bar any specific prohibitions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  methods.\par \par \uc2 Yet Ms. Collins called the Democratic bill a nonstarter, saying it was championed by ''far-left activists.''\par \par \uc2 The debate grew passionate as lawmakers in both parties contemplated the demise of a right that has existed for nearly a half-century, and an issue that is among the most divisive in American society.\par \par \uc2 ''Here we are today, a body of 100 -- 76 percent of which are male -- making decisions about the private lives of the nearly 168 million women in this country,'' said Senator \uc2 Patrick J. Leahy\uc2 , Democrat of Vermont. ''That's ludicrous.''\par \par \uc2 In a show of support that also underscored the lack of legislative options, a group of House Democrats marched across the Capitol before the vote, chanting, ''My body, my decision.''\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2 , Democrat of Washington, reflected on how she had been motivated to run for the Senate by anger at watching the way an all-male \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  grilled \uc2 Anita Hill\uc2  in 1991 during confirmation hearings for Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , whom Ms. Hill had accused of sexual harassment.\par \par \uc2 ''Here's my message to Republicans,'' Ms. Murray said. ''Women will not forget your cruelty.''\par \par \uc2 Republicans also made emotional pleas as they made the case against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Ben Sasse\uc2 , Republican of Nebraska, denounced the vote as ''gross,'' calling it a ploy by Democrats to win praise from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups. ''There is no moderation in this bill,'' he asserted.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 James Lankford\uc2 , Republican of Oklahoma, brought an oversize photograph of a baby girl to the Senate floor and implored his colleagues to keep her in mind as they decided on the bill.\par \par \uc2 ''She's in this conversation -- her future, her opportunities,'' Mr. Lankford said. ''For that, I'm being called a radical extremist, because I believe she's valuable.''\par \par \uc2 Even in defeat, Democrats ended the day with the headline they wanted voters to read: that Republicans had blocked their effort to preserve \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Schumer was clear that short of passing legislation, he wanted to show Americans ''where every single U.S. senator stands'' on the issue. He also warned on Wednesday that if Republicans won control of the Senate in November, they would outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide.\par \par \uc2 ''A national ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the extreme of the extremes, and it is now possible in a Republican Senate,'' he said, noting that Mr. McConnell had said as much last week.\par \par \uc2 After the vote, President Biden criticized Republicans, saying in a statement that their opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights ''runs counter to the will of the majority of the American people.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden urged voters to elect more senators who support the legislation.\par \par \uc2 ''If they do,'' he said, ''Congress can pass this bill in January, and put it on my desk, so I can sign it into law.''\par \par \uc2 Some felt the legislation went beyond codifying Roe v. Wade. (PHOTOGRAPH BY PETE MAROVICH FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A1); Senator Chuck Schumer warned that if Republicans won control of the Senate in the fall, they would outlaw \uc2  nationwide. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SHURAN HUANG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Senator Mitch McConnell said the legislation ''goes way, way beyond codifying the status quo; it would roll back many existing laws.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY MICHAEL A. MCCOY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A16) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220512ei5c0003y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
