\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Undoing Roe Will Disrupt More Than  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 . I'm OK With That.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matthew Walther \par \uc2 1,201 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 27\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 For years I have noticed a persistent inclination among my fellow opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Perhaps without meaning to do so, they downplay the possibility that the end of legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will entail anything except the eradication of a great evil.\par \par \uc2 The post-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  world they seem to envision outwardly resembles the one that we already inhabit, with its familiar social and economic structures intact. They are reluctant to consider that they themselves might have benefited from what those on the other side of the issue would hail as the societywide benefits of decades of legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- allowing more women to engage in wage labor, for example, or all but eliminating the social pressures that once led to shotgun marriages.\par \par \uc2 This has always seemed to me a great failure of imagination. That failure must now be rectified. With the appearance last week of a draft of what seems to be the forthcoming majority opinion in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, a decision that would overturn Roe v. Wade, Americans are being invited to consider what it would mean to live in a country in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though not illegal everywhere, is likely to be banned or heavily restricted in as many as 28 states.\par \par \uc2 For opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it is worth asking what might follow from overturning Roe -- apart from the joyful fact of hundreds of thousands of additional babies being born -- and how we should reckon with the arrival of any apparently unwelcome social or economic consequences.\par \par \uc2 American society as we know it today came into being during the 1970s, in the wake of Roe, amid rising crime rates, stagflation and the dismantling of the Fordist economic consensus in favor of deregulation and the breakup of stable, quasi-nationalized monopolies. It is not possible to conceive of our present way of life -- the decline of heavy and textile manufacturing and the rise of the service economy, financialization, the collapse of traditional familial and other social structures, the subsuming of virtually every facet of our existence into digital technologies -- in the absence of the estimated 63 million abortions that have been performed in America since 1973.\par \par \uc2 Exactly how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has affected the evolution of American society is a matter of some debate. Consider the economic implications. Twenty years ago, John D. Mueller argued in the pages of Family Policy, a conservative journal, that Roe was ''perhaps the single largest American economic event of the past century, more significant than the Great Depression or the Second World War.'' By reducing the size of the American population, he argued, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  threatened both economic growth and innovation. Legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said, had led to a reduction in living standards and trillions of dollars in lost net output.\par \par \uc2 In the years since the publication of Mr. Mueller's essay, countless economists and social scientists have argued the opposite: that legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not only compatible with but also necessary for sustained economic growth. Among other things, reduced access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is correlated with lower rates of labor force participation, reduced wages and increased job turnover.\par \par \uc2 If the actions of major corporations in states such as Texas, which recently banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after about six weeks of pregnancy, are any indication, America's business establishment agrees. The boards of corporations like \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , with a fiduciary duty to their shareholders, have announced that they will subsidize travel for employees who seek out-of-state abortions.\par \par \uc2 Opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should consider the possibility that these corporations are correct in their apparent assumption that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  contributes to the maximization of shareholder value. Are we prepared to accept the converse proposition, to invite a reduction in shareholder value by banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 The scope of the problem is far broader than economics. Research over the years has suggested that an America without \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would mean more single mothers and more births to teenage mothers, increased strain on Medicaid and other welfare programs, higher crime rates, a less dynamic and flexible work force, an uptick in carbon emissions, lower student test scores and goodness knows what else. If you sincerely believe, as I do, that every \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  means the deliberate killing of an innocent human being, is there some hypothetical threshold for negative growth, carbon dioxide levels or work force participation rates beyond which the protection of that life would be too burdensome?\par \par \uc2 For me, the answer is no. This is why, even though I find it unlikely that (for example) there is any meaningful causal relationship between access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and academic performance, I believe that those who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should not discount the possibility that its proscription will have consequences that some of us would otherwise regret. To insist, as opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  often have, that the economists John Donohue and Steven Levitt cannot be right about the correlation between Roe and the reduced incidence of crime two decades later strikes me as a tacit concession that if they were right, our position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have to be altered.\par \par \uc2 For the same reason, opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should commit ourselves to the most generous and humane provisions for mothers and children (paid family leave, generous child benefits, direct income subsidies for stay-at-home mothers, single-payer health care) without being Pollyannaish. No matter what we do, in a post-Roe world many children who would not otherwise have been born will live lives of utter misery, and many of our fellow Americans will be indifferent to their plight. If we wish to dispel the noxious argument that only happy lives are worth saving, we will have to be honest about the limits of social policy and private charity in regulating the turbid ebb and flow of human misery.\par \par \uc2 In a letter written to a friend in 1959, Flannery O'Connor lamented that some members of the clergy, when arguing in favor of Catholic teaching on procreation, felt the need to assuage concerns about overpopulation. ''I wish various fathers would quit trying to defend it by saying that the world can support 40 billion,'' she wrote. ''I will rejoice in the day when they say: This is right, whether we all rot on top of each other or not, dear children, as we certainly may.''\par \par \uc2 While the neo-Malthusian fears that animated many observers in the second half of the 20th century appear now to have been ill founded, one should see the wisdom in O'Connor's blithe acknowledgment that things might have turned out otherwise. What is right is very rarely what is convenient.\par \par \uc2 Matthew Walther (@matthewwalther) is the editor of The Lamp, a Catholic literary journal. He is writing a biography of John Henry Newman.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY Illustration by The New York Times; Photograph by mrs, via Getty FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220512ei5c00039\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
