\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Economy; Yellen Says  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban Would Take Financial Toll\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alan Rappeport \par \uc2 373 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Restrictions would damage their economic prospects, the nation's first female treasury secretary said in a Senate hearing.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet L. Yellen\uc2  said on Tuesday that the loss of access to legal abortions would damage the United States economy and deal a blow to the financial well-being of women.\par \par \uc2 ''I believe that eliminating the rights of women to make decisions about when and whether to have children would have very damaging effects on the economy and would set women back decades,'' Ms. Yellen said during a hearing before the Senate Banking Committee.\par \par \uc2 A leaked draft decision indicated that the Supreme Court was poised to overturn Roe v. Wade, the landmark 1973 case that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , raising concerns that access to abortions will be curtailed around much of the country.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Yellen, who is the first female Treasury secretary, said that such a move would represent a major economic setback for women. She said that legalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped lead to increased labor force participation among women, allowing more to finish school and increase their earning potential, and she pointed to economic research that supports that argument.\par \par \uc2 ''It makes clear that denying women access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  increases their odds of living in poverty or need for public assistance,'' Ms. Yellen said of the research.\par \par \uc2 Senator Tim Scott, Republican of South Carolina, called Ms. Yellen's argument ''harsh'' and ''piercing,'' suggesting that it was inappropriate to frame the debate over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in economic terms. He said that he was grateful to have been raised by a single mother who kept their family out of poverty.\par \par \uc2 In a tense exchange, Ms. Yellen pushed back and made the case that it is important for women to be able to have children when they have the financial resources to take care of them.\par \par \uc2 ''This is not harsh, this is the truth,'' Ms. Yellen said.\par \par \uc2 Treasury Secretary Janet L. Yellen testified Tuesday that research ''makes clear that denying women access to \uc2  increases their odds of living in poverty or need for public assistance.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY Pool photo by Tom Williams FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220511ei5b00047\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
