\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Why Democrats Are Advancing a Bill That's All but Certain to Fail\b0\par\par\uc2 By Annie Karni \par \uc2 1,403 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Under legislation that is all but certain to fail on a test vote this week, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights would be protected and a wide array of limits and bans would be barred.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Acting swiftly after a leaked Supreme Court opinion that would overturn Roe v. Wade, Senator \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , Democrat of New York and the majority leader, has set a vote for Wednesday to take up a bill that would codify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in federal law.\par \par \uc2 The bill, if passed, would protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access nationwide at a moment when it is under imminent threat, with multiple states enacting or proposing strict limits or outright prohibitions. But the legislation is all but certain to be blocked in the Senate on Wednesday, when Democrats fall short of the 60 votes they would need to break a Republican filibuster.\par \par \uc2 Still, Democrats see a political opportunity before the midterm elections in forcing Republicans to go on the record against the measure when polls show that most voters favor at least some legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They plan to use the defeat as a chance to make the case to voters that Republicans are extremists, and the only way to safeguard \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other vital protections is to elect more Democrats.\par \par \uc2 The measure does substantially more than simply codify Roe: It would invalidate a variety of restrictions to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access that have been enacted by states since the precedent was established in 1973.\par \par \uc2 Some Republicans who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have proposed an alternative, which they say would enshrine those rights in federal law. But their measure lacks specifics and any enforcement.\par \par \uc2 Here is a closer look at the Democrats' bill, the Republican proposal and the issues driving the debate.\par \par \uc2 Roe v. Wade established the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The landmark Supreme Court decision legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the country, striking down laws in many states that had previously barred the practice. It held that laws banning the procedure before fetal viability -- that is, when a fetus could survive outside the womb -- were unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 Today, that is at about 24 weeks of pregnancy. The draft Supreme Court opinion leaked last week would strike down the decision, moving the issue out of the courts and back into the hands of elected officials nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood v. Casey, another monumental case for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  that was decided in 1992, affirmed what it called the essential holding in Roe: that states may not prohibit abortions before fetal viability. It said that states could not impose an ''undue burden'' on the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Democrats' bill would invalidate a wide variety of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 The legislation that Democrats are trying to bring up this week, the Women's Health Protection Act, would explicitly give health care providers the right to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services and their patients the right to obtain them.\par \par \uc2 It goes substantially further than codifying Roe v. Wade, laying out a lengthy list of prohibited restrictions and limits -- many of which have been enacted by states, effectively putting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nearly or entirely out of reach. For instance, it would bar mandatory ultrasounds, waiting periods and requirements that providers obtain admitting privileges at local hospitals.\par \par \uc2 The legislation also would allow courts to consider several factors when deciding whether a restriction would infringe on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, including whether it would increase the costs of providing or obtaining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, including those associated with travel, child care or taking time off work.\par \par \uc2 Some Republicans who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights call the bill too broad.\par \par \uc2 The two Senate Republicans who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Senators \uc2 Susan M. Collins\uc2  of Maine and Lisa Murkowski of Alaska, have raised objections to the Democrats' bill, noting that it would bar state laws that require certain materials to be given to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients or those that require parental or guardian notification for minors seeking to terminate their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Collins said she opposed the Democrats' bill partly because it lacks an exception to give Catholic hospitals the right to refuse to perform abortions, in line with their religious beliefs.\par \par \uc2 The Women's Health Protection Act would not require any hospital system or doctor to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, Democrats note. But critics worry that because it lacks an explicit exception for religious institutions, it could open them up to lawsuits or a loss of federal funding if they refused to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 Yet the measure may lack the simple majority it would need to pass if it could make it past a Republican blockade. On Monday, Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin III\uc2 , the centrist Democrat from West Virginia who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, said he would ''wait and see'' what his colleagues had to say about the bill before deciding how he planned to vote. In February, he voted against bringing up a nearly identical measure.\par \par \uc2 Senator Bob Casey of Pennsylvania, a Democrat who has long described himself as anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, said on Tuesday that he would support the bill, potentially leaving Mr. Manchin as the lone Democratic holdout. Mr. Casey voted in February to consider the bill, but indicated for the first time that he supported the legislation itself.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have revised their bill to widen its appeal.\par \par \uc2 Since the vote in February, Democrats have tried to make their bill more palatable to moderate lawmakers in both parties.\par \par \uc2 They stripped out a lengthy series of findings, including passages that referred to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions as ''a tool of gender oppression.'' Also scrapped was a section clarifying that while the bill referred to women, it was meant to protect the rights of ''every person capable of becoming pregnant,'' including transgender men and nonbinary individuals.\par \par \uc2 But both Ms. Collins and Ms. Murkowski have indicated that they are still opposed and pressing their own alternative. Mr. Schumer said Democrats would not compromise on the substance of their bill.\par \par \uc2 A narrow Republican alternative would provide limited protections.\par \par \uc2 Last February, Ms. Collins and Ms. Murkowski introduced their own bill, the Reproductive Choice Act, which they describe as codifying Roe v. Wade. The bill is only three pages long and was written without the consultation of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups, according to representatives from those organizations.\par \par \uc2 The legislation is simple: It would declare that states cannot impose an ''undue burden'' on the ability to choose whether to terminate a pregnancy before fetal viability, borrowing key language from the Casey decision.\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates have said it leaves too much unsaid and lacks clear guidance about what states can and cannot do, leaving vital decisions in the hands of courts that have become increasingly hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. The measure does not explicitly rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans before a fetus is viable or bar any specific prohibitions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  methods.\par \par \uc2 ''They're pushing a toothless bill,'' said Autumn Katz, senior counsel at the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''It says it codifies Roe, but in reality, it does not do that.''\par \par \uc2 For instance, it is not clear that the bill would prohibit a ban like the one that is currently before the Supreme Court, Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, which involves a Mississippi law that bans nearly all abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy. The measure would leave the door open for proponents to argue that such a ban is not an ''undue burden.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats and groups like Planned Parenthood contend that the bill would invite states to continue to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. The legislation says that states ''may enact regulations to further the health or safety of a woman seeking to terminate a pregnancy,'' language that they said was similar to what is currently used to justify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions across the country.\par \par \uc2 The bill's sponsors say they are merely trying to preserve the status quo.\par \par \uc2 ''Unlike some progressive activists, Senators Collins and Murkowski want the law today to be the law tomorrow,'' said Annie Clark, a spokeswoman for Ms. Collins. She added that those who took issue with the bill ''must also disagree with Roe and Casey.''\par \par \uc2 Protesters outside the Supreme Court last week after a leaked opinion said the court would overturn Roe v. Wade. Left, the Senate majority leader, Chuck Schumer, leading Democrats out of the Capitol. Mr. Schumer has set a vote for Wednesday to take up an \uc2  rights bill. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY VALERIE PLESCH FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; TOM BRENNER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220511ei5b00042\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
