\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 New York Lawmakers Push for  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Fund to Create a 'Safe Harbor' for Women\b0\par\par\uc2 By Grace Ashford \par \uc2 916 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Lawmakers in Albany are considering a range of options to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in New York, as many other states prepare to restrict them.\par \par \uc2 Even before the Supreme Court decided Roe v. Wade in 1973, establishing a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the country, New York was a destination for women seeking the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Now, with another Supreme Court decision that could potentially reshape \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America on the horizon, the state is once more getting ready for an influx of health care refugees.\par \par \uc2 To prepare for that surge, lawmakers in Albany and the state attorney general, Letitia James, are pushing a bill that would establish a state fund to pay \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and offset costs for low-income and uninsured or underinsured \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients.\par \par \uc2 ''We've seen states where they seek to criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we have seen states where they seek to deputize and incentivize bounty hunters to report individuals who have had abortions,'' Ms. James said at a news conference on Monday.\par \par \uc2 ''New York,'' she went on, ''is a compassionate state, a state that cares about others, and a state that recognizes that if one individual is not free, then all of us are not free.''\par \par \uc2 The bill, which was first introduced last week, is the latest in a raft of legislation under consideration in Albany in the wake of the leak of a draft opinion overturning Roe. In the days since the draft became public, Democratic leaders have marshaled forces to explore a range of options aimed at strengthening and protecting the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 New York codified Roe v. Wade into state law when it passed the \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2  Act in 2019, removing restrictions on abortions including a ban on the procedure after 24 weeks. But the proposals currently under consideration aim to expand and buttress those safeguards.\par \par \uc2 One would protect New York-based providers by forbidding law enforcement from cooperating with investigations in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is criminalized, mirroring legislation that was recently passed in Connecticut. Another would prohibit New York courts from issuing subpoenas in conjunction with out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  proceedings.\par \par \uc2 And an amendment that would enshrine the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state's Constitution is gaining momentum. The sponsor of that bill, State Senator \uc2 Liz Krueger\uc2  of Manhattan, said that such a move was critically important for safeguarding the right to reproductive care in an uncertain future.\par \par \uc2 ''We can no longer afford to play a risk game,'' Ms. Krueger said, adding that the expected ruling by the Supreme Court ''opens the door for more cases, to go after other things, including same-sex marriage, fundamental rights to privacy, all these things that aren't necessarily correctly in our Constitution.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Krueger's amendment is unique in that it seeks to provide comprehensive protection from all types of discrimination, including based on gender, sexual orientation, race and disability status. Changing the Constitution is a lengthy process that requires an amendment to be passed in multiple sessions and then approved by voters, which can take years.\par \par \uc2 But both Ms. James and Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  have suggested that they would support some form of an amendment, though it is unclear how expansive it might be.\par \par \uc2 While the ultimate shape and qualifications for the proposed fund, called the Reproductive Freedom and Equity Program, remain uncertain, the sponsor of the bill in the State Assembly, Jessica Gonz\u225\'00\'E1lez-Rojas of Queens, said that she hoped it would be allotted $50 million. That money would then be distributed through the state health department to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and nonprofit groups that help people access care.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Jessica Gonz\u225\'00\'E1lez-Rojas said that the funds could be used to cover expenses for providers, from the costs of the procedures themselves to security and training, as well as travel and child-care costs for low-income families.\par \par \uc2 ''Any rollback or constraint of any health care right is fundamentally unjust, and we know it will have particular harm on women of color, those of modest means and those already suffering disparities under our current system,'' said State Senator Cordell Cleare of Harlem, another sponsor.\par \par \uc2 New York already sees thousands of people coming to the state for reproductive care, officials said, and that number is expected to grow significantly if Roe v. Wade is overturned. The \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , which advocates on behalf of sexual health and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , estimates that 40 million reproductive-age women live in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be made illegal.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for Ms. Hochul did not comment on the fund proposal but said that the governor was considering all options. Ms. Hochul has been a vocal proponent of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights on the national level, appealing to Congress to take action and inviting women seeking abortions to come to New York.\par \par \uc2 ''The Statue of \uc2 Liberty\uc2  stands in our harbor, reaching out her hand to all those who are oppressed,'' Ms. Hochul said at a rally last week. ''If you live in a state where they are willing to strip away your rights, then you are also among the oppressed.''\par \par \uc2 She added: ''You come to New York. This is your safe harbor. Come to our state and we'll take care of you.''\par \par \uc2 Supporters of \uc2  rights in Lower Manhattan after the leak of the Supreme Court draft opinion overturning Roe v. Wade. (PHOTOGRAPH BY HILARY SWIFT FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220510ei5a00041\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
