\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Midterms' Biggest  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Battleground: Pennsylvania\b0\par\par\uc2 By Trip Gabriel \par \uc2 1,690 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The leading Republicans running for governor in the state want to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The presumptive Democratic nominee promises to veto any ban.\par \par \uc2 HANOVER TOWNSHIP, Pa. -- Jan Downey, who calls herself ''a Catholic Republican,'' is so unhappy about the Supreme Court's likely reversal of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights that she is leaning toward voting for a Democrat for Pennsylvania governor this year.\par \par \uc2 ''Absolutely,'' she said. ''On that issue alone.''\par \par \uc2 Linda Ward, also a Republican, said the state's current law allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to 24 weeks was ''reasonable.''\par \par \uc2 But Ms. Ward said she would vote for a Republican for governor, even though all the leading candidates vowed to sign legislation sharply restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . She is disgusted with inflation, mask mandates and ''woke philosophy,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 ''After what's happened this past year, I will never vote for a Democrat,'' said Ms. Ward, a retired church employee. ''Never!''\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania, one of a handful of states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access hangs in the balance with midterm elections this year, is a test case of the political power of the issue in a post-Roe world, offering a look at whether it will motivate party bases or can be a wedge for suburban independents.\par \par \uc2 After a draft of a Supreme Court opinion that would end the constitutional guarantee of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights was leaked last week, Republicans downplayed the issue, shifting attention instead to the leak itself and away from its substance. They also argued that voters' attentions were fleeting, that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was hardly a silver bullet for Democratic apathy and that more pressing issues -- inflation and President Biden's unpopularity -- had already cast the midterm die.\par \par \uc2 To Democrats, this time really is different.\par \par \uc2 ''These are terrifying times,'' said Nancy Patton Mills, chair of the Pennsylvania Democratic Party. ''There were so many people that thought that this could never happen.''\par \par \uc2 If Roe v. Wade is overturned, the power to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would return to the states. As many as 28 states are likely to ban or tightly restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to a New York Times analysis.\par \par \uc2 In four states with politically divided governments and elections for governor this year -- Pennsylvania, Michigan, Wisconsin and Kansas -- the issue is expected to be a fulcrum of campaigns. In Michigan and Wisconsin, which have anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws on the books predating Roe, Democratic governors and attorneys general have vowed to block their implementation. Kansas voters face a referendum in August on codifying that the state constitution does not protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania, which has a conservative Republican-led legislature and a term-limited Democratic governor, is the only one of the four states with an open seat for governor.\par \par \uc2 ''The legislature is going to put a bill on the desk of the next governor to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , a Democrat running unopposed for the party's nomination for governor. ''Every one of my opponents would sign it into law, and I would veto it.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Shapiro, the state's attorney general, has been primarily known for defeating multiple cases brought by supporters of \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  claiming fraud after he lost Pennsylvania by 80,000 votes in 2020. When Mr. Shapiro began his campaign last year, he focused on voting rights, but he said in an interview last week that he expected the general election to become a referendum on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 His campaign said it had its best day of fund-raising after the Supreme Court draft leaked last week.\par \par \uc2 He rejected the notion that voters, whose attention spans can be short, will absorb a major Supreme Court reversal and move on by the fall. ''I'm going to be talking about rights -- from voting rights to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- until the polls close at 8 p.m. on Election Day,'' Mr. Shapiro said. ''People are very concerned about this. I expect that level of concern, of fear, of worry, of anger is going to continue.''\par \par \uc2 All four of the top Republicans heading into the primary on May 17 have said they favor strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. Lou Barletta, a former congressman and one of two front-runners in the race, has said he would sign ''any bill that comes to my desk that would protect the life of the unborn.''\par \par \uc2 Another top candidate, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , said in a recent debate that he was opposed to any exceptions -- for rape, incest or the health of the mother -- in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Mr. Mastriano, a state senator, has introduced a bill in Harrisburg to ban abortions after a ''fetal heartbeat'' is detected, at about six weeks of pregnancy. Another Republican bill would require death certificates and a burial or cremation after miscarriages or abortions.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are worried, in Pennsylvania and around the country, that their 2020 coalition lacks motivation this year after expelling Mr. Trump from the White House. The listlessness extends to Black, Latino and younger voters, as well as suburban swing voters. It was suburbanites, especially outside Philadelphia, who gave Mr. Biden his winning edge in the state.\par \par \uc2 Democratic operatives hope \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep those independent voters -- who have since swung against the president in polls -- from defecting to Republicans.\par \par \uc2 ''With Trump no longer aggravating suburban voters every week, Republicans were hoping to regain traction in the Philadelphia suburbs in 2022,'' said J.J. Balaban, a Democratic strategist in the state. ''The fall of Roe will make that less likely to happen.'' Shavonnia Corbin-Johnson, political director of the State Democratic Party, said that the end of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access would ''add to compounding racial disparities and maternal health'' for minority communities, and that the party was planning to organize aggressively around the issue.\par \par \uc2 Soleil Hartwell, 19, who works in a big-box store near Bethlehem, is typical of voters who drop off in midterm elections after voting in presidential years. But Ms. Hartwell said she would vote this year to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. ''I don't have any kids, and I don't plan on having any yet, but if I was in a situation that required me to, I should be able to'' choose the fate of a pregnancy, she said.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are deeply skeptical that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can reanimate the Democratic base. ''Their people are depressed,'' said Rob Gleason, a former chair of the Pennsylvania Republican Party. ''Nothing's going to be able to save them this year. Speaking from Philadelphia after a road trip from his home in western Pennsylvania, Mr. Gleason said: ''I stopped on the turnpike and paid $5.40 a gallon for gas. That reminds me every time I fill up, I want a change.''\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania's large Roman Catholic population -- about one in five adults -- has afforded electoral space for a tradition of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Democratic officials, including Senator \uc2 Bob Casey Jr\uc2 ., and his father, Bob Casey Sr., who served as governor. A law that the senior Casey pushed through the legislature in the 1980s included some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, which was challenged in the 1992 Supreme Court case Planned Parenthood v. Casey. The court upheld most of the state's restrictions, while affirming Roe v. Wade's grant of a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The leaked draft of the court's opinion last week, written by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , would overturn the Casey ruling along with Roe.\par \par \uc2 Still, support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Pennsylvania has gradually increased, according to polling by \uc2 Franklin & Marshall College\uc2  over more than a decade.\par \par \uc2 Last month, 31 percent of registered voters said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all circumstances, up from 18 percent in 2009. Those calling for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be illegal in all circumstances declined to 16 percent, from 22 percent in 2009. A broad middle group, 53 percent, said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal under ''certain circumstances.''\par \par \uc2 The issue had not ranked high among the state's voters before the Supreme Court leak. In a \uc2 Monmouth University\uc2  poll last month, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was cited as one of Pennsylvania voters' top two issues by just 5 percent of Democrats and 3 percent of Republicans. Inflation topped the concerns of voters in both parties.\par \par \uc2 In Hanover Township, outside Allentown, an affluent suburb in a onetime Republican enclave that has trended blue, Dave Savage and Vincent Milite, both center-right voters, were analyzing the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue through the eyes of their adult daughters, while loading groceries outside a Wegmans supermarket.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Savage, 63, said that his 30-year-old daughter felt strongly that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal, and that therefore it would be an important issue for him in November.\par \par \uc2 A retired municipal employee, Mr. Savage said he was an independent voter but had primarily voted Republican most of his life. Come November, he would not support a candidate for governor who opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, he said. ''My position is, I don't have a vagina so I have no skin in the game.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Milite read aloud a text between his two daughters, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters, in reaction to a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  post by a cousin who wrote sharply of her opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the Supreme Court leak.\par \par \uc2 ''I unfollowed her immediately,'' one of Mr. Milite's daughters texted the other. ''I would never have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but I don't have the right to tell'' others what to do, she wrote.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Milite, a supervisor in municipal government, said he supported his daughters. ''I'm a Republican, but I'm a moderate Republican,'' he said. ''I'm a Republican because this county was always Republican.''\par \par \uc2 He said he was undecided about whom to support in this month's primary. His vote in the general election also seemed up for grabs.\par \par \uc2 ''What's going to happen is, you're going to lose a lot of Republican votes'' over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Mr. Milite predicted. ''I think it's going to hurt the Republican Party.''\par \par \uc2 A march for \uc2  in Philadelphia. Pennsylvania is a test case of the political power of the issue in a post-Roe world. (PHOTOGRAPH BY RACHEL WISNIEWSKI FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); LINDA WARD, who said issues other than \uc2  rights, like inflation, will sway her vote.; SOLEIL HARTWELL, about being able to choose the fate of a pregnancy \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220509ei590003x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
