\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Foes Ponder a World After Roe Falls\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Dias and Ruth Graham \par \uc2 1,978 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court draft opinion signals a new era for the 50-year effort to end the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Next goals include a national ban and, in some cases, classifying \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as homicide.\par \par \uc2 For nearly half a century, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement has propelled itself toward a goal that at times seemed impossible, even to true believers: overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 That single-minded mission meant coming to Washington every January for the March for Life to mark Roe's anniversary. It required electing anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawmakers and keeping the pressure on to pass state restrictions. It involved funding anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lobbying groups, praying and protesting outside clinics, and opening facilities to persuade women to keep their pregnancies. Then this week, the leaked draft of the Supreme Court opinion that would overturn the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  revealed that anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists' dream of a post-Roe America appeared poised to come to pass.\par \par \uc2 The court's opinion is not final, but the draft immediately shifted the horizon by raising a new question: If Roe is struck down, where does the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement go next?\par \par \uc2 Many leaders are redoubling state efforts, where they've already had success, with an eye toward more restrictive measures. Several prominent groups now say they would support a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban after as many as 15 weeks or as few as six, all lower than Roe's standard of around 23 or 24. A vocal faction is talking about ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  abolition,'' proposing legislation to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after conception, with few if any exceptions in cases of rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 The sprawling anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  grass-roots campaign is rapidly approaching an entirely new era, one in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would no longer be a nationally protected right to overcome, but a decision to be legislated by individual states. For many activists, overturning Roe would mark what they see as not the end, but a new beginning to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access even further. It also would present a test, as those who have long backed incremental change could clash with those who increasingly push to end legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  altogether.\par \par \uc2 This week, many anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaders were wary of celebrating before the court's final ruling, expected this summer. They remembered Planned Parenthood v. Casey in 1992, when they hoped the court would overturn Roe and it ultimately did not. But they said they have been preparing for this moment and its possibilities for decades.\par \par \uc2 ''If a dog catches a car, it doesn't know what to do,'' said Carol Tobias, president of the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2 . ''We do.''\par \par \uc2 The Susan B. Anthony List, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  political group, is planning a strategy involving state legislatures where it sees room to advance their cause or protect it. The National Right to Life is trying to support its affiliates in every state as it looks to lobby lawmakers. Both groups have been hoping to build support in Congress for a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, even if it could take years, just as it did to gain momentum to undo Roe. Many Republicans have repeatedly tried to enact a ban at about 20 weeks, without success. Next week Democrats in the Senate are bringing a bill to codify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to a vote, but it is all but certain to be blocked by Republicans.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates are using the moment to re-energize their own supporters, organize protests and mobilize for midterm elections in November. \uc2 Planned Parenthood Action Fund\uc2 , \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2  and Emily's List announced Monday, hours before the leaked draft appeared, that they would spend a collective $150 million on the midterm election cycle. Other groups are planning a nationwide ''day of action'' May 14, with marches in cities including New York, Washington, Chicago and Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 The reality of the leaked draft shocked casual supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights who weren't paying particularly close attention to the issue, or who had grown numb after decades of warnings about the end of Roe.\par \par \uc2 ''People just couldn't fathom losing a constitutional right that has been enshrined for nearly half a century,'' said Kristin Ford, vice president of communications and research for \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2 . ''To see it in such stark terms has really galvanized people.''\par \par \uc2 Across the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  spectrum, everything is on the table, from instituting bans when fetal cardiac activity is detected, to pressing their case in Democratic strongholds. Some activists are prioritizing limiting medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which accounts for more than half of all abortions.\par \par \uc2 This week in Georgia, former Senator David Perdue, who is challenging Gov. Brian Kemp in the Republican primary for governor, called for a special session to ''eliminate all of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' in the state, which already has an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban at about six weeks on the books that would likely take effect if Roe is overturned.\par \par \uc2 While many fighting for restrictions believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be murder, only a small fringe openly call for punishing a woman for procuring one.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers in Louisiana, however, advanced a bill on Wednesday that would classify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as homicide and make it possible for prosecutors to bring criminal cases against women who end a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ''If the fetus is a person, then we should protect them with the same homicide laws that protect born persons,'' said Bradley Pierce, who helped draft the Louisiana legislation and leads the Foundation to Abolish \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''That's what equal protection means.''\par \par \uc2 A more prominent anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, Louisiana Right to Life, however, opposes the bill for going too far.\par \par \uc2 For the more mainstream campaigners, a post-Roe landscape would mean the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fight will become even broader, clearing the path to expand further into state politics. ''It will be different work,'' said Mallory Carroll, spokeswoman for the Susan B. Anthony List. If Roe is overturned, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists will be free to pass legislation without having to work around Roe's limits. ''Instead of just fighting for the right to pass pro-life laws, we will actually be able to pass and protect pro-life laws,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, before the leak, a coalition led by Students for Life Action told Republican members of Congress in a letter that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions even at 12 weeks of pregnancy were not sufficient but that what ultimately mattered was ''whether the infant is a human being.''\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents' biggest goal extends beyond legislation. It is an effort to change broader American culture and get more people to see a fetus as a human person with an inherent right to life. Many activists talk about making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  not merely illegal but ''unthinkable.''\par \par \uc2 Public opinion polls show that a majority of Americans say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in at least some cases. But anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists say they see plenty of room for persuasion in the details. Polling also suggests most Americans are open to some restrictions. Thirty-four percent of Americans say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal at 14 weeks of pregnancy -- roughly the end of the first trimester -- compared with 27 percent who say it should be illegal, according to a survey released Friday by the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2 . Another 22 percent say ''it depends.''\par \par \uc2 ''We are prepared to not only create a legal landscape to protect life at the federal and state levels, but also to support a culture of life,'' said Kristen Waggoner, general counsel for the \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 , which supports Mississippi's ban at 15 weeks that led to the Supreme Court case that could overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 Advocates on the left see the leaked draft laying out a playbook for a sweeping attempt to roll back other established rights. ''There are some folks on the right saying they're just turning back to the states, when in fact it's very clear their agenda is much broader than that,'' Ms. Ford of NARAL said. ''It's not just about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Criticism that such a decision could create a cultural revolution, potentially upending precedent protections for other issues, including contraception and same-sex and interracial marriage is ''hysteria and scaremongering,'' Ms. Waggoner said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  uniquely has sustained support and energy, as shown by the annual March for Life, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Carroll, the spokeswoman for the Susan B. Anthony List, noted that there have not been sustained mass protests over other landmark Supreme Court rulings, on issues such as interracial marriage and contraception.\par \par \uc2 The movement has long been divided loosely into incrementalists, mainstream groups such as the Susan B. Anthony List and the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2 , that for years focused on gaining ground restriction by restriction, and absolutists, who saw anything less than the total elimination of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as failure. This moment is a convergence of both, with the court considering reversing Roe, and states like Texas and Oklahoma effectively banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after about six weeks of pregnancy, before many women even realize they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Now there are emerging disagreements on the moral and practical benefits of strategies like prosecuting pregnant women, and making it illegal to cross state lines for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Pierce, of the Foundation to Abolish \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the group behind the Louisiana bill that would punish women who end a pregnancy, said that the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement as a whole has treated Roe as the law of the land and has ''really just tried to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  like health care, or like an industry'' instead of ''dealing with it like homicide.''\par \par \uc2 Much of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign's energy comes from a network of church ministries and small nonprofits that work directly with pregnant women, with the goal of making it possible for more women to choose options other than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the effort as a whole is only beginning to face the vast needs of pregnant women, from child care to health care to housing. Those needs will only expand as women in states that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be required to carry their pregnancies to term.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, the Trump administration offered a $1.7 million grant to a California-based network of facilities run by an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nonprofit that sought to compete with Planned Parenthood. In Texas, Republican lawmakers assigned $100 million in their 2022-23 budget to a program called Alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which distributes funds to an array of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nonprofits.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers typically provide free pregnancy tests, as well as pregnancy and parenting resources. Some centers offer services like ultrasounds and referrals to prenatal care, employment and other needs.\par \par \uc2 ''Our ministry model has been a post-Roe ministry model from the beginning,'' said Roland Warren, president and chief executive of Care Net, a network of more than 1,100 pregnancy resource centers across the country.\par \par \uc2 Critics say the centers are ill-prepared to serve as a meaningful social safety net.\par \par \uc2 ''We know they are inadequate, and we know they are not ready,'' said Katrina Kimport, an associate professor with Advancing New Standards in \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights activists call the centers ''fake clinics'' that mislead women about what they actually offer and stigmatize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ms. Kimport, a sociologist, has studied pregnant women's experiences at pregnancy resource centers. Though many clients are grateful for things like free diapers and baby clothes, ''the scale of need far exceeds what the centers were able to provide for them,'' she said. ''We're talking about prenatal vitamins and they don't have stable housing.''\par \par \uc2 Opponents of \uc2  rights have gathered every January for the March for Life. The movement is now approaching a new era.; Demonstrating on Friday at the Supreme Court. Many anti-\uc2  leaders are doubling down on state fights. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY KENNY HOLSTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A28) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220508ei5800053\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
