\par\par \uc2 BIG CITY\par \uc2 Metropolitan Desk; SECTMB\par \b \uc2 On the Front Line of the  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Debate\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ginia Bellafante \par \uc2 1,181 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Merle Hoffman was on the front lines in the early 1970s, when women would travel to New York for abortions.\par \par \uc2 In 1970, three years before the Supreme Court's decision in Roe v. Wade, four states, New York among them, repealed their anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statutes. At the time, Merle Hoffman was a concert pianist who had recently given up her professional ambitions. The world of classical music was too hermetic, but the sweeping political currents of the '60s had not drawn her in either. She settled on graduate school in psychology, supporting herself with three jobs, one of them in the office of a doctor who wanted to open an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic and asked her to run it.\par \par \uc2 This was 1971. ''I was home reading Nietzsche and criminology and psychology and practicing Chopin,'' she told me two days after the country learned, via leak, of the Supreme Court's draft opinion set to overrule Roe. ''So this was not an obvious path.''\par \par \uc2 In the half-century since Ms. Hoffman accepted the position, she has been at the forefront of the \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement as an activist and as the founder and president of Choices Women's Medical Center in Queens, one of the first \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in the country, which grew to provide a range of health services. I asked her if, in the early years after Roe's passage, she could have anticipated what now appears inevitable. The law ''never seemed precarious but it always seemed vulnerable,'' she told me. ''I should have seen it though because I was going to funerals of people who were murdered.''\par \par \uc2 She recalled the service for her friend David Gunn in 1993, an obstetrician who performed abortions and who was shot to death outside his clinic in Pensacola by a fundamentalist Christian who had joined a fringe anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, a few months earlier, headed by a former member of the \uc2 Ku Klux Klan\uc2 . ''I was in the bathroom, and \uc2 Ellie Smeal\uc2  opened her coat,'' she said. \uc2 Eleanor Smeal\uc2  had been the president of the \uc2 National Organization for Women\uc2  and helped found the \uc2 Feminist Majority Foundation\uc2 . ''She was wearing a bulletproof vest.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hoffman was drawn to the work as soon as she began. ''What wed me to this movement was the first patient,'' she told me during our conversation this week. ''Her name was Helen. She was from New Jersey. She was white, Catholic, married and had three children. She just couldn't afford another.''\par \par \uc2 There were not yet protocols for counseling in this context, nor had much thought been given to it. ''Everything was done for the first time,'' she said. ''How do I speak to this woman? I'm thinking, My God, what do I say?' I talked and I just held her hand while she had the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I realized the enormous power and vulnerability of the act -- the choice.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hoffman became increasingly angry over what she began to call ''iatrogenic pregnancies.'' These were ''caused by the ignorance and misinformation of physicians, the ego and arrogance,'' she said, referring to doctors who told women there was no need to refit their diaphragms or that contraception wasn't necessary right after giving birth.\par \par \uc2 At 32, married and ''with all the accouterments,'' Ms. Hoffman had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because she was not ready to have children. (Twenty-six years later, she adopted a little girl from Siberia.)\par \par \uc2 It was the passage of the Hyde Amendment in 1976, which banned federal funding of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through Medicaid, that politicized her. She went to Queens College, her alma mater, and approached professors. ''I banged on doors and said, 'May I come in and address the class?' I said, 'Do you know what's happening?''' The students, she found, were mostly apathetic.\par \par \uc2 Four years before that, in 1972, approximately 100,000 women from around the country traveled to New York City to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- about half traveled more than 500 miles, and some came from as far away as Arizona, Idaho and Nevada. Most went to large facilities with multiple operating rooms. Ms. Hoffman's clinic was small at the outset; the cost of the procedure there was $77, and it was covered by HIP, the health insurer, which Ms. Hoffman said had thought about opening its own facility but did not want to rattle board members who had connections to the clergy or big labor unions.\par \par \uc2 As access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  spread in the aftermath of Roe, the need for women to get on a plane or drive for six hours to see the doctors who could help them greatly diminished. Only recently has the need resurfaced. Now in her 70s, Ms. Hoffman is seeing more and more women arriving to the facility in Queens from outside New York -- from Texas, Georgia, Alabama, Ohio -- as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws have become increasingly prohibitive, as facilities have closed down, and as doctors who performed the procedures retired and were not always easily replaced.\par \par \uc2 A reversal of Roe would end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 13 states immediately or very quickly; 14 more could restrict the procedure to 22 weeks or earlier, though many of those might also ban it completely. This would put more women in the position of traveling to terminate their pregnancies, presenting challenges to the infrastructure in place to help them, Ms. Hoffman predicted.\par \par \uc2 Over the past several decades, a network -- an ''overground railroad,'' as she put it -- has developed to accommodate women who must venture far from home to end their pregnancies. Choices, for example, works closely with the Haven Coalition, founded in 2001 by a counselor at an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in New York. One day the counselor asked an out-of-town patient where she was staying, and the patient said that she was sleeping in her car because she could not afford a hotel.\par \par \uc2 The coalition formed as a nonprofit, soliciting volunteers to pick women up from their procedures, house them in guest rooms and on sofas and offer food and comfort. Since then, funds have been created to help women cover attendant expenses. ''There's a very supportive network in place, but it will be strained,'' Ms. Hoffman said. ''People -- women, men of conscience -- will have to look in the mirror and say 'What can I do?' and then do something.''\par \par \uc2 In 1989, Ms. Hoffman recalled, she stood outside of St. Patrick's Cathedral, brandishing coat hangers along with hundreds of other protesters to challenge Cardinal John O'Connor's support of Operation Rescue, an aggressive anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group. The same afternoon that we spoke, seven-foot-tall fences were being installed around the Supreme Court anticipating demonstrations.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm reliving my youth,'' Ms. Hoffman said. ''My feeling is that we minimized the strength of the opposition.''\par \par \uc2 EMAIL bigcity@nytimes.com; follow Ginia Bellafante on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @GiniaNYT\par \par \uc2 Merle Hoffman, center, in the 1980s. In the 1970s, Ms. Hoffman was the founder of an \uc2  clinic in Queens, one of the first in the country. (PHOTOGRAPH VIA MERLE HOFFMAN) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220508ei580003p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
