\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Fearing Backlash, G.O.P. Is Quiet on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jonathan Weisman \par \uc2 1,595 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 While Democrats decry a draft opinion that would eliminate the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Republicans who worked decades for this moment have been largely silent.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Republicans have spent decades attacking the landmark Supreme Court decision that legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide, but with the toppling of Roe v. Wade seemingly imminent, their leaders in Congress and around the country have grown suddenly quiet on the issue, part of a bid to avoid a backlash against their party ahead of the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 In the days after the leak of a draft Supreme Court opinion that would overturn the 50-year-old precedent, Republicans in Congress have notably refrained from taking a victory lap for having helped to install the conservative majority that has paved the way for such an outcome.\par \par \uc2 Even as some of their counterparts at the state level race forward with far-reaching \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that could even affect some methods of contraception, Republicans appear determined to recast their position on the issue as one of moderation and avert the gaze of voters away from their anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights agenda.\par \par \uc2 ''You need -- it seems to me, excuse the lecture -- to concentrate on what the news is today,'' Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  of Kentucky, the Republican leader, said on Tuesday. ''Not a leaked draft but the fact that the draft was leaked.''\par \par \uc2 The Republicans' caution reflects the potential for the eventual ruling to change the midterm political landscape. Their leaders and candidates have built a campaign to reclaim control of the House and Senate around inflation, economic uncertainty, crime, border control and American doubts that President Biden, who is deeply unpopular, can right the ship.\par \par \uc2 Now the prospect of eliminating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights has added a tectonic change to American life into the mix, threatening to upend that focus.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have signaled that they plan to use the coming decision as a rallying cry for voters to reject Republicans, portraying its implications as vast and unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 ''This is an issue that is defining for this country today, and if the American people don't stand up for equality for every American at this moment in time, we will be undermining a right to privacy in more than this context,'' said Senator Kirsten Gillibrand, Democrat of New York. She raised the specter of a conservative Supreme Court going after gay marriage, consensual same-sex relations and even contraception if the decision stands.\par \par \uc2 Republicans, by contrast, believe their candidates' job right now is to remain focused on the economy and not allow any other issue -- particularly one that could alienate suburban independent voters whose backing they need to win congressional majorities -- to distract them.\par \par \uc2 ''Big picture, tell me what the 30-year fixed mortgage rate will be and if anything has improved with gas and groceries, and I'll tell you the results,'' said Corry Bliss, a veteran strategist who advises Republican candidates. ''That is what the midterms are going to be about -- period, end of discussion.''\par \par \uc2 Republicans are talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , just not openly. A document circulated by the National Republican Senatorial Committee and obtained by Axios urged candidates to be low-key about the issue, with a post-Roe America looming as early as next month.\par \par \uc2 ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be avoided as much as possible,'' the document advised candidates to say. ''States should have the flexibility to implement reasonable restrictions.''\par \par \uc2 Republicans do not want to throw doctors and women in jail, the document continued. They certainly do not want to take away contraception. And if any party is being extreme, it instructed Republicans to argue, it is the Democrats, who will not accept even modest restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that most Americans support.\par \par \uc2 The approach is calculated to exploit the fact that Democrats, outraged about the ruling yet powerless to do anything about it, are planning a symbolic vote that puts their party on the record opposing almost any \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  limits. On Wednesday, Senate Democrats will try -- and likely fail -- to take up legislation that would not only codify the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but also nullify restrictions that have passed muster with the courts.\par \par \uc2 ''The Democrats are going to make this easy for us,'' said Mallory Carroll, vice president of communications at Susan B. Anthony List, which works to elect officials who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. She called the Democrats' Women's Health Protection Act ''far outside the American mainstream.''\par \par \uc2 And ''mainstream'' is how the Republican campaign arms want their candidates to present themselves -- as soft-spoken, compassionate, ''consensus builders,'' as the talking points put it.\par \par \uc2 ''I am pro-life, but this isn't about political labels,'' the documents suggest Republican candidates say. ''I believe all Americans want us to welcome every child into the world with open arms. But if you disagree with me, my door's always open.''\par \par \uc2 Governors like Brian Kemp of Georgia and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida have said relatively little on the issue since the draft opinion came out.\par \par \uc2 Even former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , who campaigned in 2016 on appointing Supreme Court justices who would overturn Roe, has refrained from gloating.\par \par \uc2 ''Nobody knows exactly what it represents,'' he told Politico, calling the leak of the opinion ''a terrible thing for the court and for the country.\par \par \uc2 ''We'll talk about it after we find out what the definitive version is,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 It is still possible that the court will not go as far as the draft. Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  confirmed that the leak was authentic but cautioned that the decision was not final.\par \par \uc2 Still, the problem for Republican leaders in Washington who want to downplay the implications of the potential ruling is the very clear message coming from their party's state legislators about the severe restrictions many would enact if there were no longer a right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, lawmakers in Louisiana pushed forward legislation that would do precisely what the Washington talking points deny: grant constitutional rights to ''all unborn children from the moment of fertilization,'' and classify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as homicide. Such a law could, in fact, put women and doctors in prison and ban certain types of contraception, such as IUDs, that block implantation of a fertilized egg.\par \par \uc2 Republicans in South Dakota, Indiana and Nebraska have called for special sessions of their legislatures to move on strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans as soon as a final decision is announced.\par \par \uc2 And for all the caution Republican strategists might advise, there is still the passion of the issue. New Hampshire State Representative Susan DeLemus was filmed responding to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights protesters at the State Capitol in Concord, N.H., on Thursday by screaming that they were ''murderers.''\par \par \uc2 To a certain extent, opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  say they really are in a moment of unreality. Carol Tobias, president of the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2 , said she had been preparing for a good night's sleep in her Albuquerque home when the news first broke of the draft opinion. She said she thought someone had pulled a prank on the reporters.\par \par \uc2 Even now, she said she is cautious.\par \par \uc2 ''What came out earlier this week is three months old,'' she said. ''I certainly hope it is the final draft, but I've been told it isn't. It's still possible a justice or two has changed positions.''\par \par \uc2 Republicans say their restraint on the issue makes sense. A near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban has been in place in Texas for eight months, and seemingly no political price has been paid so far.\par \par \uc2 State Representative Gene Wu, a Houston Democrat, said he worries that in Republican states that have been living with steadily rising restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the response to overturning Roe could be as muted as it has been in Texas, and that in Democratic states, voters will be reassured that their rights are safe.\par \par \uc2 ''This has been done so incrementally, it's like there's a learned helplessness. We've taken so much abuse; what's a little more?'' he said, likening women in states like Texas to the frog in the boiling pot of water. ''I hope that's not the case.''\par \par \uc2 Another factor mitigating the backlash might be the rising popularity of long-term contraception, such as IUDs, and the increased access to birth control in general, which has helped lower the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate in recent years and given more women a sense of reproductive security.\par \par \uc2 A decade-old study by the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2  found that the percentage of women in childbearing years using long-acting, reversible contraception had risen steadily, from 2.4 percent in 2002 to 8.5 percent in 2009 to 11.6 percent in 2012. The figure is about 12 percent now, said Dr. Nisha Verma, a fellow at the college and a gynecologist in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 ''The need for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will never go away,'' Dr. Verma said, but, she added, ''We've definitely seen that people have been able to take more control in their \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Another study in JAMA Internal Medicine found a 21.6 percent jump in the use of such contraception in the months after the 2016 election of Mr. Trump, with his vows to install justices who would overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 Emily Cochrane contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Emily Cochrane contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Senator Mitch McConnell of Kentucky, the G.O.P. leader, said the news was ''not a leaked draft, but the fact that the draft was leaked.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY TOM BRENNER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A11) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220510ei570000f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
