\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Runoff in South Texas Becomes Test of  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Politics\b0\par\par\uc2 By James Dobbins, Jennifer Medina and Katie Glueck \par \uc2 1,801 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A staunchly anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Democrat in Congress will face a young \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporter in a pivotal primary runoff.\par \par \uc2 LAREDO, Texas -- When Representative \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  stepped onstage at a campaign rally in San Antonio this week, he spoke of education, health care and his experience in Congress. But as Mr. Cuellar, a nine-term Democratic congressman, faces his toughest re-election challenge yet, one word did not escape his lips: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cuellar, the most staunchly anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Democrat in the House, will face a primary runoff later this month against Jessica Cisneros, a 28-year-old immigration lawyer and a progressive supporter of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Democrats across the country hope the leaked draft Supreme Court opinion overturning Roe v. Wade will help galvanize their voters in the midterm elections, potentially rallying support for the party in a year that is widely expected to benefit Republicans.\par \par \uc2 But one of the first tests of just how much the issue will motivate Democratic voters will come in a primary runoff at the end of May, in the South Texas district held by Mr. Cuellar where conservative Democrats have regularly succeeded. It is a heavily Latino district that includes the border city of Laredo and parts of San Antonio, where Catholicism dominates, and where, as Mr. Cuellar showed from the stage, the subject of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  often goes undiscussed.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Enrique Benavides III, a 51-year-old obstetrician-gynecologist who manages a women's health clinic with his father in Laredo, said that typically when patients request abortions, he gives them information about a clinic in San Antonio, more than a two-hour drive away.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Benavides described himself as a pro-choice Catholic Democrat who will vote for Mr. Cuellar. ''Democrats here are very different than those on the coasts, very Catholic and conservative,'' he said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates believe the Supreme Court's draft ruling will shake up the race, providing a helpful jolt for Ms. Cisneros. But supporters of Mr. Cuellar and some local Democratic officials say the district's voters, who lean socially conservative, are unlikely to be moved by the issue. And several national Democratic leaders who have publicly made fiery vows to maintain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are nonetheless standing behind Mr. Cuellar, with some warning that a win for Ms. Cisneros later this month could give Republicans an edge in November.\par \par \uc2 In interviews with nearly a dozen men and women in Laredo, most said they would support Mr. Cuellar in spite of -- or even because of -- his stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 These voters, several of whom expressed support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights but said they would still vote for an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  congressman, revealed the uphill battle Ms. Cisneros faces as she tries to convince voters to oust a familiar political figure whose family has long been a fixture in the community. Even among Democrats, support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights may not uniformly motivate voters, particularly working-class Latinos -- a demographic that has shown signs of drifting away from the party.\par \par \uc2 Hector Gomez, 67, has known Mr. Cuellar since they were classmates at J.W. Nixon High School in the 1970s, and has voted for the congressman in every election since he first ran in 2006.\par \par \uc2 ''He's doing his job,'' said Mr. Gomez, an antique store owner, adding that although he is Catholic and opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the issue does not determine his vote. ''Mr. Cuellar is the best choice because he's not someone you can just brush off.''\par \par \uc2 Texas' 28th Congressional District stretches from the Mexican border to San Antonio, and Laredo is its political center. A working-class city, it has been a Democratic stronghold for decades but remains culturally conservative, with residents who fill Catholic church pews on Sundays. Many describe themselves as apolitical, and said they are more focused on making ends meet than staking out positions on partisan political issues.\par \par \uc2 Before this week's leaked Supreme Court opinion, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not been the central issue in the primary campaign, though several national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups had invested heavily in the district, focusing on the new state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in Texas. Ms. Cisneros did not run a television advertisement on the issue until late last month, according to AdImpact, an ad tracking firm. Until the focus on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was renewed this week, the runoff had been a mostly sleepy affair, with observers predicting an extremely low turnout.\par \par \uc2 Now, Ms. Cisneros and her supporters have moved to use the threat to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as a primary motivator for both voters and donors.\par \par \uc2 ''We're really at a moment where people are fired up and they know how much they are at risk of losing,'' said Kristin Ford, the vice president of communications and research at the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2 , which has sent organizers to Laredo to campaign for Ms. Cisneros.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Cisneros argues that the district is not nearly as conservative as Mr. Cuellar and his backers suggest, and that attitudes are changing.\par \par \uc2 ''This ignited the urgency,'' she said in an interview on Friday. ''When we defeat the anti-choice Democrat, that's going to set the tone for the rest of the midterms that we want a pro-choice Democratic majority in power.''\par \par \uc2 Several prominent left-wing lawmakers, including Representative \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  of New York, have made fund-raising appeals for Ms. Cisneros. Earlier this week, Senator Elizabeth Warren of Massachusetts, one of Ms. Cisneros's most prominent backers, used an appearance on MSNBC to make a full-throated fund-raising pitch.\par \par \uc2 ''If you're mad when you listen to this, send Jessica Cisneros ten bucks,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 And during a recent virtual fund-raiser for Ms. Cisneros, Ms. Warren, speaking of Mr. Cuellar, said, ''When it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Henry's got a record that makes my blood boil,'' according to prepared remarks obtained by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ms. Cisneros first challenged Mr. Cuellar in 2020, when she lost the Democratic primary by 4 percentage points, and quickly made plans to pursue a rematch. Earlier this year, the F.B.I. raided Mr. Cuellar's Laredo home as part of an ongoing investigation that appears to be linked to an inquiry into the political influence of Azerbaijan, the former Soviet republic. In March, Mr. Cisneros fell less than 1,000 votes short of Mr. Cuellar's total, forcing the two into the May 24 runoff.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cuellar, who declined to comment for this article, has long defended his anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance.\par \par \uc2 ''As a lifelong Catholic, I have always been pro-life,'' he said in a statement this week. ''As a Catholic, I do not support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, we cannot have an outright ban. There must be exceptions in the case of rape, incest and danger to the life of the mother.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cuellar has repeatedly insisted that his stance mirrors the views of voters in the district. Though there has not been any public district-level polling on the issue, 2018 data from the Public Religion Research Institute found that Hispanics in Texas are less likely than other Americans to say they believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all cases, with 53 percent saying it should be illegal in most or all cases. Gallup's Values and Beliefs poll last year found that low-income voters are also more likely to identify themselves as ''pro life,'' a trend that has held steady for several years and could be a factor in Mr. Cuellar's largely working-class district. But in a poll from the Texas Politics Project at the \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2  released last June, 54 percent of Hispanics said they were opposed to a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if Roe v. Wade was overturned.\par \par \uc2 Just two days after the draft opinion was leaked, Mr. Cuellar campaigned alongside Representative \uc2 James E. Clyburn\uc2 , the House majority whip and third-ranking Democrat. Mr. Clyburn's visit, which had been announced before the leaked opinion, put him in an awkward spotlight -- supporting the only Democrat to vote against the Women's Health Protection Act, a bill that would have codified Roe v. Wade into law.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Clyburn defended the incumbent, saying Democrats are a ''big-tent party.''\par \par \uc2 ''I don't believe we ought to have a litmus test in the Democratic Party,'' he told reporters in San Antonio. ''We have to bring as many people into the party as we possibly can.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cuellar also has the support of \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , the House Speaker, and \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2 , the majority leader. He frequently touts their endorsements, leaving many progressives privately grumbling that party leaders' promises to fight for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Congress ring hollow.\par \par \uc2 But several Texas Democratic officials cautioned against the idea that an intense focus on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would reshape the race. Asked how the leaked draft Supreme Court opinion overturning Roe v. Wade might change the runoff, \uc2 Gilberto Hinojosa\uc2 , the chairman of the Texas Democratic Party, sounded skeptical that it would fundamentally alter the dynamics of the contest, as some advocates have predicted. South Texas, he said, is ''a different place.''\par \par \uc2 Angie Martinez, a 40-year-old bartender, said that while she supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, it will not prevent her from voting for Mr. Cuellar.\par \par \uc2 ''People are happy with the guy,'' she said, adding that he had helped bring more funding into the district. ''If \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ends, it's OK. Women go to Nuevo Laredo for abortions,'' she said, referring to the Mexican city across the border from Laredo.\par \par \uc2 But Maxine Rebeles, a 39-year-old middle school writing teacher, said she is eager to see Mr. Cuellar out of office.\par \par \uc2 ''He doesn't protect our water, and he doesn't protect our women,'' Ms. Rebeles said. ''When the government forces women to have children too young, bring children into unloving households, born to mothers who don't love themselves, they get into a bad cycle.''\par \par \uc2 James Dobbins reported from Laredo, Jennifer Medina from Los Angeles and Katie Glueck from New York.\par \par \uc2 James Dobbins reported from Laredo, Jennifer Medina from Los Angeles and Katie Glueck from New York.\par \par \uc2 Democrats in Texas' 28th District, with Laredo at its core, face a choice in a runoff between a firmly anti-\uc2  incumbent and a young \uc2 -rights supporter.; ENRIQUE BENAVIDES III, a doctor who helps to manage a women's clinic in Laredo. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY JASON GARZA FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Henry Cuellar, who was first elected in 2004, said that as a Catholic, ''I have always been pro-life.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY ERIC GAY/ASSOCIATED PRESS); Jessica Cisneros, a lawyer who backs \uc2  rights, on the campaign trail with Elizabeth Warren. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ILANA PANICH-LINSMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220507ei570004o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
