\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Key Races Are Shaped By  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Azi Paybarah \par \uc2 734 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, Georgia and beyond, the prospective fall of Roe v. Wade hangs over key primary and general elections.\par \par \uc2 There was never any doubt that an impending Supreme Court decision on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , expected over the summer, would have an outsize effect on the midterm campaigns this year. The issue just wasn't expected to upend the races so soon.\par \par \uc2 But with this week's leak of a draft opinion signaling that the Supreme Court is likely to overturn Roe v. Wade, candidates on both the right and left are seizing on the issue. Democrats, looking to hold on to their slim control of Congress, are hoping that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will galvanize their voters in an otherwise tough year for the party -- one that has been characterized by low approval ratings for President Biden and frustrations over the economy and inflation.\par \par \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , the Democrat vying to be Georgia's next governor in what will be one of the nation's most closely watched contests this fall, temporarily halted her campaign fund-raising in order to raise money for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups.\par \par \uc2 And Georgia's current governor, Brian Kemp, is being pushed further to the right on the issue by his primary challenger, \uc2 David Perdue\uc2 , the Trump-backed former senator. Mr. Perdue on Thursday pressured Mr. Kemp to promise to call a special session of the legislature to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  outright if the court eliminates federal protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 In the high-spending Pennsylvania Republican Senate primary, a new ad on Thursday from a super PAC that has supported David McCormick in the race warns that Dr. Mehmet Oz, the former host of ''The Dr. Oz Show'' and Mr. McCormick's top rival, ''will betray us'' because he ''isn't fully pro-life.''\par \par \uc2 The issue may also raise the profile of a far less familiar Republican candidate in the Pennsylvania race: Kathy Barnette, a political commentator who has said the only exception should be when the mother's life is at stake. The morning after the draft decision was published, Ms. Barnette wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that she was the ''byproduct of rape'' and attached a four-minute video, which has been viewed more than 350,000 times.\par \par \uc2 In North Carolina, where an open Senate seat has drawn crowded primaries on both sides of the aisle, the presumptive Democratic nominee, Cheri Beasley, is framing the Republicans in the race as extremists who oppose abortions ''even in the case of incest, rape or the health of a mother being at risk.''\par \par \uc2 The presumptive Democratic nominee for governor in Pennsylvania, \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , is not waiting for a winner to be chosen in the May 17 Republican primary to go on offense. On Thursday, Mr. Shapiro's campaign released a TV ad against State Senator \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , a leading Republican contender, saying that ''he wants to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 The leaked draft opinion has also raised the stakes in a critical Democratic rematch taking place in Texas, where the progressive lawyer Jessica Cisneros is in a May 24 runoff with Representative \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , who was the only congressional Democrat to vote against a \uc2 U.S. House\uc2  bill that would have nullified Texas's near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Minutes after the draft decision was published, she framed the race as a chance to protect those rights.\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, where several Republicans are vying for the chance to challenge Senator Mark Kelly, a Democrat, Mr. Kelly has framed the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue as a generational fight for freedoms. ''It's wrong that my granddaughter might soon have fewer rights than my grandmother,'' he wrote on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The candidates in the race's Republican primary have jumped on the issue as well. Blake Masters, a venture capitalist, described \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in graphic terms on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , and said Mr. Kelly ''wants to force your state to allow it.''\par \par \uc2 Mark Brnovich\uc2 , another leading Republican in the Arizona Senate primary and the state's attorney general, has made opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a key talking point. He said the Supreme Court should send Roe v. Wade ''to the ash heap of history where it belongs.''\par \par \uc2 A rally in support of \uc2  rights in Austin, Texas, on Tuesday after the leak of the Supreme Court draft opinion ending Roe v. Wade. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Jay Janner/Austin American-Statesman, via Associated Press FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220507ei570003v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
