\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Would Toppling an  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Precedent Portend a Disregard for Others?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Charlie Savage \par \uc2 1,538 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The legal reasoning that the Supreme Court's conservative bloc is considering to end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights could uproot a series of other past rulings that created modern rights.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- As an appeals court judge, \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 . repeatedly drew criticism from his colleagues for disrespecting precedents and established understandings of the law. Fellow judges said, for example, that one of his dissents ignored ''our precedent'' and another deviated from the axiom that laws must be interpreted ''by well-recognized rules.''\par \par \uc2 Two decades later, a leaked draft opinion shows that the Supreme Court may soon overturn precedents like Roe v. Wade and eliminate women's constitutional right to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Justice Alito wrote the draft, devoting 30 pages to arguing that it was unnecessary for the court to follow decisions that had affirmed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights for nearly 50 years.\par \par \uc2 The legal principle of stare decisis -- that previous rulings should generally be respected and not lightly overturned -- ''is not an inexorable command,'' Justice Alito wrote, adding: ''When one of our constitutional decisions goes astray, the country is usually stuck with the bad decision unless we correct our own mistake.''\par \par \uc2 The apparently imminent demise of constitutional \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights raises the question of how aggressive the Roberts court will become in overturning precedents -- and whether similar constitutional rights that protect various types of sexual activity and conjugal relationships may be challenged by cultural and religious conservatives in coming years.\par \par \uc2 For most of the Roberts court era, the Supreme Court has not been unusually aggressive about overturning precedents compared with its predecessors dating back to after World War II, data shows. But the court has been transformed by appointees from former President Donald J. Trump, giving a conservative movement faction the power to uproot what was thought to be settled law undergirding important parts of modern American life.\par \par \uc2 In his draft decision, Justice Alito himself noted that the Supreme Court established many other significant modern rights based on the same reasoning as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Those he cited include precedents barring states from criminalizing or otherwise blocking people from buying and using contraception; sexual intercourse between consenting adults of the same sex; and interracial or same-sex marriages.\par \par \uc2 Like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, those rights involve personal freedoms that are not mentioned in the Constitution, but whose existence the Supreme Court has inferred from the 14th Amendment. It limits the government's ability to take away people's liberty unfairly, which courts have interpreted as implying a right to privacy and personal autonomy over decisions involving people's bodies and relationships.\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito put forward a narrow interpretation of that doctrine, citing an analytical framework laid out in a 1997 ruling by the chief justice at the time, William Rehnquist, upholding a state law that banned assisted suicide. But Justice Alito omitted that in laying out that framework, the 1997 opinion had also discussed and cited a key 1992 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights ruling that his draft opinion would reverse as erroneous.\par \par \uc2 Essentially, Justice Alito argued that for an unwritten liberty right to be legitimate, it must be deeply rooted in the nation's history and have been understood to exist when the 14th Amendment was ratified in 1868. Back then, he wrote, many states had criminalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and there was no understanding that women had a right to end their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito also sought to mitigate any concerns that his reasoning would jeopardize other modern-era rights. None of the others involved the destruction of a fetus, so they did not raise ''the critical moral question posed by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' he wrote.\par \par \uc2 ''We emphasize that our decision concerns the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and no other right,'' he added. ''Nothing in this opinion should be understood to cast doubt on precedents that do not concern \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 But that reassurance has been greeted with broad skepticism.\par \par \uc2 For one, it is not clear that contraception, which some states outlawed before a landmark 1965 decision and its progeny, is different than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . After all, Justice Alito has already accepted, in a 2014 opinion he wrote in the case of Burwell v. \uc2 Hobby Lobby\uc2 , that companies have a right to refuse to cover contraception in employees' health plans if the owners believe, for religious reasons, that contraception is an abortifacient.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, should cultural and religious conservatives in state legislatures or Congress enact further laws violating Supreme Court precedents that established modern-era rights, proponents of those laws could invoke the same arguments to press the court to overturn those precedents, too. After all, matters like rights for same-sex couples equally have no deep historical basis and, in some people's minds, also raise critical moral questions.\par \par \uc2 Laurence Tribe, a liberal-leaning Harvard Law School professor whom Justice Alito repeatedly cited for a 1973 law review article in which Mr. Tribe criticized the reasoning of Roe v. Wade, denounced Justice Alito's assertion that the draft opinion raised no doubts about precedents that do not concern \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''That's not how principled adjudication works,'' Mr. Tribe wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . ''Either you're being a political hack or your 'only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' bit is BS.''\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito's opinion did purport to give greater fealty to precedents that helped him overturn \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Some supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have argued that they could be held to come from a different part of the Constitution: the 14th Amendment's ''equal protection'' clause. By that theory, laws barring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  amount to sex-based discrimination against women.\par \par \uc2 Judge Alito dismissed that idea as ''squarely foreclosed by our precedents'' -- citing a 1974 case about whether pregnancies must be covered by a state-run temporary employment disability insurance program and a 1993 case about anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protesters, and swiftly moving on.\par \par \uc2 The Roberts court era began in 2005, when Chief Justice Rehnquist died and President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  appointed \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr\uc2 . as his successor.\par \par \uc2 Later that year, Mr. Bush nominated his White House lawyer, Harriet Miers, to fill the vacancy created by the retirement of Justice \uc2 Sandra Day O'Connor\uc2 . But conservatives balked because she had no paper trail showing engagement with their ideology. Mr. Bush pulled her nomination and put forward Mr. Alito instead, beginning a rightward shift.\par \par \uc2 Overall, the Roberts court has not been unusually prone to overturning precedents, according to data compiled by Adam Feldman, a Supreme Court scholar and creator of the Empirical SCOTUS blog.\par \par \uc2 From 2005 through last term, the Roberts court has overturned precedents in about 1.47 cases per term, the fewest of any chief justice since World War II, Mr. Feldman's data showed. Measured another way, the court has overturned precedents in 2.27 percent of the cases it has heard, a slightly lower rate than predecessors dating back to the Warren Court of the 1950s and 1960s.\par \par \uc2 But in recent years, the makeup of the court has changed drastically. Mr. Trump replaced a moderate conservative justice, \uc2 Anthony Kennedy\uc2 , and a liberal one, \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , with justices who are considered much likelier to vote in a consistently conservative direction, \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and Amy Coney Barrett.\par \par \uc2 Along with Justice Alito, \uc2 Neil M. Gorsuch\uc2 , and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , there is now a conservative supermajority on the court. And its ideologically median justice -- who in close cases determines the majority -- has moved from Justice Kennedy or Chief Justice Roberts to Justice Kavanaugh or Justice Barrett, legal scholars say, making it much likelier to issue conservative rulings.\par \par \uc2 In 2018 and 2019, some liberals accused the Roberts court of being activist in overturning decades-old precedents on several matters. Those included holding that government workers who choose not to join unions may not be required to help pay for collective bargaining efforts, ruling that states may not be sued in the courts of other states, and permitting people to sue in federal court to seek compensation as soon as state and local governments take their property through eminent domain.\par \par \uc2 In the last two cases, Justice \uc2 Stephen G. Breyer\uc2  and Elena Kagan dissented, warning that the court was becoming too disrespectful of precedent. After the court's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  precedents, for example, Justice Breyer wrote, ''Today's decision can only cause one to wonder which cases the court will overrule next.''\par \par \uc2 At the time, Jonathan H. Adler, a libertarian-leaning \uc2 Case Western Reserve University\uc2  law professor, cited data like Mr. Feldman's analysis to rebuke liberals sounding those alarms, given that the Roberts court on average was not out of the ordinary.\par \par \uc2 But in an interview this week, Mr. Adler said he now expected the current Supreme Court to overturn precedents more frequently -- and to do so more consistently in a conservative direction.\par \par \uc2 ''Advocates and lower courts will create more opportunities to overturn precedent, in the way cases are framed and brought and the types of arguments made,'' he said. ''And without Roberts being the median justice anymore, the likelihood that the court is willing to overturn precedent I would expect to go up.''\par \par \uc2 Justice Samuel A. Alito Jr. devoted 30 pages of the draft opinion to arguing that it was unnecessary to follow long-held decisions. (PHOTOGRAPH BY DREW ANGERER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220506ei560003z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
