\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 In  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Fight, Pills Could Be the Next Focus\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck and Sheryl Gay Stolberg \par \uc2 1,938 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  allows patients to terminate early pregnancies at home. Some states are moving to limit it, while others are working to expand access.\par \par \uc2 If the Supreme Court overturns Roe v. Wade, the legal and culture wars over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that have consumed the United States for decades would increasingly shift to a new front: the use of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- a two-drug combination that can be taken at home or in any location and is authorized for use in the first 10 weeks of pregnancy -- has become more and more prevalent and now accounts for more than half of recent abortions in the United States. If the federal guarantee of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights disappears, medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely become an even more sought-after method for terminating a pregnancy -- and the focus of battles between states that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and those that continue to allow it.\par \par \uc2 ''Given that most abortions are early and medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is harder to trace and already kind of becoming the majority or preferred method, it's going to be a big deal,'' Mary Ziegler, a legal scholar who has written widely on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said. ''It's going to generate a lot of forthcoming legal conflicts because it's just going to be a way that state borders are going to become less relevant.''\par \par \uc2 About half the states are expected to quickly make all methods of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  illegal if the justices' decision in a Mississippi case resembles a draft opinion leaked this week that would nullify the 1973 ruling that legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In Louisiana, a legislative committee this week advanced a bill that would allow homicide charges to be brought against people who get abortions or perform them.\par \par \uc2 Other states would likely continue to allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and several are already taking steps to accommodate patients from the states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be outlawed.\par \par \uc2 Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is less expensive and less invasive than surgical abortions. In December, the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  made access to it significantly easier by lifting the requirement that patients obtain the first of the two pills, mifepristone, by visiting an authorized clinic or doctor in person. Now, patients can have a consultation with a physician via video or phone or by filling out online forms, and then receive the pills by mail.\par \par \uc2 But many conservative states have already begun passing laws to restrict medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including banning it earlier than 10 weeks' gestation and requiring patients to visit providers in person despite F.D.A. rules. Nineteen states ban the use of telemedicine for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This year, \uc2 Americans United for Life\uc2 , an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocacy group, listed laws against medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as first among the organization's ''pressing priorities'' for 2022.\par \par \uc2 ''In the last year, Arizona, Arkansas, Indiana, Montana, Ohio, Oklahoma, South Dakota and Texas have enacted state-level safeguards to stop mail-order \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs, and the Tennessee Legislature recently sent such protections to Gov. Bill Lee,'' Mallory Carroll, an official with Susan B. Anthony List, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, said. ''In addition to creating health and safety standards, states are also increasing requirements for reporting complications from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs. We will be working with allies in additional states to tackle this growing public health threat.''\par \par \uc2 Residents of states that would quickly ban all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  methods if Roe were overturned -- including Texas, Missouri, Utah and Tennessee -- would be legally prohibited from having telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  consultations from any location in their state, even if the doctor were located in a state with legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Such patients would have to travel to a state where an online, video or phone consultation is legal -- the IP address of the computer or phone they were using would identify where they were located. Then, they would have to receive the pills by mail at an address in a state with legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even if it were a post office box or a hotel.\par \par \uc2 Some patients are already doing this because they live in one of the states that ban the use of telemedicine for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some aspects of those laws are unclear, including whether patients who take the pills after returning to their home state are violating their state's law.\par \par \uc2 If \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were completely outlawed in those states, many more patients would travel to states where it was legal, \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts said.\par \par \uc2 Several organizations, including \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Demand and Hey Jane, now arrange telemedicine or online consultations and mail pills from one of two mail-order pharmacies that are currently authorized by the two mifepristone manufacturers to dispense that medication.\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents and states that outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to try to challenge or curtail the ability of patients to cross state lines to get the pills, legal experts said. There may be attempts by states that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prosecute doctors and other health providers in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal, for example, or to try to block organizations or funds that provide financial help for patients to travel to other states, Professor Ziegler said.\par \par \uc2 States that support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are mobilizing to block such efforts. Legislation in California would provide financial assistance to patients traveling from other states to obtain abortions and increase the number of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers. Connecticut just passed a bill that would prevent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers from being extradited to other states, bar Connecticut authorities from cooperating with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  investigations from a patient's home state and allow Connecticut residents who are sued under another state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provision to countersue.\par \par \uc2 Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  became legal in the United States in 2000, when mifepristone was approved by the F.D.A. The agency imposed tight restrictions on the drug, many of which remain in place. But access to the method increased in 2016, when the F.D.A. expanded the time frame within which the drug could be taken -- from seven weeks to 10 weeks into a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 As conservative states began passing more laws restricting access to surgical abortions, more patients opted for pills, especially because they can be taken in the privacy of one's home.\par \par \uc2 The Covid pandemic fueled that trend. The \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research organization that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, reported that in 2020, medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounted for 54 percent of all abortions.\par \par \uc2 Early in the pandemic, medical groups filed a lawsuit asking the F.D.A. to lift its requirement that mifepristone, which blocks a hormone crucial to the continuation of a pregnancy, be dispensed to patients in person at a clinic or doctor's office. Citing years of data showing that medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is safe, the medical groups said that patients faced a greater risk of being infected with the coronavirus if they had to visit clinics to obtain mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 For portions of the pandemic, the F.D.A. temporarily lifted the in-person requirement, then permanently removed it in December. In addition, the agency said pharmacies could begin dispensing mifepristone if they met certain qualifications. The agency is in the process of hammering out those qualifications with the two manufacturers of the drug, and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations said that some national retail pharmacy chains have expressed interest in being able to dispense the medication in some states, at least by mail.\par \par \uc2 The second medication, misoprostol, which causes contractions similar to those of a miscarriage and is taken up to 48 hours later, has long been available for a variety of uses with a typical prescription.\par \par \uc2 A senior Biden administration official said this week that officials are looking for further steps the administration can take to increase access to all types of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the pill method. The official, speaking on the condition of anonymity to discuss the leaked Supreme Court decision, said that President Biden directed his team ''at every aspect in every creative way, every aspect of federal law, to try to do all that's possible'' to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 As part of that effort, Mr. Biden's secretary of health and human services, \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2 , said in testimony before the Senate on Wednesday that he has established a \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care task force.\par \par \uc2 But there are tight limits on what the administration can do without action from Congress. The longstanding Hyde Amendment, which prevents taxpayer dollars from being used to terminate pregnancy, bans the use of federal funds to pay for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including through the Medicaid program, except in cases of rape, incest or life endangerment.\par \par \uc2 Some experts have suggested that the government could direct resources to groups that provide support, including housing and transportation, to patients who cross state lines seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it is possible that could violate the Hyde Amendment.\par \par \uc2 Legal and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy experts said that beyond using his bully pulpit, Mr. Biden's options are limited. They said the administration could turn to the courts and make a legal argument that doctors in the United States have a right to prescribe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication from any state.\par \par \uc2 ''Because the F.D.A. has approved \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills as safe and effective and set forth a regimen by which they have to be dispensed, states are not allowed to do anything different, because federal law pre-empts or is supreme over state law,'' said David Cohen, an expert in gender and constitutional law at \uc2 Drexel University\uc2 's law school.\par \par \uc2 But Lawrence Gostin, an expert in health law at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 , said there would also be a strong counterargument: that regulation of the medical profession is the province of states, which can therefore regulate what pharmacies prescribe.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts also predict that more patients will be ordering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills from overseas, through websites like Aid Access -- an international organization run by a physician that mails pills -- a practice the F.D.A. has tried to stop.\par \par \uc2 Professor Ziegler and others said it is hard for states or the federal government to stop or interdict the mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills because of the practical difficulties of tracking and identifying every such package.\par \par \uc2 So far, most states that restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have long adhered to a principle of targeting providers and others who help patients, but not the patients themselves. Professor Ziegler said it is possible that could also change in a post-Roe landscape because, in circumstances where the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  takes place outside state boundaries, ''there may be absolutely no one else in that state to go after but the patient.''\par \par \uc2 In the Louisiana legislature this week, a committee advanced a bill that would allow a patient who aborted a pregnancy, as well as anyone who assisted the patient, to be charged with murder. Opponents of the bill said that it was unconstitutional and could have far-reaching consequences, possibly even for practices like in vitro fertilization.\par \par \uc2 Some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates said that the availability of safe and effective \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills has eliminated one of the greatest fears in the years before Roe -- but has added a new one.\par \par \uc2 ''One of the sharpest distinctions is really between the idea of hemorrhaging and the idea of handcuffs,'' said Kristin Ford, a spokeswoman for \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2 . ''In the pre-Roe world, there was a legitimate concern about people bleeding out in back alleys. That's not the reality we face. What we're looking at now is a world of criminalization.''\par \par \uc2 Zolan Kanno-Youngs contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Zolan Kanno-Youngs contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Medication for abortions, with instructions, at a clinic in Oklahoma City. An anti-\uc2  advocacy group listed laws against medication \uc2  as first among its ''pressing priorities'' for 2022. (PHOTOGRAPH BY EVELYN HOCKSTEIN/REUTERS) (A22) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220506ei560003i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
