\par\par \uc2 THE INTERPRETER\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Roe v. Wade Inspired Activists Abroad. Is That a Riskier Strategy Now?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amanda Taub \par \uc2 1,636 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Feminist movements in some countries similarly sought \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in their courts, but for others, the goal was legislative change.\par \par \uc2 The draft Supreme Court opinion overturning Roe v. Wade that leaked Monday night is not yet final. But when the dust settles, American women may conclude that they had lost the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  the same way that an Ernest Hemingway character said he had gone bankrupt: gradually, and then suddenly.\par \par \uc2 If anything like the leaked draft becomes law, it will be the result not just of decades of campaigning, litigating and nominating of conservative judges by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups and their Republican allies, but also of a single decision that reverses the establishment of a constitutional right that had inspired \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights campaigners around the world.\par \par \uc2 So the opinion also raises a question relevant to activists everywhere: Is seeking protection for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights through courts, rather than building the kind of mass movement that can power legislative victories, a riskier strategy than it once seemed?\par \par \uc2 Roe's surprising politics\par \par \uc2 It is hard to imagine now, but at the time Roe v. Wade was decided, in 1973, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not a major issue for the American right, or even for evangelical Christians.\par \par \uc2 In fact, two years before Roe, the \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2  voted for a resolution calling for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be legalized. And though both parties were split on the issue, opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was most associated with Catholics, who tended to vote Democratic.\par \par \uc2 But just a few years later, that had changed. The shift was not spurred by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself, but by desegregation. After the Supreme Court ordered schools in the South to desegregate, many white parents pulled their children from public schools and sent them to all-white private schools known as segregation academies. After further litigation by Black parents, the I.R.S. revoked those schools' tax-exempt status, provoking widespread anger among white evangelical Christians and catalyzing their new role as a powerful conservative force in American politics.\par \par \uc2 Publicly opposing desegregation was not really socially acceptable or palatable to a broader coalition. But opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was. And \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights had followed a similar procedural path as Brown v. Board of Education and other civil rights cases, using impact litigation to win constitutional protections at the Supreme Court to override state laws. So criticizing Roe became a way to talk about ''government overreach,'' ''states' rights'' and the need to ''protect the family'' without having to actively oppose civil rights or desegregation.\par \par \uc2 Over the years, the backlash built up more steam. But the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  still seemed relatively secure, particularly after the Supreme Court reaffirmed it in Planned Parenthood v. Casey in 1992. The fact that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights remained protected in the United States, even in the face of growing political opposition, seemed like an argument in favor of seeking protections via the courts.\par \par \uc2 Activists in other countries have sought a similar path. In Colombia in 2006, Monica Roa, a lawyer for the feminist group Women's Link Worldwide, won exceptions to the country's blanket \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban by arguing that Colombia's international treaty organizations, and thus its Constitution, required exceptions for rape, incest or danger to the life or health of the mother. This year, in a subsequent case, the court went further, decriminalizing all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before 24 weeks of gestation.\par \par \uc2 Pursuing the issue via the courts allowed activists to partly circumvent the contentious politics around the issue, said Julia Zulver, a political sociologist who studied activism around \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Colombia. ''During the peace process, everything got polarized,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, the government held a referendum on a peace agreement with \uc2 the FARC\uc2  guerrilla group. To undermine public support for the deal, conservative politicians, including former President \u193\'00\'C1lvaro Uribe, sought to associate the draft agreement with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , gender education in schools and other contentious social issues.\par \par \uc2 ''As soon as the peace referendum started going through, it was like, if you're voting yes to this peace referendum, you're voting to turn your children gay, you're voting against the nation. You're voting against the idea of the nation and the family. And lumped into that are issues like women's rights or access to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Dr. Zulver said.\par \par \uc2 In Mexico, President \uc2 Andr\u233\'00\'E9s Manuel L\u243\'00\'F3pez Obrador\uc2  has been fiery in his opposition to the Mexican feminist movement, which he views as hostile opposition to his populist administration. But after years of grass-roots organizing by the movement, the country's Supreme Court decriminalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Courts are the catch\par \par \uc2 But just as Roe's passage and ability to withstand opposition seemed to map out a path to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protection, its likely fall now highlights a potential weakness of judicial protection: It is inherently dependent on the makeup of the courts. And over time, that can change.\par \par \uc2 In the United States, Republican voters' opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped fuel a decades-long effort to appoint and elect conservative judges at all levels of the judicial system. Today, the result is a conservative supermajority on the Supreme Court that not only looks set to overturn Roe, but that has also swung sharply to the right on other issues, including voting rights.\par \par \uc2 In Poland, when the far-right nationalist government failed to get a restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law through Parliament, it turned instead to the constitutional tribunal, which was stacked with justices friendly to the governing Law and Justice party. In October 2020, the tribunal effectively enshrined the failed legislation into constitutional law.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes litigation simply fizzles. In 2010, many thought that a challenge to Irish \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in the \uc2 European Court of Human Rights\uc2  might become a Roe for Europe. But the court issued only a narrow procedural decision instead.\par \par \uc2 Activism after all\par \par \uc2 In the end, it may come down to activism after all. And around the world, a pattern has emerged: successful campaigns treat \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of broader questions of national identity, and rest on sustained organizing by experienced activists.\par \par \uc2 In Ireland in 2012, the death of a young woman named Savita Halappanavar who had been denied a medically necessary \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  became a rallying cry for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights movement. In 2018, the country held a referendum to change the Constitution to legalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which passed with over 66 percent support.\par \par \uc2 As in Colombia, Irish activists sought to frame the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue as a matter of national and social identity. But this time, the dynamic was reversed: In Ireland, the most successful identity argument was made by the side arguing in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, framing \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of Ireland's European identity.\par \par \uc2 ''The framing around Ireland's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights campaign was about compassion, and how Ireland has to be the compassionate face of Europe,'' said Marie Berry, a University of Denver political scientist who has studied the Irish campaign. ''That it is more compassionate than the U.K., as the U.K. became more and more conservative, especially under Tory government. That we're in the E.U., we represent a progressive Europe.''\par \par \uc2 But the key to the movement's success may have been combining that appealing message with the organizing experience of more radical feminist groups. ''What shocked me when I was doing research with activists there was that actually, the organizing node of the whole \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights 'Repeal the 8th' campaign came from anarcho-feminist movements, which were more rooted in environmental movements than the liberal women's rights movement,'' Dr. Berry said. ''The bulk of the people who voted for it, of course, were not affiliated with the more leftist organizing nodes. But that was really the heart of the movement that made it happen.''\par \par \uc2 In Argentina, the Ni Una Menos (''Not one woman less'') movement also combined sustained, long-term organizing with framing that situated \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the broader context of a just society, presenting the lack of access to safe, legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as just one part of the broader problem of violence against women. A 2018 bill to legalize the procedure failed, but in 2020, the country legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , making Argentina the largest country in Latin America to do so.\par \par \uc2 In the United States, by contrast, legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been the status quo since the Roe decision in 1973, which made it a difficult target for that kind of sustained mass organizing.\par \par \uc2 ''I think the indigenous mobilizing, some of the more progressive kind of racial justice work, Occupy, all of the kind of the leftist nodes within those movements, haven't centered \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their advocacy because it has been, constitutionally, more or less a solved issue since the 70s,'' Berry said. And for other organizations focused on the intersection of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  with race and class, ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has always been there, but it isn't the only demand,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Centrist organizations and Democratic politicians, by contrast, have often framed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a matter of unfortunate but necessary health care services that should be ''safe, legal and rare,'' and focused activism on issues of access. That was often vital for women in rural areas or states whose burdensome regulations had made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  essentially unavailable in practice, but it did not generate the kind of mass, identity-based appeal that has been effective in countries like Ireland.\par \par \uc2 And so today, with Roe apparently on the brink of falling, American activists are considering what it will take to build their own mass movement in the style of Ni Una Menos -- and what they can accomplish before it is too late.\par \par \uc2 Audio produced by Parin Behrooz.\par \par \uc2 Audio produced by Parin Behrooz.\par \par \uc2 Activists in Bogot\u225\'00\'E1, Colombia, in February after a court ruling decriminalized certain abortions. (PHOTOGRAPH BY NATHALIA ANGARITA FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220510ei550000e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
