\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 3 Governors Buck G.O.P. Over Access\b0\par\par\uc2 By Felice Belman \par \uc2 339 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As Republican-led states across the country move to codify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, the three Republican governors in New England vowed this week to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in their states.\par \par \uc2 If the Supreme Court ultimately overturns Roe v. Wade, said Gov. \uc2 Charlie Baker\uc2  of Massachusetts, ''it would be a massive setback for women in states without responsible laws protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.''\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Phil Scott\uc2  of Vermont reaffirmed his support for an amendment to the State Constitution that would enshrine the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And Gov. \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  of New Hampshire said, ''So long as I am governor, these health care services for women will remain safe and legal.''\par \par \uc2 The region's Republican leaders are popular moderates who all characterize themselves as supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. But their positions are not uniform, and the Massachusetts and New Hampshire leaders have come under criticism from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists.\par \par \uc2 In Vermont, state law already specifically protects \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; voters will decide this fall on an amendment that would have a more lasting approach. ''The fundamental rights and liberties of all women will be defended, protected and preserved in Vermont,'' Mr. Scott said.\par \par \uc2 In Massachusetts, Mr. Baker has generally supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights but vetoed a 2020 measure intended to expand access in a variety of ways. The governor objected to lowering the age of parental consent required for minors seeking abortions and allowing later-term abortions in certain instances. Democratic lawmakers overrode that veto.\par \par \uc2 In New Hampshire, a spokesman for Mr. Sununu said this week that he would eagerly sign legislation codifying Roe v. Wade into state law. But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates were furious at his signature on legislation that included a ban on later-term abortions. Lawmakers recently approved a measure adding an exception to that ban for pregnancies that involve fatal fetal abnormalities, and Mr. Sununu has said he intends to sign it.\par \par \uc2 Gov. Charlie Baker, Republican of Massachusetts, generally supports \uc2  rights. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220505ei550005f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
