\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Backers of  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights Ask Why Biden Won't Do More to Help\b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter Baker \par \uc2 1,720 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Over the course of a half-century in national politics, President Biden has evolved from an outright critic of Roe to a seemingly reluctant and largely quiet supporter.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- When \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters protested outside the Supreme Court this week after the leak of a draft decision overturning Roe v. Wade, they turned their ire at one point toward the same president who just hours earlier denounced the possible ruling just as they were doing.\par \par \uc2 ''If you want to be a pro-choice president,'' declared Renee Bracey Sherman, a prominent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights leader and author, ''you've got to start acting like a pro-choice president.''\par \par \uc2 The crowd agreed, chanting loudly: ''Where is Joe? Where is Joe?''\par \par \uc2 For President Biden, the threat to the landmark Roe decision represents a singular challenge as he attempts to put aside a long history of evident discomfort with the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to transform himself into a champion of the constitutional right that may soon be erased from the law books.\par \par \uc2 Over the course of a half-century in national politics, Mr. Biden has rarely been the full-throated backer of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights that activists have sought, evolving from an outright critic of Roe early in his career to a seemingly reluctant and largely quiet supporter. While he has used the word ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' in written statements as president, he had never spoken the word out loud since taking office until this week when the draft ruling leaked.\par \par \uc2 ''If he won't even talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  (no votes or budget needed) or give us a plan he's won't go bold to protect us,'' Ms. Bracey Sherman, the founder and executive director of We Testify, an organization representing women who have had abortions, wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 ''He could be out making speeches,'' she added. ''He could be using his executive order powers. He could deputize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to make them immune from prosecution. He could be doing anything. He could be trying. But he's silent. And he won't meet with us to discuss plans.''\par \par \uc2 To some extent, Mr. Biden's reticence has reflected a broader unease among many Democratic leaders in recent years to highlight \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights nationally, focusing instead on specific states and districts where they are a stronger political selling point. While polls show a strong majority favor preserving Roe, they also show ambivalence about unlimited \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 White House officials insisted that Mr. Biden would use his office to do whatever he could to counter any ruling by the Supreme Court overturning Roe, but there are limits to his authority and aides outlined no specific ideas under consideration. Even in his comments in recent days, Mr. Biden has not dwelled on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but rather quickly shifted the discussion to a broader range of privacy rights that he argued would be imperiled by the opinion drafted by Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''This is about a lot more than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Mr. Biden told reporters on Wednesday, citing gay rights and birth control. ''What are the next things that are going to be attacked? Because this MAGA crowd is really the most extreme political organization that's existed in American history -- in recent American history.''\par \par \uc2 To the extent that he has discussed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week, he used phrasing that was far from the preferred language of his own side. On Tuesday, he referred to ''the judgment to choose to abort a child,'' a wording that seemed to accept the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  argument that it is a child, not merely a fetus, that is aborted.\par \par \uc2 Instead, it fell to Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to offer the administration's most robust response to the court's possible ruling.\par \par \uc2 ''Those Republican leaders who are trying to weaponize the use of the law against women -- well, we say, 'How dare they!''' she thundered on Tuesday night at the annual conference of Emily's List, a group that helps elect Democratic women supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. ''How dare they tell a woman what she can do and cannot do with her own body. How dare they! How dare they try to stop her from determining her own future! How dare they try to deny women their rights and their freedoms!''\par \par \uc2 Longtime advisers said that Mr. Biden's position on the issue was clear and that he preferred to use words like ''privacy'' rather than ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' because it appealed to a wider swath of the public. At her briefing on Wednesday, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , the White House press secretary, brushed off questions about why he referred to aborting ''a child'' and said quibbles about terminology do not undermine his fundamental stance.\par \par \uc2 ''When he talks about his commitment to protecting a woman's right to choose, women's health care, he's referring to protecting a woman's right to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' she said. ''I think most people know that.''\par \par \uc2 That was not where he started, however. Mr. Biden, a practicing Roman Catholic, has struggled with the issue over the years. A former aide said Mr. Biden would personally never have considered \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it came up in his own family but always made women's rights a priority, citing his success in enacting the Violence Against Women Act.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden was first sworn into the Senate in January 1973, just 17 days before the Supreme Court issued Roe. and at the time he accused the justices of going ''too far.'' In an interview a year later, he said a woman should not have ''the sole right to say what should happen to her body.''\par \par \uc2 In 1982, Mr. Biden voted for a constitutional amendment pushed by President \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  allowing individual states to overturn Roe. He called it ''the single most difficult vote I've cast as a U.S. senator'' but explained it in the context of his faith. ''I'm probably a victim, or a product, however you want to phrase it, of my background,'' he said. He reversed himself and voted against the amendment a year later.\par \par \uc2 For years, he voted for the so-called Hyde Amendment barring the use of federal Medicaid funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and proposed an amendment of his own to prohibit foreign aid for biomedical research related to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He voted for legislation outlawing a rare late-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure. But as he noted repeatedly in recent days, he also led the fight against confirming Robert H. Bork, an outspoken \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  foe nominated by Reagan to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 By the time Mr. Biden was gearing up for his second run for the presidency, he presented himself as a changed man. ''I was 29 years old when I came to the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , and I have learned a lot,'' he said on ''Meet the Press'' on NBC in 2007. ''Look, I'm a practicing Catholic, and it is the biggest dilemma for me in terms of comporting my religious and cultural views with my political responsibility.''\par \par \uc2 His shift in position to meet his political needs was most recently on display in 2019 as he battled more progressive opponents to win the Democratic presidential nomination. One day his campaign said he still supported the Hyde Amendment, but after an uproar on the left, he reversed his longstanding stance the next day.\par \par \uc2 As a result, both sides share uncertainty about his true convictions. ''If you follow his history on this, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has been on this issue wherever he thought it was politically expedient,'' said David N. O'Steen, the executive director of the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2 . ''Internally how does he really feel? Nobody really knows how he feels.''\par \par \uc2 Since taking office, Mr. Biden's White House released a National Strategy on Gender Equity and Equality pledging to ''protect the constitutional right to safe and legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  established in Roe v. Wade.'' The administration rolled back Trump administration restrictions on the program known as Title X, which provides access to family planning services and supports clinics that counsel women on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A Biden adviser, speaking on the condition of anonymity to discuss internal deliberations, said the president had now directed his team to be as creative as possible using executive action to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Appearing on Capitol Hill on Wednesday, his health secretary, \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2 , vowed to ''double down on the effort to make sure that the legal right of Americans, women, to access the care they're entitled to continues.''\par \par \uc2 But short of muscling legislation through Congress, or using his bully pulpit, options are limited. Some experts suggested the government could direct resources to groups that provide housing, transportation and other support to women who cross state lines seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But that could violate the Hyde Amendment.\par \par \uc2 If the court does overturn Roe, the next battleground will be over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. Since it became legal in 2000, medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become increasingly common and now accounts for more than half of recent abortions. If Roe falls by the wayside, states controlled by Republicans will most likely ban medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well.\par \par \uc2 Legal experts said the Biden administration could challenge those bans by arguing that because the pills are approved by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 , it is legal for doctors in any state to prescribe them. Asked about this, the Biden adviser said Mr. Biden would not dictate anything to the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 Some Democrats expressed frustration that their party's leaders have not been more forceful in making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights a signature issue. ''Where is the Democratic Party?'' Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  of California said on Wednesday. ''Where's the counteroffensive?''\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Amy Klobuchar\uc2 , a Democrat from Minnesota and an ally of the White House, pointed to statements by Mr. Biden and Ms. Harris and expressed confidence they would rise to the political challenge. ''I think that they will be leading on it,'' she said in an interview. ''Of course their voices will be really important.''\par \par \uc2 Sheryl Gay Stolberg and Zolan Kanno-Youngs contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Sheryl Gay Stolberg and Zolan Kanno-Youngs contributed reporting.\par 