\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 How Opinions On  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Vary, And Why That Matters\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nate Cohn \par \uc2 944 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Polling suggests an overturning of Roe v. Wade might not carry political consequences in states that would be likeliest to put in restrictions.\par \par \uc2 A majority of Americans believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases. But the story is more complicated in the states where the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is likely to be decided if -- as is now expected -- the Supreme Court overturns Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 In the states poised to put in new restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , people tend to say that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be mostly or fully illegal, based on a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  analysis of large national surveys taken over the last decade.\par \par \uc2 In the 13 states that have enacted so-called trigger laws, which would immediately or very quickly outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if Roe were overturned, 43 percent of adults on average say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in most or all cases, while 52 percent say it should be illegal in most or all cases.\par \par \uc2 Voters are more divided in the dozen or so states that have pre-Roe bans on the books or that are expected to enact new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions if Roe is overturned. In those states -- where the fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is most likely to play out in campaigns or state legislative chambers -- an average of 49 percent of adults say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in most or all cases, compared with 45 percent who say otherwise.\par \par \uc2 That is still somewhat less than the national average of 54 percent who mostly or fully support legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , compared with 41 percent who mostly or fully oppose it.\par \par \uc2 The geographic pattern evident in the results suggests that a national outcry over a court decision to overturn Roe might not carry many political consequences in the states where abortions could be immediately restricted. In some of those states, new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions may tend to reinforce the political status quo, even as they spark outrage elsewhere in the country.\par \par \uc2 But in some states, a fight over new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions might pose serious political risks for conservatives, perhaps especially in the seven mostly Republican-controlled states that are seen as most likely to enact new restrictions even though a majority of voters tend to support legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The public's views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are notoriously hard to measure, with large segments of the public often seeming to offer muddled or inconsistent answers. Polls consistently show that around two-thirds of Americans support the court's decision in Roe v. Wade and oppose overturning it. Yet just as many Americans say they support banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the second trimester, a step barred by Roe. And a more modest majority -- usually around 55 percent in broader sets of data -- supports legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in most or all cases, while people split almost evenly over whether they consider themselves ''pro-choice'' or ''pro-life.''\par \par \uc2 The poll question used here -- whether the respondent believes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in most or all cases, or illegal in all or most cases -- offers only a general sense of a voter's attitudes on the issue. It may not align exactly with whether a voter or a state electorate would support any particular restriction.\par \par \uc2 Voters who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in ''most'' cases might accept a ban on abortions after the first trimester, like the one recently enacted in Florida, which would be at odds with Roe v. Wade but affect only about 8 percent of abortions. Conversely, voters who believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be illegal in most cases might still support allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cases of rape or incest -- or perhaps even without conditions in the first trimester.\par \par \uc2 The opponents of Roe have long said they wanted to leave the issue to the voters of each state, and the data suggests that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions may cut very differently across the dozen or so states where the issue is likeliest to be in play in the months ahead.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, which has put into action the most stringent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions so far, there are few signs of a fundamental transformation of the state's politics.\par \par \uc2 Texans roughly split on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  overall, making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights more popular there than in the typical state with a trigger law. But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was almost a nonissue in the state's primary in March, with candidates staying focused on the pandemic and immigration. Only 39 percent of Texans said the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws should be ''less strict'' in a poll in February, several months after the passage of the law, which effectively bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after around six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates might be on more favorable political terrain in the more traditionally competitive Midwestern states. A modest majority of voters say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be mostly legal in states like Ohio, Michigan and Iowa, where evangelical Christians represent a far smaller share of voters than in the South. The figures are similar in other battleground states, like Arizona and Florida.\par \par \uc2 It's unclear if the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue will be enough to redraw the political map. Perhaps it will fade, as it seems to have in Texas. But the stakes are not small for Republicans in this region: The predominantly white working-class voters who swung from Barack Obama to Donald J. Trump in the 2016 presidential election tended to back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 In a postelection study, 58 percent of voters who flipped from Mr. Obama to Mr. Trump in 2016 said that they would support a law that would ''always allow a woman to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a matter of choice.''\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY  FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220505ei550004v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
