\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Demand Increases at Mississippi Clinic at Center of the Supreme Court Case\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rick Rojas \par \uc2 668 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Jackson Women's Health has seen an increase in patients, many from states like Texas and Louisiana that have restricted access to abortions.\par \par \uc2 The morning after the leak of a draft Supreme Court opinion in a case concerning Mississippi's only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, Derenda Hancock arrived at the clinic around sunrise, just as she does most days, to lead a group of volunteers escorting patients. Wednesday morning, the group was back to do it again.\par \par \uc2 Women regularly come to the clinic, Jackson Women's Health, from across Mississippi, as well as Louisiana and Texas. This week, at least one came from Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 The clinic challenged the Mississippi law that would shut down abortions there, in a case that has climbed to the Supreme Court and paved the way to what will surely be one of the most consequential decisions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in decades.\par \par \uc2 Often, it can seem like a wide gulf separates the legal fight in Washington from the practical realities on the ground. But the draft opinion has brought renewed concern about the fate of the clinic once the final ruling arrives.\par \par \uc2 ''We know there is no winning,'' Ms. Hancock said by phone as she helped direct traffic and kept watch over the perimeter of the clinic, known as the Pink House for its flamingo exterior. The volunteers that she leads go by the name Pink House Defenders.\par \par \uc2 For now, the mind-set at the clinic, among doctors, staff members and volunteers, was to keep pushing ahead for as long as they could.\par \par \uc2 ''We're going to continue to see patients,'' said Shannon Brewer, the director of the clinic. ''I don't know anything different we would do other than see patients. It's a surprise to some people for some reason. This is what we've anticipated.''\par \par \uc2 The clinic has seen a surge in demand in recent months, driven in large part by the rules implemented in Texas that are some of the most restrictive in the nation, pushing people seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to surrounding states. The clinic went from operating three days a week to five. Some months, the clinic has as many as 300 patients.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't recall ever having to turn someone away because we didn't have a slot for them until the past couple of months,'' said Dr. Cheryl Hamlin, an obstetrician-gynecologist from Massachusetts who travels to Jackson once a month, part of an out-of-state rotation of doctors who keep the clinic operating. It has been unable to find physicians in Mississippi willing to provide abortions.\par \par \uc2 There had been some discussion for plans if Roe v. Wade was overturned, such as finding ways to help women in Mississippi travel to other states to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Those patients, Ms. Brewer said, would be the ones most severely impacted by the ruling. Ms. Brewer has also talked about moving the clinic to New Mexico, if necessary.\par \par \uc2 But mostly, she wants to keep the Jackson clinic open for as long as she can. The clinic has not had a staff doctor for more than a decade. Over the years, other providers in Mississippi shuttered, buckling under the restrictions imposed by the state. The Jackson clinic abides by them, though, providing ultrasounds and mandated counseling.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Hamlin also tells patients that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  creates a heightened risk of breast cancer, just like the law dictates, even though it is a claim that experts say is unsupported by science.\par \par \uc2 At the end of her counseling sessions, in a quick dash of defiance, Dr. Hamlin urges her patients to vote, if they believe that access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is important.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm going to be more forceful,'' she said of her plea. ''What have you got to lose?''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Cheryl Hamlin and volunteer escorts outside the Jackson Women's Health Organization in Jackson, Miss., on Tuesday. (PHOTOGRAPH BY RORY DOYLE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220505ei550003k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
