\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 A Version of History That Challenges Decades of Scholarship on Criminality\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jennifer Schuessler \par \uc2 1,718 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The leaked draft opinion that would overturn Roe v. Wade also takes aim at its version of history, challenging decades of scholarship that argues \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not always a crime.\par \par \uc2 History, and arguments about history, have long been central to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  jurisprudence.\par \par \uc2 In its 1973 decision in Roe v. Wade, the Supreme Court found a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , grounded in what it described as a ''right to privacy'' provided in the Fourteenth Amendment. And that legal argument was bolstered by a historical narrative.\par \par \uc2 State laws prohibiting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all stages of pregnancy, Justice Harry Blackmun wrote in the opinion, were not of ancient or even common-law origin, but dated mostly to the late 19th century. Before that, he wrote, citing various scholars, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  early in pregnancy was legal in most states.\par \par \uc2 The leaked draft opinion in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, which would overturn Roe, offers a very different history. The 98-page draft, written by Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 ., asserts that ''an unbroken tradition of prohibiting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on pain of criminal punishment persisted from the earliest days of the common law until 1973.''\par \par \uc2 Roe, Justice Alito writes, ''either ignored or misstated this history.'' And ''it is therefore important,'' he continues, ''to set the record straight.''\par \par \uc2 The claim of an ''unbroken tradition'' of criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  set off strong criticism from many historians, including some whose work was cited in an amicus brief submitted by the \uc2 American Historical Association\uc2  and the \uc2 Organization of American Historians\uc2 , the two main organizations of professional historians in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Here are some of the historical claims in question.\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito on the History of \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Restrictions\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito begins his historical argument by saying that the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a recent invention. ''Until the latter part of the 20th century,'' he writes, ''there was no support in American law for a constitutional right to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Zero. None.''\par \par \uc2 By contrast, he claims, ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had long been a crime in every single state.'' Until the 19th century, he maintains, American law followed common law, which criminalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''in at least some stages of pregnancy.'' And the records of prosecutions, however scant, ''corroborate that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a crime.''\par \par \uc2 In the 1800s, he writes, states began passing laws that ''expanded criminal liability.'' By the time the 14th Amendment was adopted, three-quarters of the states outlawed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all stages of pregnancy, with the rest to follow within a few decades.\par \par \uc2 How Historians See the Story\par \par \uc2 The Constitution includes no references to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And it wasn't until the second half of the 20th century, Justice Alito writes, that people began claiming the idea of a basic right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mary Ziegler, the author of several books on the history of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  (and a critic of the draft decision), said that part was correct. But the opinion, she and others argue, underplays the fact that for most of the first 100 years of American history, early abortions -- before fetal ''quickening'' (generally defined as the moment when the fetus's movements can be detected) -- were not illegal.\par \par \uc2 This is the argument made in the historians' brief, which outlines the history of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulation up to 1866. For decades after the founding of the United States, common law did not regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or even recognize that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was happening at that early stage. ''That is because common law did not even acknowledge a fetus as existing separately from a pregnant woman'' before quickening, the historians argue.\par \par \uc2 The central historical claims in Roe ''were accurate,'' the brief says, ''and remain so today.''\par \par \uc2 Leslie J. Reagan, the author of ''When \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Was a Crime: Women, Medicine and Law in the United States, 1867 to 1973,'' said in an interview that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was common in the early 19th century, perhaps even more so than Roe depicted.\par \par \uc2 And regulation relied on women's own experience, since they were the ones who would know when ''quickening'' occurred. And before ''quickening,'' Professor Reagan said, taking medications or other treatments wasn't even considered \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but ''trying to get your menses'' -- menstrual period -- ''back.''\par \par \uc2 ''It was after quickening that it was against the law, and considered immoral,'' she said. ''After quickening, women themselves would stop trying to get their menses back. It was considered a life.''\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito's Sources\par \par \uc2 While the draft makes references to the historians' brief, it relies more heavily on other sources, including ''Dispelling the Myths of \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  History,'' a 2006 book by Joseph W. Dellapenna that challenged Justice Blackmun's historical arguments in Roe.\par \par \uc2 Professor Dellapenna, a law professor at \uc2 Villanova University\uc2 , cited (using a phrase Justice Alito echoes in the draft opinion) what he called an ''unbroken tradition'' of laws protecting unborn life, which stretched from English common law into the 1970s.\par \par \uc2 His book has been hotly debated by historians. But Justice Alito also draws on other sources, including a brief submitted by the legal scholars Robert P. George and John M. Finnis, who challenged the historical scholarship supporting Roe.\par \par \uc2 By the late 1860s, they argue, the legal distinction of ''quickening'' had been abandoned, ''because science had shown that a distinct human being begins at conception.'' When the Fourteenth Amendment was passed in 1868, they argue, fetuses were understood as ''persons'' deserving protection.\par \par \uc2 In a post on Tuesday in Mirror of Justice, a Catholic legal blog, Professor George wrote that the term ''person'' in the Fourteenth Amendment ''was publicly understood at the time of the framing and ratification of the amendment as including the child in the womb.''\par \par \uc2 He added on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that ''bizarrely, some critics of the leaked Alito opinion in Dobbs are trying to cast doubt on his historiography by reviving discredited claims'' about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  history included in Roe, claims he said he and Finnis had ''refuted.''\par \par \uc2 In a telephone interview, he declined to comment further on the substance of the draft opinion, calling the leak a highly damaging breach of trust. But he said that history had always been crucial to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate, since analysis of history was ''at the core of Roe.''\par \par \uc2 By focusing on history, Professor George said, Blackmun was able to claim -- falsely, he said -- ''that the Court wasn't inventing a new right, but restoring an old common-law right.''\par \par \uc2 How \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Restrictions Changed Over Time\par \par \uc2 In 1827, Illinois became the first state to criminalize abortions pre-quickening. In 1829, New York elevated the offense from a misdemeanor to a felony.\par \par \uc2 These laws were driven by various motivations. According to the historians' brief, the stricter statutes enacted through the 1840s and 1850s ''were often in response to alarming newspaper stories about women's deaths from abortions. Yet despite these new laws on the books,'' the brief says, ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  convictions remained rare.''\par \par \uc2 This echoes Professor Reagan's book, which argues (citing the historian James C. Mohr) that the earliest laws regulating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were poison-control measures meant to protect women from dangerous abortifacient drugs, rather than to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself.\par \par \uc2 But in 1857, Professor Reagan writes, the newly founded \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2  ''initiated a crusade to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at every stage of pregnancy illegal.'' The organization was driven not only by concern for fetal life but also by the desire to take control from midwives. And some members expressed concern that middle-class ''Anglo-Saxon'' women were not having as many children as Catholic immigrants and people of color.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Horatio R. Storer, a leader of the medical campaign against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , asked who would settle the nation as it spread westward. Would the frontier ''be filled by our own children or by those of aliens?'' he asked. ''This is a question that our own women must answer; upon their loins depends the future destiny of the nation.''\par \par \uc2 Debating the Connections Between \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Race\par \par \uc2 In the draft opinion, Justice Alito notes the argument that the restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws adopted starting in the mid-19th century were meant to bolster the white, Protestant birthrate. But he dismisses the claim, saying it is based on only a handful of supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. ''It's quite a leap to attribute these motives to all the legislators whose votes were responsible'' for the new laws, he writes.\par \par \uc2 Instead, he writes, ''there is ample evidence'' that anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws were ''spurred by a sincere belief that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  kills a human being.''\par \par \uc2 Instead, Justice Alito notes arguments that proponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights were the ones with racist motives. In a footnote, he refers to an amicus brief submitted in an unrelated 2019 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case, which argued that early 20th-century proponents of ''liberal access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' were motivated by a desire to reduce the Black population.\par \par \uc2 ''It is beyond dispute that Roe has had that demographic effect,'' Justice Alito writes, citing government data showing that ''a highly disproportionate percentage of aborted fetuses are Black.''\par \par \uc2 He also cites Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 's much-noted fiery concurrence in that 2019 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case, in which he assailed early birth control advocates like Margaret Sanger as racist eugenicists who wanted to suppress the births of ''undesirable'' individuals and populations.\par \par \uc2 While Sanger herself did not support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Justice Thomas wrote, other family planning advocates did so ''for eugenic reasons.'' And today, he warned, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  retains the potential ''to become a tool of eugenic manipulation.''\par \par \uc2 The historical relationship between the early family planning movement and eugenicist beliefs (which were widely held across American society in the early 20th century) is complex and intensely disputed.\par \par \uc2 But Professor Ziegler questioned how Justice Alito could dismiss the notion that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictionists in the 1850s were motivated even in part by bigotry, while citing claims that it was a motivation of some 20th-century supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 People on both sides of the issue, she said, were driven by a mix of motives. ''The idea the Court thinks it can weed out the nativist impulses'' on one side, while emphasizing those impulses on the other, she said, ''is historically implausible.''\par \par \uc2 Above, opponents and supporters of \uc2  rights outside the statehouse in Trenton, N.J., in April 1973, a few months after the Roe v. Wade ruling, written by Justice Harry Blackmun, left. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY ASSOCIATED PRESS; PAUL HOSEFROS/THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220505ei550003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
