\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Firms Might Lose Option Of Silence About  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emma Goldberg, Alisha Haridasani Gupta and Lauren Hirsch \par \uc2 1,732 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In a deeply divided nation, public statements on Roe v. Wade can affect everything from recruiting to a company's bottom line. So far, that has kept almost everyone quiet.\par \par \uc2 Some political and cultural events set off a cascade of emails from brands -- sneaker, makeup and food companies telling their customers that they stand with them in a stressful time, or reminding them to vote. But after a draft opinion obtained by Politico revealed the Supreme Court's intention to overturn Roe v. Wade, the overwhelming reaction from corporate leaders was silence.\par \par \uc2 ''This is an issue that many companies have shied away from,'' said Miriam Warren, \uc2 Yelp\uc2 's chief diversity officer.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Warren, whose company has been among the most vocal in support of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, is hoping the silence will break. She sees voicing an opinion, one way or another, as a necessity for recruiting and retaining talent.\par \par \uc2 ''The days of companies not wading into political issues, or not speaking out on things that are perceived as private or personal, are over,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigners, however, said the silence from companies made business sense.\par \par \uc2 ''It is generally a mistake for corporate leaders to wade into political issues, particularly divisive political issues where they might alienate half their customer base,'' said Anne Cori, chairman of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group Eagle Forum.\par \par \uc2 And for now, that logic seems to hold. There were scattered responses expressing dismay with the draft opinion -- largely from women-focused brands and the women who run them, many of whom previously spoke out on the subject.\par \par \uc2 OKCupid, the dating service, wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  on Tuesday that overturning Roe v. Wade would be ''unacceptable,'' adding: ''Tag a brand you want to see take action.'' Kate Ryder, chief executive of Maven, a women's and family health group, wrote that her team had been making plans on how to help companies protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access for their workers if Roe was overturned. \uc2 Sheryl Sandberg\uc2 , chief operating officer of \uc2 Meta\uc2 , wrote on her personal \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page that it was ''a scary day for women.''\par \par \uc2 Beyond that, among most Fortune 500 companies, substantive statements were few and far between, whether in support of or opposition to the court's draft opinion.\par \par \uc2 In recent years, business leaders have dipped their toe into political discourse, making public statements in support of \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  or voting rights or marriage equality. Some companies that wouldn't have dreamed of engaging in politics a decade ago felt that the Trump era demanded at least a news release.\par \par \uc2 Even more recently, corporate leaders were reminded of how fraught engagement can be. \uc2 Disney\uc2 , for example, faced internal backlash when its leadership declined to take a strong stance against Florida's Parental Rights in Education act, which critics often refer to as the ''Don't Say Gay'' law. But when the chief executive did take a public stance, the company was crucified on social media, and the state revoked its special tax benefits.\par \par \uc2 Now, with the expected demise of the country's landmark \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, corporate leaders are confronting the hottest of hot-button issues. In a \uc2 Pew Research\uc2  poll in 2021, 59 percent of Americans said they believed that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases, while 39 percent said it should be illegal in all or most cases. People on all sides of the issue feel strong about it, with nearly a quarter of Americans saying they will vote only for candidates who share their views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to Gallup.\par \par \uc2 That all adds up to many reasons a company would want to avoid making any statement on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- and all the more reason that customers and workers could come to see it as necessary. A company's position on the end of Roe could have repercussions for how it hires in an increasingly competitive labor market, and how customers view its brand.\par \par \uc2 ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a health care issue, health care is an employer issue, so \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an issue for employers,'' said Carolyn Witte, chief executive of \uc2 Tia\uc2 , a women's health care company. On Tuesday, \uc2 Tia\uc2  announced that it would provide medication abortions through its telemedicine platform in states where it operated and where doing so was legal.\par \par \uc2 For some major companies that have been known to weigh in on political and social issues, this week has been unusually quiet. \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , \uc2 Disney\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 , PwC, \uc2 Salesforce\uc2 , \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2 , ThirdLove, Patagonia, \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and Business Roundtable were among the companies and organizations that declined to comment or take a position, or did not respond to requests for comment about whether they plan to make public statements about their stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Hobby Lobby\uc2 , which in 2014 brought a suit to the Supreme Court challenging whether employer-provided health care had to include contraception, made no public statement and did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Other companies did wade in. \uc2 United Talent Agency\uc2  said it would reimburse travel expenses for employees affected by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  said it would ensure its employees ''have the resources they need to make choices about their \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' \uc2 Levi Strauss & Company\uc2 , which has said its benefits plan will reimburse employees who have to travel out of state for health care services such as abortions, said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a business issue.\par \par \uc2 ''Efforts to further restrict or criminalize that access would have far-reaching consequences for the American work force,'' the company said in an email to \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . ''It would jeopardize workplace gains women have made over the past 50 years.''\par \par \uc2 The stakes of making any statement -- corporate, or personal as a company leader -- are clearly high.\par \par \uc2 In September, \uc2 John Gibson\uc2 , the chief executive of Tripwire Interactive, a gaming company based in Georgia, wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that he was ''proud'' of the Supreme Court for ''affirming the Texas law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for babies with a heartbeat.'' His comments angered colleagues, and within a few days he was replaced.\par \par \uc2 ''The comments given by \uc2 John Gibson\uc2  are of his own opinion, and do not reflect those of Tripwire Interactive as a company,'' read a statement from Tripwire Interactive leadership. ''Our leadership team at Tripwire are deeply sorry and are unified in our commitment to take swift action and to foster a more positive environment.''\par \par \uc2 Tripwire did not reply to a request for comment. In a tweet after his departure from the company, Mr. Gibson said, ''To the many fans, friends and peers across the belief spectrum that have reached out to offer care and support, thank you.''\par \par \uc2 Consumer-facing companies also have to think about what customers will say: Two-thirds of consumers say they base purchasing decisions on a brand's social position, according to research from Edelman in 2018.\par \par \uc2 ''If I'm \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , and I'm located in the South, I think I'd have bigger concerns about political repercussions and repercussions from consumers -- particularly if I act alone,'' said Amanda Shanor, an assistant professor at the \uc2 Wharton School of the University of Pennsylvania\uc2 , where she focuses on constitutional law. Arkansas, home to \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's headquarters, is among the 13 states set to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately or very quickly if Roe v. Wade is reversed.\par \par \uc2 Alienating customers is one hazard. But companies also have to think about the labor market.\par \par \uc2 Women make up roughly half the work force, and those who are unable to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are less likely to be employed full time six months after denial of care, according to a 2018 paper. The percentage of women participating in the labor force has grown significantly since the Roe ruling in 1973; between 1962 and 2000, it jumped from 37 to 61 percent.\par \par \uc2 And in areas of the country where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is most restricted, executives sometimes face recruiting challenges. \uc2 Vivek Bhaskaran\uc2 , chief executive of QuestionPro, a technology services company that moved its headquarters from San Francisco to Austin just before the pandemic, said Texas' restrictive laws were hampering its ability to recruit talent.\par \par \uc2 ''I've done tons of interviews, and in almost all of these conversations we end up talking about the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law in one way, shape or form,'' he said. ''One lady said, 'My personal values are not really tied to Texas -- are you going to force me to move to Texas?'''\par \par \uc2 Solugen, a Houston-based chemicals company, decided to open a second office in Boston in the coming months to accommodate recruits who are uncomfortable moving to Texas, said Gaurab Chakrabarti, the company's chief executive and co-founder.\par \par \uc2 The handful of companies that took action after Texas banned abortions at the six-week mark last year could be a harbinger of what the wider corporate world might do in the coming weeks and months. \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  disclosed in a securities filing that it was providing travel benefits to employees seeking abortions outside their home state. \uc2 Yelp\uc2 , which has just over 200 employees in Texas, announced that it would cover expenses for workers who needed to travel out of state for abortions. The head of \uc2 Match Group\uc2 , \uc2 Shar Dubey\uc2 , announced a fund for employees seeking abortions.\par \par \uc2 Amalgamated Bank\uc2  was one corporate entity that had tried to avoid addressing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  publicly -- until this week. A vice president of the bank, Maura Keaney, followed along as the Supreme Court heard arguments on the Mississippi law directly challenging Roe in December and felt ''hopeless,'' she said. Still, at the time she didn't say anything publicly.\par \par \uc2 ''As the days and weeks went on, it became clear to me through talking to allies in the space, our clients and our employees, that just wasn't a functional perspective for either me to take personally or for the bank to take,'' Ms. Keaney said.\par \par \uc2 She was working to ensure that the bank -- founded in 1923 by a labor union of mostly immigrant women workers -- could pay travel costs for out-of-state abortions for its employees and create a fund to help grass-roots organizations.\par \par \uc2 An announcement was planned for later this month. Instead, the bank made it public on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Protesting the potential overturning of Roe v. Wade in New York on Tuesday. Some major companies were quiet after the leak of a Supreme Court draft. (PHOTOGRAPH BY HILARY SWIFT FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B3) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220505ei550003h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
