\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Gathered at the Court to Protest, or to Celebrate\b0\par\par\uc2 By Linda Qiu and Sarahbeth Maney \par \uc2 699 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Scores of protesters took to the steps in front of the Supreme Court on Tuesday hours after a draft opinion overturning the landmark Roe v. Wade decision leaked. Armed with megaphones, signs, buttons and at least one letterpress for speedy poster production, groups supporting and opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights demonstrated and occasionally confronted one another in front of the barricaded court.\par \par \uc2 ''Pro-choice is a lie, babies don't choose to die,'' a throng of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists chanted.\par \par \uc2 ''Bans off our bodies'' and ''my body, my choice,'' their counterparts replied.\par \par \uc2 The size of the crowd grew gradually during the day. Senator Elizabeth Warren, Democrat of Massachusetts, delivered a short speech denouncing the ''extremist Supreme Court.''\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  spoke to a few of the protesters. Here's how they described the moment.\par \par \uc2 ''I was surprised at the nature of the decision, but I wasn't surprised at the content. I think that what we're seeing is 50 years of religious overreach by the Catholic bishops into American government, in the Supreme Court, in Congress, and last night, we just happened to see it in the Supreme Court,'' said Ashley Wilson, 32, the communications director for \uc2 Catholics for Choice\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''When we landed at DCA, turned on our phones, we saw the news about the Supreme Court leak and, you know, a range of emotions. As soon as we were able to, we joined our colleagues and friends and booked it to come to rally in celebration last night and, of course, got like three hours of sleep and returned here in the morning,'' said Robert Byrd, 29, an activist with Pro-Life San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 He added: ''To support democracy it means to recognize the voice of people in each and every state, and to support any valid concept of human rights means that you must recognize every human, every member of the human species. You can't discount some people just because they're smaller, less mature.''\par \par \uc2 ''The Supreme Court is out of line trying to take back multiple rulings, Casey v. Planned Parenthood and also Roe v. Wade. But also legislators need to step up here because we had the chance to enact federal law that will protect reproductive choice and it's been stalled. So I really hope this motivates legislatures to, before the midterms, get something through,'' said Izzy Rowland, 19, a student in Washington, D.C. She continued: ''And of course, you have to have the whips come in and get the party in shape. And it shouldn't be a partisan issue, but that's how it's been broken down.''\par \par \uc2 ''I was upset about the leak. And I think it's the same institution that I felt could get to stay away from the politics of it. So I think I was really disappointed to see whoever leaked it. It was a personal thing, I think, to release it because they thought they could affect the decision. So I think that was just wrong. And then my second was, you know, we have to get this work done,'' said \uc2 Kristen Day\uc2 , 53, the executive director of Democrats for Life of America.\par \par \uc2 ''I think women are awesome,'' she added. ''We can have a baby, have a job and go to school, and we can do all those things. So let's not tell them when they can't. And I feel like with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they sometimes will say you can't do any of those things -- unless you have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that somehow a child is going to hold you back. I just don't believe that.''\par \par \uc2 ''Having the right over your own body so implicitly ties into all of the other things that we're fighting for that if we ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , then we're effectively controlling people of color especially and trans people -- I don't want to leave anyone out of this debate -- but it's freedom to just exist,'' said Nat McGartland, 27, a graduate student at the University of Maryland.\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPHS BY SARAHBETH MANEY/THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220505ei540000g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
