\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Draft Jolts Lawmaker Who Revealed Her  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emily Cochrane \par \uc2 434 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When Representative \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2 , Democrat of Missouri, saw the leaked draft of a Supreme Court ruling overturning Roe v. Wade, she immediately called her staff Monday evening with an urgent question: Is this real?\par \par \uc2 ''My brain was just saying, how could this happen?'' she recalled in an interview Tuesday on learning about the draft opinion on the right to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''As much as we knew it was coming, how could it actually be a real thing? Who could be that cruel?''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Bush is one of the few lawmakers who has publicly disclosed that she had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as a teenager. She testified before the House Oversight Committee last fall about choosing to terminate a pregnancy after she was assaulted on a church trip, joining two other Democratic lawmakers -- Representatives \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2  of Washington and Barbara Lee of California -- in recounting a personal experience as part of an emotional plea to further codify the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''I'm pretty broken up,'' Ms. Bush said Tuesday. ''Whether you have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or whether you have the child, no one is on that table with you. No one is on that bed with you.''\par \par \uc2 Born in 1976, after Roe v. Wade was decided, Ms. Bush said she never questioned whether she would have access to a safe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . She added, ''Now we're going to change this -- after 49 years, go backward, and then tell, raise up, a generation of folks that, 'OK, you thought that this was an option for you, and now, it's not.'''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Bush, who is Black and gained national attention as an activist in the Black Lives Matter protests, said she was concerned that other women of color would be disproportionately affected if Roe v. Wade is overturned. Her office was already fielding calls about the draft decision, she added, saying that her immediate focus was on reassuring people that they still would be able to seek an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until a formal decision was announced.\par \par \uc2 ''One of the first things that we have to do is make sure that people know that you're OK right now,'' she said. ''And then also, looking at what we need to do -- what are the urgent things that have to happen? Because there is yet still power in numbers, there is, and we have to use that.''\par \par \uc2 ''My brain was just saying, how could this happen?'' Representative Cori Bush, Democrat of Missouri, said on Tuesday. (PHOTOGRAPH BY T.J. KIRKPATRICK FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES\u239\'00\'EF\u191\'00\'BF\u189\'00\'BD\u239\'00\'EF\u191\'00\'BF\u189\'00\'BD) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220504ei5400053\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
