\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Decades-Old Arguments From Right Finally Find a Majority to Adopt Them\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 1,366 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 . argued two basic points: The Constitution is silent on the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Roe's flaws make it unworthy of respect as precedent.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The draft Supreme Court opinion overruling Roe v. Wade is a sort of white paper prepared by the conservative legal movement long ago, awaiting only the arrival of a conservative majority ready to adopt it.\par \par \uc2 The opinion, by Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 ., set out familiar critiques of the Roe decision, which established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1973. Indeed, it is an expert distillation of conservative talking points dating to the Reagan administration.\par \par \uc2 They boil down to two propositions. First, the Constitution is silent on the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meaning that states can allow it, regulate it or ban it. Second, Roe was so poorly reasoned, divisive and unworkable that it does not deserve the respect usually owed to precedents.\par \par \uc2 The draft opinion, published by Politico, also gives evidence of an emboldened majority that is prepared to take assertive steps to revise settled understandings in other areas of the law, notably the Second Amendment, affirmative action and religious objections to gay rights.\par \par \uc2 Until the death of Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  in 2020, Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr\uc2 . held the key vote in closely divided cases. Generally conservative, he nonetheless preferred to move incrementally and to guard the legitimacy of the court.\par \par \uc2 But the replacement of Justice Ginsburg by Justice Amy Coney Barrett, President Donald J. Trump's third appointee to the court, changed the dynamic. The chief justice was now outflanked by the five justices to his right.\par \par \uc2 If nothing else, the draft opinion demonstrates at least one thing: that soon after the justices heard arguments in a challenge to Roe v. Wade in December, at least five of them voted to overrule it at their private conference. Politico reported that Justices Alito, Barrett, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , \uc2 Neil M. Gorsuch\uc2  and \uc2 Brett M. Kavanaugh\uc2  made up the majority.\par \par \uc2 The court's three liberal members -- Justices \uc2 Stephen G. Breyer\uc2 , \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and Elena Kagan -- are sure to dissent. Chief Justice Roberts's position is unclear.\par \par \uc2 The court confirmed the authenticity of the draft on Tuesday even as it cautioned that ''it does not represent a decision by the court or the final position of any member on the issues in the case.''\par \par \uc2 The draft opinion was circulated in February. Further drafts have doubtless been produced and exchanged. The court's actual decision is not likely to land until late June.\par \par \uc2 It is possible that the court's bottom line will change. It is very probable that the details of the leaked opinion will evolve in later drafts. But the draft nevertheless gives a good overview of the structure of the arguments that could put an end to Roe.\par \par \uc2 They start with the words of the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 ''The Constitution makes no reference to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Justice Alito wrote, ''and no such right is implicitly protected by any constitutional provision.'' He acknowledged that the due process clause of the 14th Amendment, which protects ''liberty,'' ''has been held to guarantee some rights that are not mentioned in the Constitution.''\par \par \uc2 But those implicit rights, he wrote, quoting a 1997 decision, ''must be 'deeply rooted in this nation's history and tradition' and 'implicit in the concept of ordered liberty.'''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he wrote, is not such a right. ''Indeed,'' Justice Alito wrote, when the 14th Amendment was adopted, ''three-quarters of the states made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a crime at all stages of pregnancy.''\par \par \uc2 Other rights protected by the 14th Amendment, he added, did not have to balance a competing interest: that of fetal life.\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito's second main point was that the doctrine of stare decisis, Latin for ''to stand by things decided,'' does not preclude overruling Roe and Planned Parenthood v. Casey, the 1992 decision that reaffirmed Roe's central holding that states may not ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before fetal viability, around 23 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ''Roe was egregiously wrong from the start,'' Justice Alito wrote. ''Its reasoning was exceptionally weak, and the decision has had damaging consequences. And far from bringing about a national settlement of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue, Roe and Casey have enflamed debate and deepened division.''\par \par \uc2 In four places, the draft opinion quoted from a dissenting opinion in a companion case to Roe chastising the majority for exercising ''raw judicial power.'' If the draft or something like it is adopted to overrule Roe, the court may hear a version of the same criticism.\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito included a long list of decisions in which the court had overruled precedents. They included some of the court's greatest hits, including landmark cases on racial equality and gay rights.\par \par \uc2 ''Without these decisions,'' he wrote, ''American constitutional law as we know it would be unrecognizable, and this would be a different country.''\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito, generally an opponent of citing foreign law, said that the viability framework was at odds with international practice.\par \par \uc2 ''The viability line, which Casey termed Roe's central rule, makes no sense, and it is telling that other countries almost uniformly eschew such a line,'' he wrote. ''The court thus asserted raw judicial power to impose, as a matter of constitutional law, a uniform viability rule that allowed the states less freedom to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than the majority of western democracies enjoy.''\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito, summarizing the views of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents, wrote that there was less need for access to the procedure given societal developments.\par \par \uc2 ''They note that attitudes about the pregnancy of unmarried women have changed drastically; that federal and state laws ban discrimination on the basis of pregnancy, that leave for pregnancy and childbirth are now guaranteed by law in many cases; that the costs of medical care associated with pregnancy are covered by insurance or government assistance; that states have increasingly adopted 'safe haven' laws, which generally allow women to drop off babies anonymously; and that a woman who puts her newborn up for adoption today has little reason to fear that the baby will not find a suitable home,'' he wrote.\par \par \uc2 Those assertions are contested and may draw comparisons to a passage in Justice \uc2 Anthony M. Kennedy\uc2 's majority opinion in a 2007 decision upholding the federal Partial-Birth \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Ban Act. Justice Kennedy supplemented his legal analysis with observations about motherhood that some women found patronizing.\par \par \uc2 ''While we find no reliable data to measure the phenomenon,'' he wrote, ''it seems unexceptionable to conclude some women come to regret their choice to abort the infant life they once created and sustained. Severe depression and loss of esteem can follow.''\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration, in a brief supporting the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in the new case, argued that overruling Roe would threaten many rights, including to same-sex marriage and contraception.\par \par \uc2 ''None of those practices is explicitly mentioned in the Constitution,'' the brief said, ''and most of them were widely prohibited when the 14th Amendment was adopted.''\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito rejected that view.\par \par \uc2 ''To ensure that our decision is not misunderstood or mischaracterized, we emphasize that our decision concerns the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and no other right,'' he wrote. ''Nothing in this opinion should be understood to cast doubt on precedents that do not concern \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 All the court was doing, he said, was returning the question of whether and how to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to elected lawmakers. ''Roe and Casey must be overruled,'' Justice Alito wrote, ''and the authority to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  must be returned to the people and their elected representatives.''\par \par \uc2 He said the court should not care about the public's reaction to the decision, failing to anticipate that the release of the draft opinion would give the court a vivid preview.\par \par \uc2 ''Whatever influence the court may have on public attitudes must stem from the strength of our opinions,'' he wrote, ''not an attempt to exercise 'raw judicial power.'''\par \par \uc2  rights advocates surrounded anti-\uc2  activists in front of the Supreme Court on Tuesday after a draft of an opinion overruling Roe v. Wade leaked. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Leigh Vogel for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020220504ei540003t\par }\par \b \uc2 Search 